chx:Document a doc:ReportSection ; rdfs:label "How Bitcoin works: one real transaction from Alice's wallet to the blockchain, every figure recomputed"@en ; skos:definition "This document explains chapter 2 of the 3rd edition concept by concept, adding the terms the chapter uses without defining them, and re-reads every quotation from its source and recomputes every number, hash and encoding from the bytes of the real transaction the chapter follows." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02/research> ;
    doc:hasSection chx:S_Transaction, chx:S_Network, chx:S_Blockchain, chx:S_Semantics, chx:S_Practice .
chx:S_Transaction a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transaction" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 1 ;
    skos:definition "A transaction is the unit of action in Bitcoin: a message telling the network that the owner of certain bitcoins has authorized the transfer of that value to another owner. The new owner can in turn spend the value by creating another transaction, and so on, which gives a chain of ownership. This first branch of the chapter follows one such transaction, Alice's payment for a podcast episode in Bob's online store, from the moment it is conceived to the moment it is spent onward (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nEverything else in Bitcoin exists to carry transactions safely. The network propagates them, miners gather them into blocks and the blockchain records them, so a student who knows what a transaction contains, and how transactions refer to one another, has the vocabulary for the rest of the course. The book adds a point that is easy to miss: bitcoins do not exist physically or as digital data that could be emailed. What exists is a database on every full node that says who controls how many bitcoins, and a transaction is the data that convinces the nodes to update that database (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nIn the chapter Alice scans a payment request in Bob's store, her wallet builds a transaction, the network relays it and a miner records it in a block. The same object is met in every wallet, every block explorer and every node, because all of them read and write the same kind of data. Later chapters open it up: the keys and signatures that authorize it, the format in which it is sent, and the fees that pay for it.\n\nThe branch is organised around the questions a learner asks in order. What parts does a transaction have? Who is allowed to spend an output, and how is that proved? How do transactions link to one another, and what forms do they commonly take? In which units is value counted? How does a wallet construct a transaction, and how does the payment begin with a request? Each question is a group of concepts below, and later groups rely on the words defined in earlier ones.\n\nTwo cautions apply from the start. A transaction does not move coins from one place to another: it changes who may spend certain outputs, because the book says there are no physical coins or even individual digital coins and that the coins are implied in transactions (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). And this chapter simplifies on purpose: it names the signature, the script and the identifier without opening them, so each is given a concept here and a full treatment in a later chapter." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Transaction> .
chx:S_TransactionPart a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction part"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transaction part" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 2 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Transaction ;
    skos:definition "A transaction part is one of the pieces a transaction is assembled from. The chapter names the two sides, the inputs that spend funds and the outputs that receive them, and the small difference between them that pays the miner. To describe them exactly this group also explains the unspent output, the identifier by which a transaction is referred to, the size that a transaction occupies, the fee rate by which miners compare fees, and the ledger image that gives the whole structure its meaning (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nReading a transaction correctly means knowing which part does which job. An input says what is being spent and who allows it, an output says what is created and who may spend it next, and the fee is whatever remains. Confusing these parts leads to the classic misunderstandings, such as believing that a payment is a single amount sent from one address to another.\n\nThe parts are met in every block explorer, which retrieves a transaction and shows what it spends and what it creates, and in every wallet that has to assemble them. The chapter's own illustration shows Alice's payment to Bob's store as one transaction whose input is an earlier output and whose two outputs are the payment and the change. The same transaction is printed byte by byte in the book's chapter on transactions, which lets this course check every figure in its description.\n\nThe concepts of this group are taken in the order in which a transaction is read: the double-entry ledger, which supplies the bookkeeping picture that holds the parts together, then the input, the output, the fee that is implied by both, the size that the transaction takes up in a block, the fee rate that turns a fee and a size into a comparable price, the unspent output that links an input to its past, and the identifier that names a transaction." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#TransactionPart> .
chx:S_DoubleEntryLedger a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Double-entry ledger"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Double-entry ledger" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 3 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionPart ;
    skos:definition "A ledger is a book of record in which each movement of value is written down as an entry, and double-entry bookkeeping is the practice of recording every movement from two sides: what leaves one account and what arrives in another. The chapter uses this picture directly. It says that transactions are like lines in a double-entry bookkeeping ledger, with one or more inputs, which spend funds, on one side and one or more outputs, which receive funds, on the other (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe image gives a student a first mental model for something that has no physical form. Instead of thinking of coins travelling between wallets, one thinks of entries in a shared book, each entry saying which earlier entries it consumes and which new ones it creates. The same image explains why the blockchain is described as the authoritative journal of all transactions: a journal is the chronological book in which such entries are first written down (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe picture appears in the chapter's figure of a transaction as a bookkeeping line, in the discussion of the transaction fee, and in the very first overview of Bitcoin, where the system is said to consist of users with wallets, transactions that are propagated across the network, and miners who produce the consensus blockchain, the authoritative journal of all transactions (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Nakamoto's paper speaks of a record that cannot be changed without redoing the proof of work (Nakamoto, 2008).\n\nIn an ordinary ledger the two sides of an entry balance exactly. In Bitcoin they balance only once the fee is counted: the outputs add up to slightly less than the inputs, and the difference is an implied fee collected by the miner (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). For Alice's payment the inputs total 100000 satoshis, the outputs 75000 and 20000, and the fee 5000, so that outputs plus fee equal inputs. The check sum([75000, 20000, 5000]) == 100000 evaluates to True, and the same total follows when the two output amounts are read out of the transaction's own bytes.\n\nThe analogy is a teaching device and has limits. A bookkeeper's ledger names accounts and their owners, while a Bitcoin transaction names no person: it refers to earlier outputs and to conditions for spending the new ones. The transaction data also has no field for a running balance per owner. A balance is computed by a wallet, which adds up the unspent outputs it can spend." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#DoubleEntryLedger> .
chx:S_Input a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Input"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Input" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 4 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionPart ;
    skos:definition "An input is the part of a transaction that spends funds. It does not contain coins; it points to an earlier transaction's output, and it carries the proof that the person spending is entitled to do so. In the chapter's figure, the input of Alice's payment is labelled Tx1:0, meaning the output of her earlier transaction in which Joe sold her bitcoins (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nInputs are what turn separate transactions into a connected history. Because every input names the output it consumes, anyone can follow a payment backward through the chain of ownership, and anyone can see whether the same output is being spent twice. The proof of ownership in the input, a digital signature that can be independently validated by anyone, is what lets strangers accept a payment without knowing who the payer is (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nInputs are found in every transaction. A simple payment has one input, a consolidation has many, and a miner's reward transaction has a special input that spends nothing (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). When a wallet constructs a transaction, choosing the inputs is its first task, as the concepts on coin selection and on getting the right inputs explain.\n\nThe book's chapter on transactions calls the reference that an input contains an outpoint. It consists of the 32-byte identifier of the transaction in which the funds were received and an output index, a 4-byte unsigned integer counted from zero that picks one output of that transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A full node uses the outpoint to find the earlier output, and from it learns the amount and the conditions for spending it. Real transactions do not state the value of their inputs; software must look up the output that is referenced, which for Alice's real transaction is an output of 100,000 satoshis (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Reading the bytes of that transaction shows one small difference from the figure: the output index stored in the input is 1, whereas the simplified figure labels the reference Tx1:0. The identifier inside the input is also stored in the opposite byte order to the one used when it is displayed: reversing the bytes eb3ae38f... of the stored form gives 4ac54180..., the form that Bitcoin Core's commands accept (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nAn input cannot be partly spent, in the same way that a banknote cannot be torn in two: the whole of the referenced output is consumed, and any surplus must be sent back to the payer as change (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The word input also invites a misreading. It does not mean money entering an account, but an earlier output being used up. And a figure is a simplification: when a detail matters, such as which output of the earlier transaction is meant, read it from the transaction itself." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Input> .
chx:S_Output a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Output"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Output" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 5 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionPart ;
    skos:definition "An output is the part of a transaction that receives funds. It consists of an amount, counted in satoshis, and a script, the condition that must be satisfied to spend that amount later. In Alice's payment one output carries 75000 satoshis for Bob's store and another carries 20000 satoshis back to Alice, so the outputs together add up to 95000 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nOutputs are where ownership is created. The chapter explains that an output is created with a script saying, in effect, that it is paid to whoever can present a signature from the key corresponding to Bob's public address. Because only Bob's wallet holds that key, only Bob can later spend the output. The book calls this encumbering the output with a demand for a signature from Bob (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nOutputs are met in every transaction. They are also what a wallet counts when it reports a balance: the balance is the sum of the outputs that the wallet's keys can spend and that nobody has spent yet. Each output that is still unspent is a coin-like unit that a later transaction can take as an input. The book notes that an output can hold as little as zero and as much as 21 million bitcoins, a limit explained under the concept of consensus rules (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA transaction can have several outputs, and they are numbered from zero in the order in which they appear, which is what the second half of an outpoint refers to. In the bytes of Alice's real transaction the output at position 0 carries 20000 satoshis, her change, and the output at position 1 carries 75000 satoshis for Bob's store; the later figure of the chain accordingly shows Bob spending the output Tx2:1. The sum of all outputs is at most the sum of all inputs, and what is left over is the fee: sum([75000, 20000]) evaluates to 95000, and the 5000 satoshis missing from the 100000 that Alice spent form the fee.\n\nOutputs are all-or-nothing in the same way as inputs: a later transaction takes a whole output or leaves it alone. An output also never expires. It stays unspent, and is counted in every full node's database, until a valid transaction spends it, which is why the book warns that outputs of very small value can become uneconomical to spend and are a burden on the nodes that must keep track of them (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Output> .
chx:S_TransactionFee a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction fee"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transaction fee" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 6 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionPart ;
    skos:definition "The transaction fee is a small payment to the miner who includes a transaction in a block. It is never written in the transaction as a separate number. It is implied by the difference between the value of the inputs and the value of the outputs, so any value that the outputs do not claim goes to the miner (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe fee is how a user asks the network to process a payment in a timely fashion. The chapter on fees explains that it is not a fee in the usual sense: it is not an amount set by the protocol or by any miner, but much more like a bid in an auction for the limited space in a block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is one of two payments that reward a miner, the other being the newly created bitcoins of the block reward. Nakamoto's paper already described it: if the output value of a transaction is less than its input value, the difference is a fee added to the incentive of the block containing the transaction (Nakamoto, 2008).\n\nThe wallet adds the fee when it constructs the transaction; Alice only has to choose a destination, an amount and a fee. The fee can always be read as the difference between the sum of the inputs and the sum of the outputs, and a miner's reward transaction collects the sum of all fees in its block.\n\nFor Alice's payment the arithmetic is simple. Her input is Tx1's output of 100000 satoshis, the two outputs total 95000 satoshis, and so 100000 - sum([75000, 20000]) evaluates to 5000: the fee is 5000 satoshis. The figure is derived from the amounts that the book prints, and it is the miner, not Bob, who receives it. The book's own diagram of the chain lists the same fee of 5000 for the transaction to Bob's store and a fee of 8000 for Bob's onward transaction.\n\nBecause the fee is implied, a wallet that mistakes the amounts can send an enormous fee without any warning from the format, since there is no field to check. Wallets should therefore make it hard to pay an excessive fee by accident, while still allowing it on purpose, as the chapter on fees advises (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Fees also do not depend on the value being sent, but on how much space the transaction takes, which the concepts of size and fee rate explain." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#TransactionFee> .
chx:S_TransactionSize a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction size"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transaction size" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 7 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionPart ;
    skos:definition "The size of a transaction is the room that it takes up in a block. Because every block can hold only a limited amount of transaction data, software needs a measure for it. The book names the modern unit weight, and an alternative expression of the same measure, the virtual byte or vbyte, of which one equals four units of weight; a vbyte is thus easy to compare with the plain byte, the unit of eight bits, that older blocks used (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nSize is what makes block space a scarce good. A block is limited to 4 million units of weight, so the number of transactions that fit depends on how large each one is, not on how much value it moves (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A payment of a million bitcoins and one of a single satoshi may occupy the same room, and a miner who has to choose between them looks at the fee that each pays for that room, which is the idea behind the fee rate.\n\nSize appears whenever a wallet shows how much a payment costs, and in the figures that block explorers and Bitcoin Core report for a transaction. Alice's real transaction is reported by Bitcoin Core as 194 bytes in size, 143 vbytes and 569 units of weight (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Bitcoin Core's consensus code defines the block limit as MAX_BLOCK_WEIGHT of 4,000,000 and the factor between the weight and the old byte as WITNESS_SCALE_FACTOR of 4 (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nThe weight of a transaction is the sum of the weights of its fields, each field's serialized size in bytes being multiplied by a factor (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The factors are chosen so that the part of a transaction that holds its proofs counts for less than the rest, which keeps the cost of spending an output low. Reading the 194 bytes of Alice's transaction and counting the proof part once and everything else four times gives 569 units of weight, and 569 divided by four, rounded up, gives 143 vbytes, as (569 + 3) // 4 evaluates to 143 in whole numbers. A block of 4,000,000 units of weight is therefore a block of at most one million vbytes.\n\nThe three numbers answer different questions. The size in bytes is the length of the serialized data, the weight is what the block limit counts, and the virtual byte is the weight expressed in a familiar unit. Mixing them up is a common reason for fee figures that do not match between two tools. The factors themselves belong to a later chapter on transactions, and a student should treat the number 4 as a fixed part of today's rules, not a law of nature." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#TransactionSize> .
chx:S_FeeRate a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Fee rate"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Fee rate" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 8 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionPart ;
    skos:definition "The fee rate is the fee a transaction pays for each unit of the space it takes in a block. Miners do not compare transactions by their total fee alone; they divide the fee by the size of the transaction, as a shopper divides the price of a bag of rice by its weight to find the best deal. The most common unit today is the satoshi per virtual byte (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nBlock space is scarce, and the fee rate is the price at which it is sold. A transaction pays a single fee however large it is, but a larger transaction leaves room for fewer others in the block, so a miner who wants the most revenue from a block fills it with the transactions that pay the highest fee per unit of space. The chapter says accordingly that transactions are added to a new block prioritized by the highest fee rate first (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nFee rates matter whenever a wallet lets a user choose how quickly a payment should be processed. A user who wants a payment confirmed sooner chooses a higher fee rate; one who can wait chooses a lower one. The book lists several units in which a fee rate is written, among them satoshis per virtual byte, which it calls the most commonly used today, and bitcoin per kilo-vbyte, which Bitcoin Core mainly uses (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe calculation is a plain division. Alice's payment paid 5000 satoshis and takes up 143 virtual bytes, so its fee rate is 5000 / 143, which rounds to 34.97 satoshis per virtual byte. A miner sorts the candidate transactions by this ratio and takes them from the top until the block is full. The size of 143 comes from the transaction's own bytes, as the concept of transaction size shows, and the fee of 5000 from the amounts in the chapter.\n\nThe units are a source of expensive mistakes. The book warns that a fee rate copied from a field with one denominator into a field with another can lead to paying a thousand times too much, and that switching the numerator could mean a hundred million times too much (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A fee rate is also a market price, not a rule of the protocol, so what counts as a good rate changes with demand for block space; the figure of 34.97 describes one payment, not a recommendation." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#FeeRate> .
chx:S_UnspentOutput a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Unspent transaction output"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Unspent transaction output" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 9 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionPart ;
    skos:definition "An unspent transaction output, abbreviated UTXO, is a transaction output that has not yet been used as an input by any later transaction. The glossary defines it as an unspent transaction output that can be spent as an input in a new transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A wallet's bitcoins are, in this sense, nothing other than the unspent outputs it can unlock.\n\nThe idea explains how Bitcoin keeps track of ownership without accounts. A full node holds a copy of every confirmed transaction's unspent outputs, and a valid new transaction may spend only outputs that are in that set. Each time a new block arrives, the outputs that its transactions spend are removed from the database and the outputs they create are added, so the set of unspent outputs is the current state of the whole system, and a double-spend is simply an attempt to use an output that is already gone (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe chapter meets UTXOs when it describes how Alice's wallet gets the right inputs. A wallet that runs on a full node holds every unspent output, while many user wallets run as lightweight clients that track only their own (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The network chapter adds that some implementations keep a database of all unspent outputs, which holds millions of entries and, unlike the mempool, does not usually differ between nodes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nMark each output with a flag that says whether it has been spent. Starting from the outputs of Alice's chain, the expression that lists the outputs whose flag is false returns Tx2:0 and Tx2:1, because Tx1:0 was consumed by Alice's payment. A node that receives a new transaction performs the same filter at scale: for each outpoint it looks the output up in its set, rejects the transaction if the output is missing or already spent, and otherwise treats it as valid in this respect.\n\nTwo misreadings are common. An unspent output is not an account balance, and a wallet's total is the sum of many separate outputs, each of which is spent whole. And an unspent output is still tied to a condition: it counts as spendable only for the party that can satisfy its script. Wallets therefore track UTXOs for their own keys and ignore the rest." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#UnspentOutput> .
chx:S_TransactionIdentifier a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction identifier"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transaction identifier" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 10 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionPart ;
    skos:definition "The transaction identifier, or txid, is the 32-byte name of a transaction. It is derived from the transaction's own data by a hash function, so it is not assigned by anyone, and two different transactions cannot sensibly share one. In the chapter the txid is what an input uses to refer to the transaction in which funds were received (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nNames make references possible. Because a txid summarizes the transaction it identifies, a reference to it cannot be redirected to different content, and anyone holding the transaction can recompute the name and confirm it. This is also what makes a chain of transactions auditable: every link is a txid plus a position, and both can be checked independently by any node.\n\nThe txid is the search key in every block explorer: the chapter says that explorers accept a Bitcoin address, a transaction hash, a block number or a block hash. The book prints the identifier of Alice's payment to Bob as 466200308696215bbc949d5141a49a4138ecdfdfaa2a8029c1f9bcecd1f96177, which is 64 hexadecimal characters, since the book notes that it takes 64 hexadecimal characters to display 32 bytes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nBitcoin Core computes the identifier by hashing the serialized transaction without its proof data (Bitcoin Core, 2026); hashing means applying the double SHA-256 construction that the concept of the hash function shows, and the result is shown with its bytes reversed. Doing exactly that to the 194 bytes of Alice's real transaction reproduces the printed identifier. The reversal is the byte-order trap the book describes: a hash is displayed to users in one byte order but used internally in another, so the identifier that the transaction's input stores, eb3ae38f..., is the earlier transaction's identifier read the other way round, and reversing its bytes gives 4ac54180... (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA txid belongs to the transaction as it was built, and it is not proof that the transaction has been confirmed: a payment can have a txid and still wait in the mempool. The byte-order difference is a frequent source of confusion for programmers: pasting the stored form into a block explorer, or the displayed form into a program that expects the stored one, finds nothing, because the hash is the same and only its reading differs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#TransactionIdentifier> .
chx:S_Script a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Script"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Script" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 11 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionPart ;
    skos:definition "A script is a small program written in Bitcoin's own scripting language, called Script. The book describes it as a Forth-like, stack-based language and says that both the script placed in an output and the legacy input script used in a spending transaction are written in it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). An output script specifies the conditions that must be met to spend the output in the future, such as who is authorized to spend it and how they will be authenticated; an input script satisfies those conditions and allows the output to be spent (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nScripts are what make spending conditions flexible without making the rules of the network complicated. The first version of Bitcoin did not simply attach a public key to an output; it let payments go to an output script and let the spender answer with an input script, so that conditions such as needing two signatures out of two keys could be expressed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's chapter on keys states the idea in one sentence: bitcoins are received to an output script that acts like a public key, and spending is authorized by an input script that acts like a signature (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nIn the chapter, the script is the thing Alice's wallet puts in the output when it encumbers the payment with a demand for a signature from Bob (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Every full node runs the scripts of every transaction it validates. The most common form in the early years was a payment to the hash of a public key; Alice's real transaction uses newer forms of output, and its two output scripts have different lengths, 34 bytes for the change and 22 bytes for the payment, as the bytes of the printed transaction show (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nScript is a stack-based language: it reads items from left to right, pushes numbers onto a stack, and operators remove items from the stack, act on them and may push a result back. The book's first example is the script 2 3 OP_ADD 5 OP_EQUAL. The numbers 2 and 3 are pushed, OP_ADD replaces them by their sum 5, the number 5 is pushed, and OP_EQUAL replaces the two top items by TRUE if they are equal. A small interpreter that follows these rules ends with the stack [True] (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A transaction is valid when the script ends with a true value on top of the stack, and the input script and the output script are executed separately with the stack handed from one to the other (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nScript is deliberately limited. It has no loops and only conditional flow control, so it is not Turing complete, which guarantees predictable execution times; and it is stateless, so a script behaves the same way on every system (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's arithmetic example is also a warning: an output with the script 3 OP_ADD 5 OP_EQUAL can be spent by anyone who knows that the number 2 satisfies it, because a script grants authority to whoever can satisfy it, not to a named person (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Script> .
chx:S_Serialization a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Serialization"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Serialization" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 12 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionPart ;
    skos:definition "Serialization is the writing of a structured object as a flat sequence of bytes, in an order and a format on which sender and receiver agree, so that the receiver can rebuild the same structure. A transaction in a wallet is a structure of inputs, outputs and amounts; a transaction on the network is a string of bytes. The book names this form directly, calling serialized Bitcoin transactions the data format that software uses for sending transactions (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nEverything exact in this chapter depends on the serialized form. The identifier of a transaction is a hash of its bytes, so the bytes must be defined to the last detail; the size that a fee pays for is the length of those bytes; and the signature commits to them. If two programs serialized the same transaction differently they would compute different identifiers and the network would not work. This is why the description of the format belongs to the consensus-critical part of Bitcoin and not to a matter of taste.\n\nThe reader meets the serialized form as a long hexadecimal string. The book prints Alice's real transaction in that form in its chapters on Bitcoin Core and on transactions, and the same string appears in both; it is 194 bytes long, and every number this course quotes about that transaction was read out of it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Wallets exchange this form when a transaction is signed on one device and sent from another, and block explorers offer it for download.\n\nThe format is a sequence of fixed and variable fields. Amounts are eight-byte integers written with the least significant byte first, so the change output's 20,000 satoshis appear in the bytes as 204e000000000000, and reading those eight bytes back as a little-endian integer gives 20000, as the calculation above shows. Counts, such as the number of inputs, are written in a compact form that uses one byte for small values and more for larger ones. Reading the whole string field by field recovers the one input, the two outputs and the witness, which is how the checks of this chapter obtain the amounts without trusting any figure in the text.\n\nA serialized transaction is not readable by eye, and it is not encrypted either: it is simply a precise encoding, and anyone with the format can decode it. Two cautions follow. First, the identifier is computed from the serialization without the witness data, so the same payment has two byte-lengths and two hashes, which the concepts of the identifier and of the size explain. Second, the hexadecimal string is a printing of the bytes, not the bytes themselves, so a program must decode the text before hashing it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Serialization> .
chx:S_ByteOrder a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Byte order"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Byte order" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 13 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionPart ;
    skos:definition "Byte order, also called endianness, is the direction in which the individual bytes of a multi-byte value are written down. Writing the most significant byte first is called big-endian and is what people do when they write ordinary numbers; writing the least significant byte first is called little-endian and is what most processors do internally. Both orders describe the same value, and a reader must know which one is meant, because the two printings look like completely different numbers.\n\nBitcoin uses both orders, and the mismatch is a standing source of confusion that the book itself warns about: hash digests are displayed to users in one byte order but are used internally in a different byte order (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A student who does not know this will conclude that the identifier stored inside Alice's input is a different transaction from the one her wallet shows, and a programmer who does not know it will search a block explorer for a hash that exists and find nothing.\n\nThe clearest example is in this chapter's own data. The identifier of the transaction in which Joe paid Alice is printed by the book and accepted by Bitcoin Core as 4ac54180...8fe33aeb, while the bytes stored inside Alice's input, read in the order in which they appear, spell eb3ae38f...8041c54a (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The amounts in a serialized transaction are stored little-endian, and so are the four-byte fields of a block header, while a hash is displayed big-endian; both forms occur within the same 194 bytes.\n\nReversing a sequence of bytes converts between the two readings. Taking the displayed identifier, decoding it from hexadecimal into 32 bytes, reversing them and printing them again gives the stored form, whose first eight bytes are eb3ae38f27191aa5, as the calculation above shows; doing it a second time returns the original. The reversal is on whole bytes, not on hexadecimal characters: reversing the characters of the string would exchange the two halves of every byte and give a different value altogether.\n\nThree practical rules follow. Reverse bytes, never characters. Reverse only values that the convention requires, which in this chapter means transaction and block hashes, not the witness or the output scripts. And when a tool reports something that does not match, try the other order before concluding that the data is wrong, because the book's note about the two byte orders applies to almost every hash a reader will paste from one program into another (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#ByteOrder> .
chx:S_Outpoint a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Outpoint"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Outpoint" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 14 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionPart ;
    skos:definition "An outpoint is the exact name of one transaction output: the identifier of the transaction that created it together with the position of that output within the transaction. The book's chapter on transactions uses the word for the field inside an input and states its shape, an outpoint containing a 32-byte transaction identifier followed by an output index that is a four-byte unsigned integer starting from zero (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter's figures write an outpoint in the short form Tx1:0, a transaction label and a position separated by a colon.\n\nThe outpoint is the link that makes the whole structure of Bitcoin work. Because an input names one and only one earlier output, a node can look it up, learn the amount and the spending condition, and check whether it is still unspent; and because the identifier is a hash, the reference cannot be redirected to other content. The set of unspent outputs that a full node keeps is in effect a table indexed by outpoints, so the double-spend check is a lookup of one outpoint.\n\nThe reader meets outpoints in block explorers, where every input is shown with the transaction it spends and the position within it, and in the chapter's two figures of transaction chains, where arrows join an output to the input that consumes it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In Alice's real transaction the outpoint names the transaction 4ac54180...8fe33aeb and the output at index 1, and Bob's later transaction, the chapter says, spends Tx2:1, the output of 75,000 satoshis.\n\nAn outpoint is formed by putting the two parts together, as the small example does by joining an abbreviated identifier and the index with a colon. In the serialized bytes the two parts sit next to each other: 32 bytes of hash, stored in the reversed order that the concept of byte order describes, then four bytes holding the index as a little-endian integer. Nothing else is needed, because the amount and the conditions are not repeated in the input; the book stresses that transactions do not explicitly include the value of their inputs and that software must find the previous output (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nTwo warnings. The index counts from zero, so the first output of a transaction is output 0 and the familiar off-by-one error here means spending the wrong output or none at all. And an outpoint is not an address and not an owner: it identifies a position in the ledger, and who may spend it is decided by the script in the output it names, which is why a chain of outpoints can be followed without learning anybody's identity." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Outpoint> .
chx:S_Witness a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Witness"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Witness" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 15 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TransactionPart ;
    skos:definition "The witness of an input is the data that proves the input may be spent. The book gives the word its general sense, saying that an abstract name for the data held by an input script is a witness, and then names the arrangement that this chapter's transaction uses: the idea of separating the rest of the data in a transaction from its witness for the purpose of generating a transaction identifier is called segregated witness, or segwit (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The field that holds the separated data is called the witness structure (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe separation matters for two reasons that this chapter has already met. The first is the identifier: because the witness is left out of the data that is hashed, the identifier of a transaction no longer changes when the proof changes, which removes a family of problems the book lists as circular dependence and third-party and second-party malleability (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The second is size: the witness bytes are counted once in the weight while the rest are counted four times, which is why spending an output is cheaper than it would otherwise be and why the three size figures of a transaction differ.\n\nThe reader meets the witness in the bytes of Alice's transaction, which is written in the newer format. Two extra fields at the front mark that format, which the book calls the extended serialization: a marker that must be zero and a flag that in the current protocol is one, and the book notes that a transaction with no witness must instead use the older, legacy serialization, which has neither those two fields nor the witness structure (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The witness in Alice's transaction holds one item of 65 bytes, the signature and its hash-type byte, as the digital-signature concept explains.\n\nThe figures can be read out of the transaction. Its full serialization is 194 bytes and the part without the witness is 125, so 69 bytes belong to the witness and the two marker bytes; the weight is the stripped part counted four times plus the witness part once, which the expression computes as 125 times three plus 194, that is 569, the figure Bitcoin Core reports, and dividing by four and rounding up gives the 143 virtual bytes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The transaction identifier is the double hash of the 125-byte stripped form, which is what Bitcoin Core's source does when it hashes the serialization without witness data (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nDo not read the separation as hiding: the witness is public, it is sent to every node and it is committed to by the block, so nothing about it is secret. Note also that the word has two uses that the book keeps apart, the witness as the abstract name for authorizing data and the witness structure as the field that carries it, and that the course's later chapters on authorization open both. Finally, a transaction with no witness at all is serialized in the older format, so a program that assumes the two marker bytes are always present will misread such a transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Witness> .
chx:S_Cryptography a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Cryptographic foundations"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Cryptographic foundations" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 16 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Transaction ;
    skos:definition "A transaction asks every computer on the network to believe something without trusting anyone: that the person spending certain bitcoins is entitled to spend them. Mathematics alone makes that possible, and this group collects the mathematical tools on which the rest of the chapter silently relies. They are the hash function, which condenses any data into a short fingerprint; the key pair and the public-key cryptography behind it; the elliptic curve on which Bitcoin's keys live; the digital signature that proves entitlement; and the checksum that protects a string from typing errors. The chapter itself only names these tools in passing, so each is given a plain explanation here (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nAlmost every later concept of the chapter is built from these tools. A transaction identifier is a hash, a block is identified by a hash, mining is a search for a hash with a special property, and an input is accepted only when it carries a signature that was made with the right private key. A reader who does not know what a hash or a signature guarantees cannot judge what the network can and cannot promise. The book's own chapter on keys says it will examine public keys, private keys, signatures and hash functions and then use all of them together to describe addresses (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThese tools are met far beyond Bitcoin. Hash functions protect stored passwords and verify downloaded files, public-key cryptography secures every encrypted web connection, and checksums guard credit card numbers and bank account numbers against typing mistakes. The book notes that public-key cryptography was invented in the 1970s and is a mathematical foundation for modern computer and information security (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Bitcoin chooses among such tools and combines them in a particular way, and the combination is what is new.\n\nThe concepts of the group are taken in the order in which a learner needs them. The hash function comes first because the others use it. The key pair and public-key cryptography follow, since they explain who can spend, and the elliptic curve explains the mathematics under Bitcoin's keys. The digital signature then joins keys and hashes into a proof, and the checksum closes the group with a use of hashing for error detection. Each concept has a small calculation that you can run, and the calculations were all executed with the Python standard library.\n\nTwo warnings are in order. First, these tools are quite different in what they promise: a hash function provides no secrecy, a signature provides no secrecy either, and neither is the same thing as encryption. Second, a clever tool is only as safe as its use. The book warns that anything less than completely random when a private key is chosen can significantly reduce the security of the key and of the bitcoins it controls (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Read each concept below with the question what exactly does this guarantee, and what does it leave open?" ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Cryptography> .
chx:S_HashFunction a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Hash function"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Hash function" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 17 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Cryptography ;
    skos:definition "A hash function is a procedure that takes data of any length, however long or short, scrambles it thoroughly and outputs a value of fixed length, which is called the hash or the digest of the data. The book describes it as a function that takes a potentially large amount of data, scrambles it, and outputs a fixed amount of data, and it points out two properties. The same input always gives the same output, and a secure function makes it impractical for anybody to choose a different input that produces an output seen before (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nBecause of these two properties a digest works as a commitment: it is a promise that, in practice, only one input produces it. This lets Bitcoin refer to large objects by a short name that nobody can redirect to something else. A hash is also the raw material of mining, since the network's lottery is a search for an input whose hash falls below a threshold, and nobody can find such an input except by trying one after another (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Without hash functions Bitcoin would need a central party to say what a transaction or a block is.\n\nBitcoin uses the function called SHA-256, whose output is always 256 bits long, that is, 64 hexadecimal digits, whatever the size of the input (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The identifier of a transaction, the identifier of a block, each node of a merkle tree and the proof of work are all results of applying SHA-256 twice in a row, which the book calls double-SHA256 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Outside Bitcoin the same family of functions checks the integrity of downloaded software and stores passwords in a form that cannot be read back.\n\nYou can try it directly. Hashing the phrase Hello, world! followed by a line break with SHA-256 gives d9014c46...f72ff5, the value printed in the book, and the result has 64 hexadecimal digits, that is 256 bits (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Changing a single character changes the whole result: hashing the same phrase with a question mark in place of the exclamation mark produces a digest that differs from the first in 61 of its 64 digits. Nobody can predict how a digest will change, and this unpredictability, together with the fixed length, is what makes searching for a special hash a pure matter of trial.\n\nA hash function is not encryption. It has no key and no way back, so you cannot recover the input from the digest, but if the possible inputs are few, anyone can simply hash all of them and compare. A digest also says nothing about who made the data: anyone can compute it. And the guarantee that two different inputs will not give the same digest is a statement about the effort needed to find such a pair, not an absolute impossibility, because a function with a fixed-length output must map many inputs to the same output; the book calls such a pair a collision and says finding one is computationally infeasible (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#HashFunction> .
chx:S_KeyPair a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Key pair"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Key pair" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 18 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Cryptography ;
    skos:definition "A key pair consists of two numbers that belong together: a private key and a public key. In Bitcoin the private key is a number, usually derived from a number picked at random, and the public key is calculated from it by a mathematical operation that is easy in one direction and impractical in the other. The book states that a Bitcoin wallet contains a collection of key pairs, each consisting of a private key and a public key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe pair separates two abilities that ordinary passwords combine. Anyone may be told the public key, and with it can receive funds or check a signature, but only the holder of the private key can produce a valid signature. The book says that control over the private key is the root of user control over all funds associated with the corresponding public key, and that revealing the private key to third parties is equivalent to giving them control over the bitcoins it secures (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The same text adds that a lost private key cannot be recovered, and the funds it secured are lost forever.\n\nEvery wallet manages key pairs, as the chapter's wallets for Alice and Bob do, although the chapter itself never shows one. The private key is usually written as 64 hexadecimal digits, that is, 256 bits, and the book gives 1E99423A...A526AEDD as an example of a randomly generated private key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Outside Bitcoin, key pairs are what secure shell logins, signed software updates and encrypted email use as their basis.\n\nThe private key can be any number from 0 up to n minus 1, where n is a constant slightly smaller than 2 to the power 256, defined as the order of the elliptic curve that Bitcoin uses; to create one, software picks a random 256-bit number and checks that it is below n (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The public key K is then obtained by multiplying a fixed point G on the curve by the private key k, so that K equals k times G. The book prints the public key of its example key as the coordinates x = F028892B...DC341A and y = 07CF33DA...2E505BDB, and an implementation of the multiplication in the Python standard library reproduces both numbers from the private key.\n\nThe private key is a number, not a file or a word, and its secrecy depends entirely on how it was chosen. The book advises against writing one's own random number generator and recommends a cryptographically secure generator seeded with enough entropy, because a process that is less than completely random can significantly reduce the security of the key (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The public key cannot be turned back into the private key by the known mathematics, but this is a statement about today's computers and algorithms, not a proof that it can never be done." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#KeyPair> .
chx:S_PublicKeyCryptography a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Public-key cryptography"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Public-key cryptography" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 19 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Cryptography ;
    skos:definition "Public-key cryptography, also called asymmetric cryptography, is a branch of cryptography in which each participant has two related keys instead of one shared secret. One key can be made public and the other must stay private. It rests on mathematical functions that are easy to calculate in one direction and infeasible to calculate in the opposite direction with the computers and algorithms available today. The book names two such functions, prime number exponentiation and elliptic curve multiplication, and says that Bitcoin uses elliptic curve addition and multiplication (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nBefore this invention, two parties who wanted to protect a message needed to share a secret in advance, which does not scale to a network of strangers. Public-key cryptography removes that need. The book explains that in Bitcoin it is not used to encrypt transactions, because transactions are public. Its useful property is the ability to generate digital signatures: a private key can be applied to a transaction to produce a signature that only someone who knows the private key could have made, yet anyone with the public key and the transaction can verify it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nYou use it every time a browser opens a secure web page, and wallets use it every time they spend. The book says that public-key cryptography was invented in the 1970s and is a mathematical foundation for modern computer and information security (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In Bitcoin, the public key is used to receive funds and the private key is used to sign the transactions that spend them, and a node verifies the signatures without ever learning a private key.\n\nA tiny example shows the idea of a one-way function. In arithmetic with remainders modulo 17, the power 3 to the 7th gives 2187, which leaves the remainder 11 when divided by 17, and computing this takes a few multiplications. Going backward means asking which exponent gives remainder 11; with so small a modulus one can simply try all exponents and find that the answer is 7. With numbers of 256 bits the same search is hopeless, and that gap between forward and backward effort is what the cryptography relies on. Bitcoin uses the elliptic-curve version of this problem, which the concept of the elliptic curve describes.\n\nThe security is conditional. It depends on the assumption that no efficient method of reversing the function is known, and on the keys having been chosen at random; the book says that the private key must stay secret at all times and be protected from accidental loss (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The word public must not be misread: the public key may be shared, but the private key must never be. Note, too, that signing is not encrypting, so a Bitcoin transaction is readable by everyone, and its privacy rests on other things, such as the fact that addresses name no person." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#PublicKeyCryptography> .
chx:S_EllipticCurve a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Elliptic curve"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Elliptic curve" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 20 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Cryptography ;
    skos:definition "An elliptic curve is the set of points whose two coordinates satisfy a certain cubic equation. Bitcoin uses one particular curve, defined in a standard called secp256k1, whose equation is the very simple y squared equals x cubed plus 7. The calculation is done not with ordinary real numbers but modulo a very large prime number p, that is, over a finite field, so that the curve becomes a scattered pattern of points rather than a smooth line (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nPoints on an elliptic curve can be added to each other by a geometric rule, and adding a point to itself repeatedly gives multiplication of a point by a whole number. This multiplication is easy to carry out but, when the numbers are large, practically impossible to undo: given the result, finding the number is the discrete logarithm problem. The book calls this a trap door function and says it is the basis for unforgeable and secure digital signatures that prove control over bitcoin funds (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Bitcoin's choice of this particular curve and its constants is part of the protocol, so every implementation must use exactly the same ones.\n\nThe curve appears wherever Bitcoin software derives a public key from a private key or checks a signature. The book notes that many implementations use the libsecp256k1 library for the curve mathematics (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), and Bitcoin Core carries a copy of it in its source tree. Elliptic curves are also used in other protocols, such as secure web connections, because they give strong security with short keys.\n\nThe curve secp256k1 is fixed by the constants p, the generator point G and the order n. The prime is p = 2^256 - 2^32 - 977, which the book writes as 2^256 minus 2^32 minus 2^9 minus 2^8 minus 2^7 minus 2^6 minus 2^4 minus 1; the two forms are equal, because the powers from 2^9 down to 2^4 and the 1 add up to 977 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The generator point is the same for everybody, and a check with exact integer arithmetic shows that its two coordinates satisfy the curve equation modulo p, since the remainder of y squared minus x cubed minus 7 is 0. Over a toy field of only 17 elements the same equation has just 17 pairs of coordinates, which is why a picture of such a curve is only an illustration.\n\nThe picture of a smooth curve in textbooks is misleading for Bitcoin. With a finite field the curve is a cloud of points in a very large grid, and the geometric rule of tangent lines and reflections is only an analogy for the algebra that is actually computed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Writing your own implementation of the mathematics is also dangerous: simple code like that in the examples of this course is not protected against timing attacks, and real wallets use carefully reviewed libraries." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#EllipticCurve> .
chx:S_DigitalSignature a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Digital signature"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Digital signature" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 21 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Cryptography ;
    skos:definition "A digital signature is a number, or a pair of numbers, calculated from a message and a private key, which anyone can check against the corresponding public key. The book explains that a signature consists of two parts: an algorithm for creating a signature for a message using a private key, and an algorithm that allows anyone to verify the signature given the message and the public key. In Bitcoin the message is the transaction, or more exactly a hash of a specific part of the transaction data (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe chapter says that a transaction contains proof of ownership for each amount being spent, in the form of a digital signature from the owner that can be independently validated by anyone (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The later chapter on signatures lists three purposes: the signature proves that the controller of the private key has authorized the spending, the proof cannot be denied afterwards, and the authorized transaction cannot be changed by unauthenticated third parties (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Together these replace the bank's signature card and the clerk's judgement with a calculation that thousands of independent nodes can repeat.\n\nTwo signature algorithms are used in Bitcoin today, the Schnorr signature algorithm and the elliptic curve digital signature algorithm, ECDSA (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Alice's real transaction carries one witness item of 65 bytes, which is the length that the book gives for a Schnorr signature of 64 bytes followed by one byte that names the signature-hash type; its last byte is 01, the code of the type that signs almost the whole transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Outside Bitcoin, digital signatures are used to sign software updates, contracts and web certificates.\n\nThe mechanism can be run with the textbook ECDSA formulas on the curve of the previous concept. Using the book's example private key, the program signs the message pay Bob 75000 and obtains two numbers r and s; checking them against the public key returns True. If the message is changed to pay Bob 95000, the same pair fails the check and returns False, which is the integrity guarantee in miniature. The program fixes the secret nonce k for the sake of a repeatable output, which a real signer must never do: the book describes the nonce as private, and notes that the extra complexity of ECDSA has in some cases resulted in software accidentally leaking private keys (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA signature is tied to what was signed. Bitcoin lets a signer choose, with a flag called SIGHASH, which part of the transaction the signature commits to, so a signature is not simply a stamp on the whole document (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The signature also does not identify a person: it shows only that someone controlling the private key authorized this transaction. Finally, forging a signature without the private key is believed infeasible, not proven impossible, and a stolen private key lets a thief sign validly, with no way for the network to tell." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#DigitalSignature> .
chx:S_Checksum a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Checksum"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Checksum" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 22 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Cryptography ;
    skos:definition "A checksum is a short piece of data calculated from a longer piece and attached to it, so that a recipient can recompute it and see whether the longer piece was damaged on the way. The book describes the checksum in the old base58check address format as an additional four bytes added to the end of the data, derived from the hash of the encoded data, which can therefore be used to detect transcription and typing errors (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The newer bech32 format has a stronger checksum of its own.\n\nAddresses are typed, spoken, copied and photographed by people, and a single wrong character could send funds to an address that nobody controls. A checksum turns that silent loss into an immediate error message. The book says that it prevents a mistyped Bitcoin address from being accepted by the wallet software as a valid destination, an error that would otherwise result in loss of funds (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Bech32 goes further: in an address of the expected length it is guaranteed to detect any error affecting four characters or fewer, and for an address typed with a few errors it can tell the user where the errors are (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nEvery Bitcoin address has a checksum, and Bob's address in the chapter's payment request, bc1qk2g6u8..., is a bech32 address. The same idea protects credit card numbers, bank account numbers and ISBNs. In Bitcoin Improvement Proposal 173 the checksum of bech32 is defined by a polynomial calculation over the characters, with a final value that must equal a fixed constant for the address to be valid (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nThe older method is easy to run. The checksum of the data is the first four bytes of the double-SHA256 hash of the data. For the word bitcoin that gives f1ef1bf1, and changing a letter of the word produces four entirely different bytes, so an altered text is detected with overwhelming probability (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Bob's bech32 address can be verified with the procedure in BIP 173: running the specified calculation over the address gives the value 1 for the address in the payment request, and for the same address with one character changed the result is different from 1, so the wallet refuses it (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nA checksum detects accidents, not attacks. An adversary who changes the data can simply compute a new matching checksum, so a checksum protects against a mistyped character but not against a malicious substitution, for which one needs a signature. The guarantee of bech32 also holds only if the length is unchanged: the book reports that in the original bech32 design it was easy to add or remove letters q before a final p without invalidating the checksum, a weakness that the later bech32m variant was made to remove (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Checksum> .
chx:S_BitsAndBytes a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Bit and byte"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Bit and byte" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 23 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Cryptography ;
    skos:definition "A bit is the smallest unit of digital information: a single binary digit that is either 0 or 1. A byte is a group of eight bits, and it is the unit in which computers normally measure the length of data. The two units describe the same thing at different granularities, so one length can always be converted into the other by multiplying or dividing by eight. Every size that this chapter quotes, from the 32 bytes of a transaction identifier to the 256 bits of a private key, is a statement in one of these two units.\n\nThe chapter cannot be read without them. The book says that the output of SHA256 is always 256 bits long whatever the size of the input, that an input refers to an earlier transaction by a 32-byte identifier, and that output indexes are 4-byte unsigned integers (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). These are the same kind of statement, and a reader who cannot move between the two units will think that a 256-bit hash and a 32-byte hash are different objects. Precise sizes also matter for a different reason: the room a transaction takes in a block is counted in bytes, and that is what its fee buys.\n\nYou meet both units in every tool. Bitcoin Core reports the size of Alice's transaction as 194, counting bytes; the book describes a key as a 256-bit number; a block header is 80 bytes, which is 640 bits; and the chapter's figures for the first synchronization of a node are given in gigabytes, which are multiples of the byte (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Outside Bitcoin the same units measure files, memory and network speeds, where the bit is traditional for transmission rates and the byte for storage.\n\nThe conversion is a multiplication by eight. The identifier of the first block's only transaction is 32 bytes of data, and reading it with the standard library and multiplying its length by eight gives 256, so the two descriptions agree. A byte written in hexadecimal takes exactly two characters, because one hexadecimal digit stands for four bits, which is why a 32-byte value is printed as 64 characters, as the book notes when it says that it takes 64 hexadecimal characters to display 32 bytes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nWatch the capital letter and the prefix. A lowercase b usually means bit and a capital B means byte, so 1 MB and 1 Mb differ by a factor of eight. Prefixes are also ambiguous: a kilobyte may mean 1,000 or 1,024 bytes depending on the author, which is one reason the book quotes the block limit as a weight of 4,000,000 rather than in megabytes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). And a byte is not a character: text becomes bytes only after an encoding is chosen, which is why the standard for payment requests has to say that each octet of the UTF-8 sequence is percent-encoded (Schneider and Corallo, 2012)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#BitsAndBytes> .
chx:S_HexadecimalNotation a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Hexadecimal notation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Hexadecimal notation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 24 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Cryptography ;
    skos:definition "Hexadecimal notation writes numbers in base sixteen, using the ten digits 0 to 9 and then the letters a to f for the values ten to fifteen. Because sixteen is two to the fourth power, one hexadecimal digit corresponds exactly to four bits and two digits to one byte. This makes hexadecimal the usual way to print binary data for human eyes: it is far shorter than writing out bits and, unlike decimal, it lines up exactly with byte boundaries.\n\nAlmost every value this chapter quotes is written in hexadecimal: the transaction identifier, the block hash, the private key, the address's underlying program, the compact form of the mining target. Without the notation these would have to be printed as enormous decimal numbers in which no part corresponds to a field. The notation is also what lets a reader check a length at a glance, since the book's remark that 64 hexadecimal characters display 32 bytes is just the statement that two digits make a byte (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe identifier of Alice's payment, 46620030...d1f96177, is 64 hexadecimal characters. The compact encoding of the first block's mining target is printed as 1d00ffff, and the same block's hash begins with a long run of zeros, which is only visible because the hash is written in hexadecimal. Outside Bitcoin, colours in web pages, memory addresses in debuggers and bytes in network traces are all written the same way.\n\nConverting is a matter of weighted digits. The two-digit value ff stands for fifteen sixteens plus fifteen, that is 255, the largest value a byte can hold; the four-byte value 1d00ffff read as one number is 486,604,799. Python's standard library converts in both directions, with int(text, 16) reading a hexadecimal string and hex(number) writing one, and the small program above shows all three results together. Hexadecimal is case-insensitive as a notation, although Bitcoin's newer address format deliberately forbids mixing cases for a different reason, its checksum (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nTwo traps are worth naming. The first is that hexadecimal is only a notation: the identifier of a transaction is 32 bytes of data, not 64 characters of text, and a program that hashes the printed form instead of the bytes gets a completely different answer. The second is the order in which the bytes are written down, which Bitcoin does differently in different places; the concept of byte order explains that trap in detail." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#HexadecimalNotation> .
chx:S_Entropy a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Entropy"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Entropy" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 25 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Cryptography ;
    skos:definition "Entropy, in the sense used in cryptography, is a measure of how unpredictable a value is to someone who does not know it. It is counted in bits: a value drawn uniformly at random from two equally likely possibilities has one bit of entropy, from four possibilities two bits, and from two to the power 256 possibilities 256 bits. Entropy is therefore not a property of the number itself but of the process that produced it; the same number may be unguessable if it was drawn at random and worthless if it was the year of someone's birth.\n\nEvery guarantee in this chapter that rests on a secret rests on entropy. A private key is only safe because an attacker would have to search an unimaginably large space, and that space is large only if the key was really chosen at random. The book makes the point sharply: it advises the reader not to write their own code to create a random number, to use a cryptographically secure pseudorandom number generator seeded with sufficient entropy, and it warns that any process that is less than completely random can significantly reduce the security of the private key and of the bitcoins it controls (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nYou meet entropy wherever a secret is generated: when a wallet creates a key, when a browser sets up an encrypted connection, when a system picks a password salt. The book compares the size of Bitcoin's private key space, two to the power 256, with the number of atoms in the visible universe, and says that picking a private key is in effect picking a number between 1 and two to the power 256 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The same chapter notes that the private key must be less than the order of the curve, a number only slightly smaller than that bound.\n\nCounting entropy is counting possibilities. If a value is chosen uniformly from N equally likely possibilities it has the base-two logarithm of N bits of entropy, so a uniform 256-bit number has 256 bits, which the small calculation above confirms by taking the bit length of two to the power 256 and subtracting one. Weak choices collapse this number: a key derived from a six-letter lowercase word has at most the entropy of twenty-six to the sixth power, about twenty-eight bits, and a modern computer can try every one of those in seconds. The gap between twenty-eight and 256 bits is the whole difference between a secret and a formality.\n\nEntropy cannot be judged by looking at a value. A key full of apparently random digits may have been produced by a predictable generator, and the result will look exactly as unguessable as a real one. Nor does more entropy help anywhere else: the chapter's hashes, identifiers and addresses are public values, and adding randomness to them changes nothing. Entropy matters only for the values that must stay secret, which in this chapter means the private key behind the signature." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Entropy> .
chx:S_Nonce a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Nonce"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Nonce" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 26 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Cryptography ;
    skos:definition "A nonce is a value that is varied in order to change the output of a cryptographic function without changing the data that is really being protected. The word is a contraction of number used once. The book introduces it in exactly this role: a variable used to vary the output of a cryptographic function, in that case to vary the output of the SHA256 commitment to a phrase (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Two very different nonces appear in this chapter, and keeping them apart matters: the secret nonce used when a signature is made, and the public nonce in a block header that a miner changes while searching for a proof of work.\n\nA nonce is what turns a fixed, deterministic function into something that can be searched. A hash function always gives the same output for the same input, so a miner who wants a different output must change the input, and the nonce is the field reserved for that purpose. In signing, the nonce serves the opposite purpose: it makes two signatures over the same message different, which is necessary for the mathematics of the signature scheme to stay secure. The book describes the signing step as first generating a private nonce, and warns that the extra complexity of handling it has in some cases resulted in software accidentally leaking private keys (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe mining nonce is a field of the block header, four bytes wide, described by the book's glossary as a 32-bit field whose value is set so that the hash of the block will contain a run of leading zeros, while the rest of the fields may not be changed because they have a defined meaning (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter's own description of mining is the same idea in words: if the output does not match the template, the miner makes a small change to the candidate block and tries again (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The signing nonce is not stored anywhere: it is used once and discarded.\n\nThe book's own miniature example can be run. Setting the goal that the hexadecimal hash of the phrase Hello, world! followed by a number should begin with a zero, and trying the numbers in order, the first that succeeds is 32, giving the digest 09cb91f8...ccbec314, exactly the value and the nonce that the book prints (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). One hash verifies the answer, while thirty-two were needed to find it, and that asymmetry is the whole of proof of work. The mining nonce has only about four billion possible values, so real miners exhaust it and then change another field, which the book calls the extra nonce solution (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe two nonces must never be confused, because their rules are opposite. The mining nonce is public and may be anything; the signing nonce must be secret and must never be reused, since reusing one with the same key allows the private key to be recovered. A nonce is also not a password or a key: in mining it is simply the part of the input that the searcher is allowed to change, and in signing it is a one-time random value that is thrown away as soon as the signature exists." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Nonce> .
chx:S_Encryption a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Encryption"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Encryption" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 27 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Cryptography ;
    skos:definition "Encryption is the transformation of data into a form that cannot be read without a key, together with the matching transformation that turns it back. Its purpose is secrecy. It is one of several things that cryptography can provide, and this chapter mentions it mainly in order to say what Bitcoin does not do with it. The term is needed because two of the tools explained here, the hash function and the digital signature, are regularly mistaken for encryption, and the mistake leads to wrong expectations about what the system protects.\n\nThe distinction decides what is private on a blockchain. The book states plainly that public-key cryptography in Bitcoin is not used to encrypt, that is, to make secret, the transactions, and that its useful property is instead the ability to generate digital signatures (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Every transaction in this chapter, including Alice's, is therefore readable by anyone: the amounts, the conditions on the outputs and the signature itself are public. What a reader cannot do is forge a signature, and that is a guarantee about authorship, not about secrecy.\n\nEncryption is met all around Bitcoin even though it is not inside a transaction. A browser connecting to Bob's shop uses it, a wallet file on disk may be encrypted with a passphrase, and the book's network chapter describes encrypted and authenticated connections between nodes, so that an observer of the wire cannot read what two peers exchange (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The course notes for this chapter make the same separation when they describe the key as what unlocks a previous output rather than as something that hides data (Altunel, 2021).\n\nThe simplest possible illustration is a reversible operation with a key. Combining each byte of the words pay Bob with the key value 0x5a by the exclusive-or operation gives the unreadable bytes 2a3b237a183538, and applying the very same operation again with the same key returns the original text, as the program above shows. Real encryption uses far stronger constructions, but the shape is this: one operation forward with a key, one back with a key. A hash function has no such backward step and no key, and a signature does not hide the message it is attached to.\n\nDo not expect encryption to give privacy on the blockchain, and do not expect signatures to give secrecy. The book's chapter on keys lists the private key's requirement as remaining secret at all times, which is a matter of storage and not of encrypting transactions (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Note also that encrypting a payment would defeat the system's purpose: nodes must read a transaction in order to verify it, which is why the data is public and the privacy of a user rests on other things, such as the fact that an address names no person." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Encryption> .
chx:S_Linkage a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Linkage"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Linkage" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 28 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Transaction ;
    skos:definition "A single transaction is only the beginning of the story, because bitcoins are never created in a vacuum: each is spent from something that existed before and gives rise to something that will exist afterwards. This group deals with the links between transactions. It explains the chain that results when each transaction spends the outputs of an earlier one, the change that a spender receives back because an output cannot be partly spent, and the choice that a wallet makes among the outputs it could spend, which is called coin selection.\n\nThe links are what turn individual transactions into a history, and the history is what lets any node decide whether a payment is genuine. If Alice's payment spends an output created by Joe's transaction, and that output has not been spent before, the payment is valid in this respect; if it has been spent, the payment is a double spend. The three concepts of the group show how the requirement is met in practice, and also how wallet design decisions leave traces that others can read.\n\nThe chapter makes the links visible in two illustrations: a chain of three transactions in which the output of one is the input of the next, and the later chain from Joe through Alice and Bob to Gopesh, Bob's web designer (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The idea is met in block explorers, which show for every input the transaction it spends, and in every wallet, which has to remember which outputs it can still use.\n\nThe group is taken in the order of the story. The transaction chain explains what a link is and how Alice's payment is connected to the payment she received from Joe. Making change explains why Alice's transaction has two outputs. Coin selection explains how a wallet picks the inputs in the first place. The sizes of the amounts in the book's figures, 100,000, 75,000, 20,000 and 5,000 satoshis, are used throughout and were checked by calculation.\n\nLinks are public, and public links can be followed by anybody. A chain of transactions is a record of where each output came from, so the choices that a wallet makes in building the links affect the privacy of its owner. Keep this in mind while reading the concepts: the same facts that make Bitcoin auditable also make it traceable." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Linkage> .
chx:S_TransactionChain a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction chain"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transaction chain" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 29 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Linkage ;
    skos:definition "A transaction chain is the sequence of transactions that arises when each transaction spends outputs created by an earlier one. In the chapter, Alice's payment to Bob's store uses the output of a previous transaction as its input: the one in which her friend Joe gave her bitcoins in return for cash. The book labels that earlier transaction Tx1 and Alice's payment Tx2, and its figure shows Tx2 referring to Tx1 by an arrow and by the label Tx1:0 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe chain is the answer to the question of where bitcoins come from and where they have gone. Because each input names the output it consumes, anyone can follow a payment back through the chain of ownership, and the owner of an output can be changed only by a transaction that spends it. When Bob spends the output he received, he extends the chain; the chapter's last illustration shows Alice's transaction as part of a chain from Joe to Gopesh (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The same structure underlies the idea of provenance, which the final concepts of this chapter take up.\n\nThe book describes the reference in an actual transaction as the 32-byte transaction identifier of the transaction in which Alice received the money from Joe, followed by a colon and a number that gives the position of the output, counted from zero (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Software cannot read the value of an input from the input itself: the book stresses that actual Bitcoin transactions do not explicitly include the value of their inputs, so software has to find the previous transaction output being spent (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe numbers in the book's illustration make the chain concrete. Tx1 sent 100,000 satoshis to an output locked by Alice's key. Tx2 spends it and creates two outputs, 75,000 satoshis for the podcast and 20,000 satoshis back to Alice, and the 5,000 satoshis that are left over form its fee, as the calculation 100000 - 75000 - 20000 gives. Bob then spends the 75,000 output, which is Tx2:1, in Tx3, which pays 67,000 satoshis in a single output, so that the fee of Tx3 is 75000 - 67000, or 8,000 satoshis, as the figure states (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Each link is simply a pair of a transaction identifier and an output position.\n\nThe figure is a simplification, and the real transaction differs from it in a detail. In the figure Alice's input is labelled Tx1:0, which is the output of 100,000 satoshis, the first of the two outputs drawn for Tx1; but in the bytes of the real transaction the output index stored in the input is 1. The order of Tx2's outputs, by contrast, agrees with the figure: the change of 20,000 satoshis is at position 0 and Bob's 75,000 satoshis at position 1, which is why Tx3 spends Tx2:1 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Remember also that a chain of transactions links outputs, not people: it does not say who the owners are, only which outputs were spent to create which others." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#TransactionChain> .
chx:S_MakingChange a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Making change"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Making change" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 30 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Linkage ;
    skos:definition "Making change is the practice of adding to a transaction an output that returns the surplus to the person who is paying. The book explains the need with money in a shop: if you buy a five-dollar item with a twenty-dollar bill you expect fifteen dollars back, and in the same way a transaction input, like a currency note, cannot be partly spent (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). If a purchase costs 5 bitcoins and you only have an input worth 20, you send one output of 5 to the store owner and one of 15 back to yourself, not counting the fee (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nWithout change, every payment would have to match an available output exactly, or the surplus would be given to the miner as an enormous fee. The change output lets a wallet spend any output, whatever its size, and keep the remainder. It also explains why most payments have the same shape: one input and two outputs, one for the receiver and one for the sender (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). For the sender, the change output is simply a new output that can be spent in a later transaction.\n\nAlice's payment is the example. Her single input of 100,000 satoshis cannot be divided, so her wallet creates a payment output of 75,000 satoshis for Bob's store and a change output of 20,000 satoshis for herself, and the remaining 5,000 satoshis are the fee (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book adds that, at the level of the protocol, there is no difference between a change output and a payment output, and it calls the address the change goes to a change address (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe wallet's arithmetic is a subtraction. With an input worth 100000 satoshis, a payment of 75000 and a fee of 5000, the change is 100000 - 75000 - 5000, which is 20000. The result matches the first output that the real transaction contains. A transaction without a change output is possible too; the book calls it a changeless transaction, says it can have only a single output, and shows Bob creating one as Tx3. It is practical only when the amount to spend is about the same as the inputs minus the fee (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe book hopes that payment and change look identical on the blockchain, since the change address is often a new address of the sender's wallet, for privacy reasons, and a third party could not then tell which output is the change (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Alice's real transaction does not fully achieve that ideal: its two output scripts have different lengths, 34 and 22 bytes, so an observer can tell them apart. And the change is not extra money: it is an ordinary output that nobody can use unless the wallet keeps the key, so a wallet that loses the change key loses the change." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#MakingChange> .
chx:S_CoinSelection a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Coin selection"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Coin selection" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 31 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Linkage ;
    skos:definition "Coin selection is the process by which a wallet decides which of its unspent outputs to use as inputs in a payment. Different wallets use different strategies. The book says they might aggregate many small inputs, or use one that is equal to or larger than the desired payment, and that unless the wallet can match the payment plus the fee exactly, it will need to generate change (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe choice has consequences that last. The book compares it with handling cash: if you always use the largest bill in your pocket you end up with a pocket full of loose change, and if you only use the loose change you will often be left with only big bills. People subconsciously find a balance between these extremes, and Bitcoin wallet developers strive to program this balance (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A wallet that always picks small outputs builds large transactions that cost more in fees; one that always picks large outputs creates many small outputs that may later be too small to be worth spending.\n\nIn the chapter the choice is trivial, because Alice's wallet holds one output that is sufficient to pay for the podcast; had it not been, her wallet might have had to combine several smaller outputs, like picking coins from a purse, until it could find enough (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Real wallets perform coin selection on every payment, and some let the user choose the outputs by hand.\n\nA simple strategy can be programmed in a few lines. Suppose a wallet holds outputs of 60000, 30000 and 25000 satoshis and must pay 75000 plus a fee of 5000, a need of 80000. Taking outputs from the largest down until the sum reaches the need picks 60000 and 30000, whose sum 90000 leaves a change of 10000. This is only one of many strategies, and its trade-offs differ from those of, for example, preferring the smallest sufficient set or the set that gives no change at all.\n\nThe selection is visible to the outside world. Spending several outputs in one transaction shows that one party probably controls all of them, which weakens privacy. And every input costs space and therefore fee, so the cheapest set now is not always the best for later. Treat coin selection as a design decision with several competing aims, and remember that the book presents it as a balance, not as a single correct method." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#CoinSelection> .
chx:S_Form a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Form"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Form" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 32 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Transaction ;
    skos:definition "Transactions come in a few typical shapes, which this group calls forms. A form is described by how many inputs and outputs a transaction has and by what it is used for. The chapter names three: the simple payment, with one input and two outputs; the consolidation, which spends several inputs into a single output; and payment batching, which pays several recipients with a single transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nRecognizing the forms helps a reader to interpret what a block explorer displays and to guess what the sender intended. A transaction with many inputs and one output is probably a tidy-up of small amounts, one with many outputs is probably a business paying many people, and one with one input and two outputs is probably an ordinary payment with change. The forms also show that nothing in the format limits a transaction to one payer and one payee.\n\nThe forms are met first in the three illustrations of the chapter: the most common transaction, the consolidation that aggregates funds, and the batch that distributes them (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). They are then met in the world. Ordinary wallets create simple payments all day; wallets and businesses create consolidations as housekeeping, usually when fees are low; and services that owe money to many people create batches on a schedule. A reader who opens almost any block in an explorer will find all three among its transactions, and being able to name them makes a block readable instead of a wall of numbers.\n\nThe group is taken from the commonest to the least common. The simple payment is the baseline that the rest of the chapter has used, and the real transaction of Alice has one input and two outputs, which is checked by reading its bytes. The consolidation reverses the proportions, and the batch spreads the outputs. All three obey the same rule, that the outputs together cannot exceed the inputs and the difference is the fee.\n\nA form is a description of a typical shape and not a rule of the protocol, which sees only a list of inputs and a list of outputs and checks the same conditions whatever the shape. The names used here are the conventions of the course and of the book, so another text may count the forms differently. A transaction can also combine the features, for example by consolidating many inputs while paying two recipients and returning change, in which case no single name fits. Finally, a form says nothing about who built the transaction or why: the shape is a hint about intention, never evidence of it, and reading too much into it is the mistake that the privacy remarks of this branch warn against." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Form> .
chx:S_SimplePayment a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Simple payment"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Simple payment" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 33 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Form ;
    skos:definition "A simple payment is the most common form of transaction. It has one input and two outputs: the first output pays the receiver and the second returns the change to the sender (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is the shape of Alice's payment to Bob's store, and the form that most people have in mind when they speak of sending bitcoin.\n\nThe form is common because it is the natural result of how outputs behave. A person usually owns an output that is larger than the item to be bought, an output cannot be partly spent, and so the wallet writes one output for the seller and one back for the buyer. Understanding it explains why Alice's balance after paying Bob is not simply her earlier balance minus 75,000 satoshis in a single step: the old output disappears and a new, smaller one takes its place.\n\nThe shape is the one in the chapter's figure of the most common transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is also what the real transaction contains: a program that reads the bytes of Alice's transaction finds one input and two outputs, so the pair (1, 2) is exactly what the form prescribes. The same shape can be seen in the large majority of everyday payments on a block explorer.\n\nCounting is all that is needed. If the number of inputs is one and the number of outputs is two, and the amounts are 100000 for the input, 75000 and 20000 for the outputs, then the fee is the 5000 that remains. The order of the outputs is not fixed by the form: in Alice's real transaction the change comes first and the payment second, which is why the book's later figure refers to the output Tx2:1 when Bob spends his payment (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA simple payment is a description, not a promise. Some payments have a single input and a single output, the changeless transactions, and some have a second output that is not change, for instance a donation. The two-output pattern also invites a privacy question: when the outputs have different amounts, an observer may guess that the larger one is the change, or the smaller; the guess is only a guess." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#SimplePayment> .
chx:S_Consolidation a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Consolidation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Consolidation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 34 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Form ;
    skos:definition "A consolidation transaction spends several inputs into a single output. The book offers a picture from everyday money: it is the real-world equivalent of exchanging a pile of coins and currency notes for a single larger note (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Wallets and businesses create such transactions to clean up lots of smaller amounts.\n\nMany small outputs are inconvenient. Each needs a separate input when it is spent, and every input adds to the size and therefore to the fee of a later transaction. A wallet or business that has received many small payments can combine them in one transaction, at a time when fees are low, and later pay with a single large output. Because the chapter on coin selection explains that wallets balance small and large outputs, the consolidation can be seen as the inverse of an ordinary payment.\n\nThe form is shown in the chapter's illustration of a consolidation transaction aggregating funds (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Exchanges and shops that receive many small payments run consolidations regularly, and block explorers show them as transactions with many inputs and a single output. The book does not give a fixed number of inputs: it says only that the transaction spends several inputs into a single output.\n\nThe arithmetic is unchanged: the single output is the sum of the inputs minus the fee. As an illustration, three outputs of 30000, 25000 and 20000 satoshis consolidated with a fee of 1500 give one output of 73500 satoshis. The numbers are invented for the example, and the fee is assumed. What matters is the shape, many inputs and one output.\n\nConsolidation has costs that the picture of a single note hides. All the inputs are spent together, and so a watcher can conclude that they probably belong to the same owner, which is a loss of privacy. It must also be paid for now: spending many inputs makes a large transaction, and a large transaction pays a larger total fee than a small one at the same fee rate." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Consolidation> .
chx:S_PaymentBatching a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Payment batching"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Payment batching" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 35 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Form ;
    skos:definition "Payment batching is the form in which one transaction pays to multiple outputs representing multiple recipients. The book says that it is seen often on the blockchain and that commercial entities use it to distribute funds, for example when processing payroll payments to multiple employees (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The transaction has few inputs, often one, and many outputs.\n\nA business that pays many people would otherwise create a separate transaction for each, and every one of those transactions would carry its own overhead. A batch gathers the payments in one transaction. It is the counterpart of the consolidation: instead of many inputs flowing into one output, one flow of funds is spread to many outputs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A batch also makes the payer's own accounting simpler, as a single transaction records the whole payroll.\n\nThe chapter shows the form in its illustration of a batch transaction distributing funds, drawn as one input flowing into several outputs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In practice the businesses that use it are those that owe money to many people at once: exchanges paying withdrawals, payroll services paying salaries, mining pools paying their members. A reader meets such a transaction in a block explorer as a short list of inputs above a long list of outputs, each with its own address and amount, and can usually tell at a glance that one party built it. The same shape also appears whenever a service combines the payments of many customers to save fees, which is why a batch is not always the work of a single payer.\n\nThe arithmetic is again the same: the inputs equal the outputs plus the fee. Suppose a single input of 200000 satoshis is used to pay four employees 60000, 55000, 50000 and 32000 satoshis, a total of 197000, and a fee of 3000 is paid. Then 200000 - 197000 - 3000 equals 0, so there is no change; the batch is also a changeless transaction in the sense of the previous concepts. The amounts are invented for the illustration.\n\nThe recipients of a batch can see the other outputs and their amounts, because the transaction is public, so a payroll batch discloses the full list of salaries paid in one transaction to anyone who looks. A batch also depends on a single set of inputs and a single signature step, and a mistake in building it affects every recipient at once." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#PaymentBatching> .
chx:S_Unit a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Unit"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Unit" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 36 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Transaction ;
    skos:definition "A unit is a standard quantity in which value is counted, and a currency usually has several of them: one for everyday prices, smaller ones for small change, and sometimes a very small one in which the accounts are actually kept. Bitcoin's unit of account is the bitcoin, but the protocol itself counts in a far smaller unit, so that every amount in a transaction is a whole number and no rounding is ever needed. This group introduces that unit, the satoshi, together with the fractions of a bitcoin that are built on it, and it explains the convention by which the course writes amounts down (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA reader who does not know the units cannot read a transaction or a fee, because the same payment is described by three different numbers depending on the unit chosen. The prices in Bob's store are quoted in dollars and in bitcoin, while the figures in the diagram of the transaction chain are satoshis, because that is what the protocol itself uses (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A mistake of the unit is therefore not a small error but a factor of one hundred million, and the chapter on fees warns that confusing the denominator or the numerator of a fee rate can mean overpaying by a thousand or by a hundred million times (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Stating the unit beside every figure is the cheapest possible protection against that class of mistake.\n\nUnits are met wherever an amount is displayed. A wallet usually shows a balance in bitcoin with up to eight decimal places, and many wallets offer a setting that switches the whole display to satoshis instead. A fee is almost always quoted as a rate in satoshis per virtual byte, which mixes the smallest unit of value with a unit of size. A block explorer shows both forms side by side for the same transaction. And the figures of this chapter, 100,000 satoshis in and 75,000 and 20,000 out, are the protocol's own unit, while the invoice that started the payment asks for 0.01577764 bitcoin (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe group has a single concept, the satoshi, because every other unit is a plain multiple of it. The relation to the bitcoin is a fixed number and the division is exact: a bitcoin is exactly 100,000,000 satoshis, so a whole number of satoshis can describe any amount that can be sent, and the total supply of 21 million bitcoins is 2.1 quadrillion satoshis (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The intermediate names follow the same arithmetic, so a millibitcoin is a thousandth of a bitcoin and therefore 100,000 satoshis, the figure the chapter gives for the output that Joe's transaction created for Alice (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Because the protocol stores amounts as integers of satoshis, the sums in a transaction never carry a rounding error.\n\nDo not mix units in a calculation, and do not trust a number that arrives without one. Many defects and many costly payments have come from treating a figure in one unit as a figure in another, which is why this course states the unit next to every number it writes down. Two further cautions belong here. The names of the fractions are conventions and not protocol rules, so a wallet may offer units that another wallet has never heard of; and converting a decimal amount in bitcoin into satoshis with ordinary floating-point arithmetic can introduce a small error, which is one reason the protocol counts in whole satoshis in the first place." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Unit> .
chx:S_Satoshi a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Satoshi"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Satoshi" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 37 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Unit ;
    skos:definition "The satoshi is the smallest unit of bitcoin that can be recorded in a transaction: one hundred-millionth of a bitcoin, or 0.00000001. The book says the Bitcoin network can transact in fractional values, from the millibitcoin, one thousandth of a bitcoin, down to 1/100,000,000th of a bitcoin, which is known as a satoshi (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In a serialized transaction the amount of an output is stored as a whole number of satoshis.\n\nThe name has a short history. When Bitcoin was first created, the smallest unit had no name and some developers called it the base unit; later many users began calling it a satoshi in honor of Bitcoin's creator, who published the whitepaper under the name Satoshi Nakamoto (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The practical importance is that integer arithmetic is exact: the protocol counts whole satoshis and never needs fractions, so there are no rounding errors in the totals of a transaction.\n\nSatoshis are met in the figures of the chapter, where Tx1 sends 100,000 satoshis, which are 0.001 bitcoin, and Alice's payments are 75,000 and 20,000 satoshis (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). They are also the unit of the fee rate, satoshis per virtual byte. The consensus rules cap the total at 21 million bitcoins, which is 2.1 quadrillion satoshis, and Bitcoin Core defines one bitcoin as COIN = 100,000,000 and the maximum amount as 21,000,000 times that (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe conversions are plain division and multiplication. A millibitcoin is 10**8 // 1000 satoshis, which is 100000, exactly the 100,000 satoshis, or 0.001 bitcoin, that the book gives for the output of Tx1. A bitcoin amount from a payment request, such as 0.01577764, converts to 1,577,764 satoshis when it is multiplied by 10**8 with exact decimal arithmetic. Doing the conversion with ordinary floating point numbers invites small errors, which is one reason why the protocol itself counts in whole satoshis.\n\nThe book states a convention that students often overlook: it uses the same pluralization rules as for dollars, so the text speaks of 10 bitcoins and 0.001 bitcoins, and of millibitcoins and satoshis in the same way (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Another caution concerns display: wallets may show amounts in bitcoin or in satoshis, and a user who reads 100000 where 0.001 was expected has made a mistake of a factor of one hundred million in the other direction." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Satoshi> .
chx:S_PaymentRequest a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Payment request"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Payment request" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 38 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Transaction ;
    skos:definition "A payment request is the message through which a seller tells a buyer what to pay, to whom and why. In the chapter the request is created automatically by Bob's e-commerce system as a QR code that holds an invoice, and Alice's wallet reads it so that she never has to type an address or an amount. This group explains the pieces: the address-bearing web link called a URI, the QR code that carries it, the improvement proposal that defines its format, and the invoice itself (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nTyping a long address by hand is slow and error-prone, and an address typed wrongly can lose the payment. A payment request puts the address, the amount and a description into one object that a wallet can read in one step. The book notes that, unlike a QR code that simply contains a destination address, the invoice is a QR-encoded URI that contains a destination address, a payment amount and a description, so that the wallet application can prefill the information and show a human-readable description to the user (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe request is met in every online and shop payment with Bitcoin: on the checkout page, in an invoice, on a receipt. In the chapter, Alice scans the code on display with her smartphone, sees a payment for the correct amount to Bob's Store, and selects Send to authorize it; within a few seconds Bob sees the transaction on the register (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The course notes of this chapter, written for the second edition, show the same flow with an older style of address (Altunel, 2021).\n\nThe group begins with the URI, the general kind of web address that the request uses. The QR code is then described as the carrier. The improvement proposal is explained next, since the format is defined in one; and finally the invoice of the chapter, the Bitcoin URI of BIP 21, is dissected piece by piece and executed.\n\nThe request itself is not a transaction. Nothing is sent to the network when a code is displayed or scanned, and the buyer can decline. The format is also only a convenience: a wallet must still ask the user to approve the payment, and the standard itself says that clients must not act on such a URI without getting the user's authorization (Schneider and Corallo, 2012)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#PaymentRequest> .
chx:S_Uri a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Uniform resource identifier"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Uniform resource identifier" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 39 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PaymentRequest ;
    skos:definition "A uniform resource identifier, URI, is a string of characters that names or locates something according to a general format. It begins with a scheme, such as http or bitcoin, followed by a colon, and then a path and optionally a query that carries additional parameters after a question mark, separated by ampersands. The standard BIP 21 says that Bitcoin URIs follow the general format for URIs as set forth in RFC 3986, that the path component consists of a bitcoin address, and that the query component provides additional payment options (Schneider and Corallo, 2012).\n\nA common format lets different programs cooperate without knowing about each other. A web page or a QR code can carry a link, the operating system can recognize the scheme, and the wallet that registered itself for it can open it. The standard says the purpose of the scheme is to enable users to easily make payments by simply clicking links on webpages or scanning QR codes, and recommends that graphical bitcoin clients register themselves as the handler for the bitcoin: scheme (Schneider and Corallo, 2012).\n\nEveryone who uses the web has seen URIs: web addresses are URIs with the scheme http or https. The glossary of the book says, in its entry for the improvement proposal BIP21, that it proposes an improvement of the bitcoin uniform resource identifier scheme (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the chapter the URI is the content of Bob's QR code.\n\nPython's standard library splits a URI into its parts. Applying urlsplit to the start of Bob's URI returns the scheme bitcoin, the path bc1qk2g6u8p4qm2s2lh3gts5cpt2mrv5skcuu7u3e4, which is the address, and the query amount=0.01577764. Characters outside the allowed range in the query are first encoded in UTF-8 and then each byte is written as a percent sign and two hexadecimal digits, so that an apostrophe becomes %27 and a space %20 (Schneider and Corallo, 2012).\n\nA URI is text, and text can be forged. A link on a web page can show one thing and contain another, and a malicious page can offer an address that is not the seller's. That is the reason for the rule that a wallet may not act on a URI without the user's authorization, and why the user should compare the address and amount shown by the wallet with what was expected (Schneider and Corallo, 2012)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Uri> .
chx:S_QrCode a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "QR code"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "QR code" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 40 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PaymentRequest ;
    skos:definition "A QR code is a square pattern of black and white dots that serves as a form of barcode: a camera reads it and recovers the text it stores. The book's first chapter describes the code shown on the receive screen of Alice's wallet in this way (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In Bitcoin, QR codes carry addresses and payment requests from one device to another without any typing.\n\nThe code removes the need to type or to copy a long, easily mistyped string, and works between two devices that are not connected to each other at all, just a screen and a camera. For merchants it is the cheapest possible point of sale: a display and a printed or on-screen code. The chapter relies on it when Bob's system creates a QR code that contains an invoice, which Alice scans with her smartphone (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nWallets display codes to receive and scan codes to pay. Hardware signing devices, according to the book, may also connect to a wallet with a camera and QR codes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The size of a code depends on how much it holds and in what mode: the book explains that the mixed-case base58check alphabet requires extra space to encode in QR codes, whereas a bech32 address can be written in capital letters before it is encoded, which allows a special QR encoding mode that uses less space (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); BIP 173 also lists, among its reasons for a new address format, that Base58 needs a lot of space in QR codes, as it cannot use the alphanumeric mode (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nWhat a QR code stores is a string of characters, and the same text can be packed more or less densely. BIP 173 says that inside QR codes an address in uppercase should be used, as that permits the alphanumeric mode, which is 45 percent more compact than the normal byte mode, and that decoders must not accept strings that mix upper and lower case (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). Bob's address has 42 characters; written in capitals it still consists only of letters and digits, which the short program above confirms. The packing itself is done by the encoder in the wallet or in the shop's software, and a student can treat it as a black box.\n\nA QR code is not secure in itself. What is displayed is whatever someone chose to print, and a printed code that is covered by another one would replace the address without any visible sign. Check the address and the amount that your wallet decodes before you approve the payment, and the book's own tip about the chapter's code is to try to scan it with your wallet to see the address and amount, but not to send money (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#QrCode> .
chx:S_Bip a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Bitcoin improvement proposal"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Bitcoin improvement proposal" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 41 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PaymentRequest ;
    skos:definition "A Bitcoin improvement proposal, abbreviated BIP, is a design document that provides information to the Bitcoin community or describes a new feature for Bitcoin or its processes or environment. The first of them, BIP 1, says that a BIP should provide a concise technical specification of the feature and a rationale for it, and that BIPs are the primary mechanism for proposing new features, collecting community input on an issue and documenting design decisions (Taaki, 2011). The book's appendix says there are three kinds: standard, informational and process BIPs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nBitcoin has no company that decides how it changes. The BIP process gives the community a public and traceable way to propose changes and record the reasoning. BIP 1 states that the author of a BIP is responsible for building consensus within the community and documenting dissenting opinions, and that because BIPs are maintained as text files in a versioned repository, their revision history is the historical record of the feature proposal (Taaki, 2011). A BIP is not law: a standards track BIP matters only if the software and its users adopt it.\n\nThe chapter meets a BIP when it states that the invoice URI is defined in BIP 21 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's other chapters refer to many more, among them BIP 173 for the bech32 address format, which Bob's address in the invoice uses. The glossary of the book defines a BIP in the same sense, giving BIP 21 as its example (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nEach BIP begins with a header that gives its number, title, authors, status and type. BIP 21 has the title URI Scheme, the layer Applications and the type Specification, and its status is now Closed because it has been superseded; BIP 321, assigned in November 2024 and with the status Complete, replaces it. The little program above reads the status from such a header (Schneider and Corallo, 2012) (Corallo, 2024). The documents are plain text, so anyone can read them and quote them, which is how this chapter checks its statements.\n\nA proposal is not a decision. Many BIPs are never adopted, and a status such as proposed or draft says nothing about use. Status also changes: the BIP that the book cites for the invoice format, number 21, is no longer the current document, because it was replaced by a newer one that adds modern address types, and the book's reference is therefore a snapshot of 2023 (Corallo, 2024). Always check the status of the document you cite." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Bip> .
chx:S_Bip21Uri a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "BIP21 URI"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "BIP21 URI" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 42 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PaymentRequest ;
    skos:definition "A BIP21 URI is a Bitcoin payment request written as a web link in the format defined by BIP 21. It begins with the scheme bitcoin: and the address, optionally followed by a question mark and parameters such as the amount, a label for the recipient and a message that describes the payment. The chapter shows Bob's invoice in this form and explains its components: a Bitcoin address, the payment amount 0.01577764, a label for the recipient address, Bob's Store, and a description, Purchase at Bob's Store (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe format lets a wallet fill in a payment from a single scan and show the payer what is being paid for. The standard says the amount, if given, must be written in decimal bitcoin, with a period as the decimal separator, that the label and message are texts, and that clients must not act on such a URI without getting the user's authorization (Schneider and Corallo, 2012). The separation of address from description also keeps personal information out of the payment: the standard's rationale is that a URI should not represent an exchange of personal information, but a one-time payment (Schneider and Corallo, 2012).\n\nBob's invoice is the example in the chapter, displayed as a QR code on his checkout page (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Wallets register themselves for the bitcoin: scheme so that a link on a page opens the payment. BIP 21 was assigned in January 2012 and has the authors Nils Schneider and Matt Corallo; it is now superseded by BIP 321, which names bech32 and bech32m addresses explicitly and adds parameters for other payment methods, whereas the grammar of the older text names only base58 addresses (Schneider and Corallo, 2012) (Corallo, 2024).\n\nThe URI can be taken apart with the standard library. Splitting off the query and parsing it, then percent-decoding the label, returns Bob's Store, since %27 is the apostrophe and %20 a space. The amount 0.01577764 is a decimal number of bitcoin, which as exact decimal arithmetic equals 1,577,764 satoshis. The address, bc1qk2g6u8..., passes the bech32 checksum test of BIP 173, which the concept of the checksum explains.\n\nThe invoice in the book is an illustration, not a record of the real payment. The amount 0.01577764 bitcoin is 1,577,764 satoshis, while the real transaction pays 75,000 satoshis to Bob's store, and the 22-byte output script of the real payment holds a different 20-byte program than the one encoded in the invoice's address. Do not conclude from the book's figures that the address in the URI received the payment. The book's own tip says to try scanning the code, but do not send money (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Bip21Uri> .
chx:S_Address a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Bitcoin address"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Bitcoin address" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 43 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PaymentRequest ;
    skos:definition "A Bitcoin address is a short string of letters and digits that stands for the condition under which a payment can later be spent. The book's glossary describes an address as a string of letters and numbers that is really an encoded form of a hash related to a public key, and says that you ask others to send you bitcoin to one of your addresses much as you ask them to send email to your email address (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The address in this chapter, bc1qk2g6u8...uu7u3e4, is written in the newer format defined by BIP 173, which the book calls bech32 (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017).\n\nAn address exists for the payer's convenience and safety. A payer does not need to know anything about scripts, keys or hashes; the wallet turns the address into the output script that encumbers the payment, and the checksum inside the address catches a mistyped character before any money moves. The book makes the consequence explicit: the checksum prevents a mistyped address from being accepted by the wallet software as a valid destination, an error that would otherwise result in loss of funds (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe address is the first thing a reader meets in the chapter, as the path component of the invoice's payment link, and the chapter lists it among the components of that link together with the amount, the label and the description (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Block explorers accept an address as a search term, the chapter says, along with a transaction hash, a block number or a block hash. Addresses also appear on receipts, on printed codes in shops and in the course notes for this chapter, where the older style beginning with a 1 is used (Altunel, 2021).\n\nThe newer format is readable in three parts. A human-readable prefix, here bc for the main Bitcoin network, is separated by the digit 1 from the data, which encodes a version number and a program of twenty bytes, and ends with six characters of checksum (Wuille and Maxwell, 2017). The program in Bob's address is b291ae1c...9485b1c, and the checksum calculation over the whole string returns the value that BIP 173 requires for a valid address. The small example above reads off the three obvious features: the prefix bc1, the length of 42 characters, and the fact that the address is written entirely in lower case, which the format recommends.\n\nAn address is not an account and not an identity. It holds no balance of its own, since a balance is the sum of the unspent outputs that a wallet can spend, and it names no person, which is why following a chain of transactions does not by itself reveal who the parties were. Addresses are also meant to be used once: the book explains that a change address is usually a new address of the sender's wallet for privacy reasons (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Finally, the address in the book's invoice is an illustration: a check of the real transaction shows that its payment output encodes a different twenty-byte program, so the printed address did not receive the printed payment." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Address> .
chx:S_ExchangeRate a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Exchange rate"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Exchange rate" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 44 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PaymentRequest ;
    skos:definition "An exchange rate is the price of one unit of a currency expressed in another currency. In this chapter it is the number that turns a price in United States dollars into an amount of bitcoin. The chapter says that the prices at Bob's store are listed in the local currency, US dollars, but that at checkout customers have the option of paying in either dollars or bitcoin, and that the checkout cart displays the price in dollars and also in bitcoin at Bitcoin's prevailing exchange rate (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe rate is where Bitcoin meets the ordinary economy, and it explains a figure that otherwise looks arbitrary. The amount in the invoice, 0.01577764 bitcoin, is not a round number because it is the dollar price of the podcast episode converted at the rate of that moment. It also explains why a merchant who quotes in a local currency carries no currency risk on the listed price while a merchant who quotes in bitcoin does. The chapter earlier notes that people accept a money only if they expect to be able to spend it later, which is a statement about value over time (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nYou meet the rate on every checkout page that offers bitcoin, in every wallet that shows a fiat equivalent beside an amount, and in the figures of this chapter. The book's first chapter describes the same step from the other side, when Alice buys bitcoins from Joe for cash at an agreed price (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Exchange rates are also what make the chapter's dollar comparisons meaningful, such as the coffee-shop analogy in which card payments below twenty-five dollars are accepted without a signature (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe arithmetic is a division. If a podcast episode costs twenty-five dollars and one bitcoin is worth 1,584 dollars at that moment, the price in bitcoin is 25 divided by 1584, which rounds to 0.01578283 bitcoin, a figure of the same shape and size as the invoice's amount. The dollar price and the rate here are an illustration chosen to show the calculation, not figures from the book; what the book gives is the converted amount. Converting the other way, an amount in bitcoin multiplied by the rate gives a price in dollars, and the same amount in satoshis is the bitcoin amount multiplied by one hundred million.\n\nA rate is a market price at an instant, and it is the one number in a payment request that goes stale. An invoice that was correct when it was displayed may be worth more or less a few minutes later, which is why merchants normally let a quoted bitcoin amount expire. The rate is also not part of Bitcoin: no rule of the protocol knows about dollars, and the figures printed in the book are a snapshot of 2023. Treat a rate as context for reading the chapter's amounts, never as a constant." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#ExchangeRate> .
chx:S_Construction a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Construction"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Construction" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 45 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Transaction ;
    skos:definition "Construction is the work of turning a decision to pay into a valid transaction. In the chapter this is done entirely by Alice's wallet application, which contains all the logic for selecting inputs and generating outputs to build a transaction to Alice's specification (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This group describes what the wallet does, in the order in which it does it: find inputs that can pay, create outputs, add a fee and sign.\n\nConstruction is where the abstract parts of the first groups come together. A user sees only a destination, an amount and a fee; everything else, from the choice of inputs to the change, is a decision of the software. Understanding it explains why two wallets can build different transactions for the same payment, and why a wallet bug can lose money.\n\nEvery wallet does this, for every payment. The book points out that a wallet can construct a transaction even if it is completely offline, if it already knows which inputs it controls, just as one can write a check at home and later send it to the bank in an envelope (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The group contains one concept, the construction of a transaction.\n\nThe wallet starts from the outputs it controls, which it tracks. If it runs on a full node, it contains a copy of every confirmed transaction's unspent outputs; many user wallets run as lightweight clients that track only their own (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). From these it chooses inputs, creates an output for the receiver, adds a change output when the inputs exceed the payment, leaves a fee, and signs.\n\nThe wallet is trusted with decisions the user never sees, so the user must trust its code. That is why the book recommends a design in which wallets are open and reviewed, and why this course returns to wallets in a later chapter. The wallet cannot be trusted to know what has happened on the network if it is offline, either: a transaction built offline is only a candidate until it is sent." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Construction> .
chx:S_TransactionConstruction a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction construction"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transaction construction" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 46 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Construction ;
    skos:definition "Constructing a transaction means assembling its inputs and outputs and signing it. The book describes the user's part as small: Alice only needs to choose a destination, an amount and a transaction fee, and the rest happens in the wallet application without her seeing the details (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The wallet finds inputs that can pay the amount, creates the outputs and attaches the signature that proves she may spend the inputs.\n\nA transaction that has been constructed contains, in the words of the book, everything necessary to confirm ownership of the funds and assign new owners, so it can be sent to the network by any route (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This is a major reason why Bitcoin needs no trusted intermediary: the proof of authorization travels inside the transaction, and nodes do not need to know who built it or how it reached them.\n\nIn the chapter the steps are described in sections of their own. The first is getting the right inputs: the wallet keeps track of the available outputs belonging to the addresses in the wallet, which here includes the output from Joe's transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Next it creates the outputs. The output for Bob is created with a script that says, in effect, that it is paid to whoever can present a signature from the key corresponding to Bob's public address; the book calls this encumbering the output with a demand for a signature from Bob (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The second output returns the change, and the fee is not stated: it is implied by the difference between inputs and outputs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe sums can be rehearsed. With an input of 100000 satoshis, a payment of 75000 and a fee of 5000, the change is 100000 - 75000 - 5000, which is 20000, and the two output amounts are 20000 and 75000, exactly the amounts of Alice's real transaction. The signing step cannot be shown with these numbers, because it requires the private key and a hash of parts of the transaction. The book notes that a transaction does not need to be constructed and signed while connected to the network (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nFour cautions follow. A wallet that has no knowledge of the current state of the network may build a transaction whose inputs have already been spent. A fee that is chosen too low may make the payment wait, and one that is chosen far too high is paid to the miner without any warning from the format. The signature covers the transaction as built, so a wallet cannot adjust the amounts after signing. And, the book adds, a wallet running on a lightweight client has less information than one running on a full node (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#TransactionConstruction> .
chx:S_Wallet a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Wallet"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Wallet" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 47 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Construction ;
    skos:definition "A wallet is the software through which a person uses Bitcoin. The book's glossary describes it as software that holds all your Bitcoin addresses and secret keys and that you use to send, receive and store your bitcoin (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In practice it does four things: it keeps the keys, it keeps track of which outputs those keys can spend, it builds and signs transactions, and it presents the result to the user as a balance and a history. In this chapter there are three of them, Alice's, Bob's and, in the figures of the chain, Joe's and Gopesh's.\n\nThe wallet is where all the complexity of the chapter is hidden from the user. The book says that Alice's wallet application contains all the logic for selecting inputs and generating outputs to build a transaction to her specification, and that she only needs to choose a destination, an amount and a transaction fee while the rest happens without her seeing the details (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Everything a reader has met so far, coin selection, change, fees, scripts and signatures, is a decision that this program makes, which is why trusting a wallet is as consequential as trusting a bank used to be.\n\nThe chapter meets wallets at every step: Alice's wallet reads the payment request and prefills the payment, builds the transaction and sends it; Bob's wallet recognises the incoming payment because it contains an output redeemable by Bob's keys, and can independently verify that the transaction is well formed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book distinguishes wallets that run on a full node, which hold a copy of every confirmed transaction's unspent outputs, from the many user wallets that run as lightweight clients and track only the user's own (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023); and it adds that a wallet can build a transaction while completely offline.\n\nThe arithmetic a wallet performs for a payment is the subtraction of this chapter. Given the outputs it controls, the amount to pay and the fee to leave, it works out the change: with one output of 100,000 satoshis, a payment of 75,000 and a fee of 5,000, the change is 20,000, which is what Alice's real transaction contains. Around that subtraction sit the steps described elsewhere in this branch: choosing which outputs to spend, writing an output script for the receiver, writing one for the change, and signing each input with the private key that the output being spent demands.\n\nA wallet does not contain bitcoins. It contains keys and a view of the ledger, which is why a backup of the keys restores the funds and a copy of the balance does not. Two further cautions belong here. A wallet that is offline cannot know whether an output it believes it controls has already been spent, so a transaction built offline is only a candidate until the network accepts it. And the user cannot see what the wallet decided, so a defect in the software can overpay a fee or leak a change address without any warning; the book's chapter on fees asks wallets to make an excessive fee rate hard to pay by accident (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Wallet> .
chx:S_Network a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Network"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Network" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 48 ;
    skos:definition "The Bitcoin network is the set of computers that run the Bitcoin protocol and exchange transactions and blocks with one another. This second branch of the chapter follows Alice's transaction out of her wallet and across that network. The chapter describes the network in two sentences that the whole branch unfolds: it is a peer-to-peer network in which each peer participates by connecting to several other peers, and its purpose is to propagate transactions and blocks to all participants (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe branch matters because the network is what makes the system work without a company in the middle. There is no server that collects payments and no directory that decides who may take part, so a transaction reaches the miners only because independent computers pass it on, and each of them checks it first. That is also what makes the network robust: the chapter notes that it does not matter how or where a transaction is transmitted, since the transaction contains everything necessary for it to be processed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Any route will do, and no single point can block a payment.\n\nA reader meets the network every time a wallet reports that a payment has been sent and a shop reports a few seconds later that it has arrived. The chapter's own account is exactly this: Alice's wallet can send the transaction to any node over any kind of connection, wired, wireless or mobile, or even to another program such as a block explorer that will relay it to a node, and within a few seconds Bob sees it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's chapter on the network adds the scale: about ten thousand listening nodes running various versions of Bitcoin Core and a few hundred running other implementations (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe branch has two groups. The first asks who takes part: the full node that verifies everything, the lightweight client that verifies a little, the peer that is simply another node one is connected to, and the peer-to-peer architecture that these form. The second follows the journey: the transmission of the transaction from the wallet, the gossiping by which each node forwards what it has accepted, the propagation that results, the memory pool in which an unconfirmed transaction waits, the recipient's own check, and the block explorer through which a reader can watch all of it from outside.\n\nThree cautions apply throughout the branch. Reaching the network is not the same as being recorded: the chapter is careful to say that a transaction does not become part of the blockchain until it is included in a block, which belongs to the next branch (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Being received by many nodes is not a vote either, since each node decides for itself by the rules. And a peer is not a trusted party: everything that arrives from one is checked, which is exactly why a node can safely connect to strangers." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Network> .
chx:S_Participants a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Who takes part"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Who takes part" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 49 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Network ;
    skos:definition "This group names the kinds of program that make up the network and says what each of them can and cannot check. The chapter introduces them in one passage: peers in the Bitcoin peer-to-peer network are programs that have both the software logic and the data necessary to fully verify the correctness of a new transaction, that the connections between peers are often drawn as the edges of a graph whose nodes are the peers themselves, and that for that reason Bitcoin peers are commonly called full verification nodes, or full nodes for short (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe distinctions in this group decide whom a user has to trust. A program that verifies every rule for itself needs to trust nobody; a program that asks others for answers has to trust what it is told, at least in part. The chapter uses exactly this difference when it describes Bob's situation: his wallet can verify that Alice's transaction is well formed, but only if Bob is using his own full node can his wallet further verify that the transaction spends valid unspent outputs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The same difference runs through the rest of the course.\n\nThe group has four concepts, from the most capable to the most abstract. The full node verifies every rule and keeps the data needed to do so. The lightweight client keeps far less and verifies far less, which is what makes a wallet possible on a telephone. The peer is simply another node that a node has a connection to, and the connection is the unit of the network's structure. The peer-to-peer architecture is the shape that results when every participant is of the same kind and no participant is special.\n\nResist two tidy pictures. The first is that these kinds are sharp: the book's network chapter explains that a full node may also carry routing, mining and wallet functions, and that some nodes keep a complete archival copy while others do not, so the roles overlap (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The second is that more participants automatically means more security; what matters is how many independently verify the rules, which is why this group separates the counting of nodes from the question of what each of them actually checks." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Participants> .
chx:S_FullNode a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Full node"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Full node" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 50 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Participants ;
    skos:definition "A full node, which the chapter calls a full verification node, is a program that has both the software logic and the data necessary to fully verify the correctness of a new transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's network chapter states the same thing about blocks: full nodes verify every transaction in every block on the valid blockchain with the most proof of work, process every block starting after the genesis block, and can therefore verify any transaction independently and authoritatively (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The word node comes from the picture of the network as a graph whose dots are the programs and whose lines are their connections.\n\nThe full node is the reason the chapter can open by saying that the Bitcoin system does not require trust in third parties, and that each user can run software on their own computer to verify the correct operation of every aspect of the system (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A user with a full node accepts a payment because their own program checked it; a user without one accepts it because somebody else said so. The book adds that running a full node gives the pure Bitcoin experience, independent verification of all transactions without the need to rely on, or trust, any other system (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe chapter meets a full node three times: when Alice's wallet hands the transaction to any node it can reach, when every node that receives it forwards it only if it is valid, and when Bob's wallet, if it runs on his own full node, checks that the transaction spends valid unspent outputs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's network chapter says that more than ninety-five per cent of full nodes run some version of Bitcoin Core and that it identifies itself as Satoshi in the version string it sends to its peers, for example as /Satoshi:24.0.1/ (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nVerification is the running of a checklist. The book's mining chapter prints the list that a node applies to every transaction it receives, and its items are the ones this chapter has prepared: the syntax and data structure must be correct, neither the list of inputs nor the list of outputs may be empty, the weight must be small enough to fit in a block, each output value and the total must be within the allowed range, the outputs being spent must match outputs in the memory pool or unspent outputs in a block of the best chain, the sum of the inputs may not be less than the sum of the outputs, and the scripts of each input must validate against the corresponding output scripts (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The small example stands for that checklist: a transaction is accepted when every item passes, and the count of items is what was checked.\n\nA full node is not a miner and not a wallet, although one program may do all three. It also does not make a user anonymous or protect their keys, and it does not remove every trust: a user still trusts the software they downloaded, which is why the next chapter of the course is about verifying a release of Bitcoin Core. Finally, a node's view can be briefly out of date, since it learns of a new block only when a peer sends it; what it never does is accept a block or transaction that breaks a rule it enforces." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#FullNode> .
chx:S_LightweightClient a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Lightweight client"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Lightweight client" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 51 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Participants ;
    skos:definition "A lightweight client is a Bitcoin program that does not verify everything. The chapter introduces the kind in a single clause, saying that because full nodes use more resources, many user wallets run lightweight clients that track only the user's own unspent outputs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's network chapter gives the method a name: such clients download only the block headers, not the transactions in each block, and verify payments by a procedure called simplified payment verification (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nLightweight clients exist because verification costs resources that a telephone does not have. The book says they are designed to run on space- and power-constrained devices such as smartphones, tablets and embedded systems (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The trade is explicit: the client gets a usable wallet in a pocket and gives up the ability to check everything for itself, relying instead on peers to provide partial views of the relevant parts of the blockchain on demand (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Knowing which of the two kinds a wallet is tells a user exactly how much of the chapter's guarantee applies to them.\n\nThis chapter meets the kind twice. When Alice's wallet gets the right inputs, the book contrasts a wallet running on a full node, which contains a copy of every confirmed transaction's unspent outputs, with the many user wallets that run as lightweight clients (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). And at the end of the chapter, lightweight clients are said to partially verify payments by confirming that the transaction is in the blockchain and has several blocks mined after it, which gives assurance that miners expended significant effort committing to it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe saving comes from the size of a header. A block header is eighty bytes whatever the block contains, while a full block can hold about four million bytes of transactions, so the chain of headers alone is roughly ten thousand times smaller than the full chain, which is the factor the book gives and which the division above reproduces (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). To check that a particular transaction is in a block, the client asks a peer for a short path of hashes through the block's merkle tree and recomputes the root, as the concepts of the merkle tree and of simplified payment verification explain.\n\nA lightweight client cannot tell by itself whether a transaction obeys the rules; it can only see that it was committed to by work. The book's comparison is a tourist with a street map against a tourist asking strangers for directions (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). There is a privacy cost as well, since asking peers which transactions concern you tells them which transactions concern you, and the book devotes a section to that problem (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter's warning about block explorers is the same point in another form: looking something up may tell the operator what you are interested in (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#LightweightClient> .
chx:S_Peer a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Peer and connection"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Peer and connection" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 52 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Participants ;
    skos:definition "A peer is another participant of the network with which a node maintains a connection in order to exchange transactions and blocks. The chapter uses the word in its description of the architecture: the Bitcoin network is a peer-to-peer network, with each peer participating by connecting to several other peers (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's network chapter adds that the full nodes that take part are peers to each other, that they can all perform the same functions and that there are no special nodes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nPeers are how a node learns anything at all. There is no server to ask, so every unconfirmed transaction and every new block reaches a node from a peer, and everything the node has accepted is passed on to its peers in turn. The word also carries a claim about equality: because the participants are of the same kind, no one of them can be removed to stop the network, and the chapter can say that it does not matter how or where a transaction is transmitted (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). For an operator, each connection is also a small cost in memory and bandwidth.\n\nThe chapter meets peers when Alice's wallet sends the transaction to any node it can reach and when that node forwards it to all the other nodes to which it is connected (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's network chapter describes the first thing two peers do, exchanging version messages that state how many blocks each has, and then headers and blocks until the shorter chain has caught up (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A reader who runs a node sees its peers listed with the software each is running.\n\nA connection is a link between two nodes over which messages travel, and a node has several at once; the small example counts a set of three and names the first. The structure matters more than the number: the book describes the nodes as interconnecting in a mesh with a flat topology, with no server, no centralized service and no hierarchy, and says that nodes in such a network both provide and consume services at the same time (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A node finds candidates to connect to from addresses it has learned before, a process the book's network chapter calls network discovery.\n\nA peer is not a trusted party, and this is the single most important thing to remember about the word. Everything a peer sends is checked against the rules by the receiving node, which is why connecting to unknown computers is safe for the data. The number of peers is also not a measure of correctness, and a node that is connected to many peers still accepts only what passes its own checks. Finally, peers see what you send them: the book's chapter on lightweight clients and privacy explains that what a program asks its peers for can reveal what it is interested in (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Peer> .
chx:S_PeerToPeerNetwork a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Peer-to-peer network"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Peer-to-peer network" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 53 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Participants ;
    skos:definition "A peer-to-peer network, often abbreviated to P2P, is a network in which the participants are equals: each of them performs the same functions, each both provides and consumes services, and none of them is special. The book states this of Bitcoin directly, adding that the nodes interconnect in a mesh network with a flat topology, that there is no server, no centralized service and no hierarchy within the network, and that such networks are inherently resilient, decentralized and open (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nFor Bitcoin the architecture is not a technical preference but a consequence of the goal. The book writes that Bitcoin is a peer-to-peer digital cash system by design, that the network architecture is both a reflection and a foundation of that characteristic, and that decentralization of control can only be achieved and maintained by a flat and decentralized consensus network (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Nakamoto's paper is titled for the same idea and proposes a solution using a peer-to-peer network in which the participants timestamp transactions by hashing them into an ongoing chain of proof of work (Nakamoto, 2008). If there were a server, whoever ran it could decide what counts as a payment.\n\nThe shape is met in the chapter whenever a transaction travels: Alice's wallet does not have to be connected to Bob's wallet, nor to use the internet connection that Bob offers, though both of those are possible too (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Beyond Bitcoin, the book names the early internet as a preeminent example of the architecture, where the nodes of the network were equal, and file sharing as its largest and most successful application, with Napster as the pioneer and BitTorrent as the most recent evolution (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nWhat makes the structure work is that every node keeps several connections, so that the whole is connected without any central point. The number of links grows quickly with the number of participants: a group of five nodes in which everyone is connected to everyone has five times four divided by two, that is ten links, as the calculation above shows. A real network does not connect everyone to everyone, because a path of a few hops is enough: the chapter says that a transaction rapidly propagates out across the network, reaching a large percentage of the nodes within a few seconds (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nPeer-to-peer does not mean that every participant does the same amount of work, nor that there are no services built on top. The book notes that beside the base protocol there are others used for mining and for lightweight wallets, provided by gateway servers that bridge into the peer-to-peer network, and that various companies run nodes as network edge routers on which exchanges, wallets, explorers and payment processors are built (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Those services are centralized even though the protocol beneath them is not, and a user who relies on one of them is relying on its operator." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#PeerToPeerNetwork> .
chx:S_Propagation a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Propagation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Propagation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 54 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Network ;
    skos:definition "Propagation is the spreading of a transaction or a block from the computer that first has it to the rest of the network. This group follows Alice's payment through that spreading, from the moment her wallet transmits it to the moment a reader can look it up from outside. The chapter's three short sections on transmitting the transaction, how it propagates and Bob's view are the backbone of the group, and the memory pool and the block explorer are added so that the whole journey can be described in the words the later chapters use (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe group explains why a payment can be trusted within seconds even though it is not yet recorded. Each node that forwards the transaction has first verified it, so propagation is not mere copying: it is a wave of independent checks. That is also the reason the chapter can say that invalid data does not travel far, and the reason a merchant may make a judgement about an unconfirmed payment, as the security group of the next branch discusses.\n\nA reader meets propagation as the few seconds between pressing Send and seeing the payment appear in the receiver's wallet. The chapter states the figure twice: within a few seconds, about the same amount of time as a credit card authorization, Bob sees the transaction on the register, and even if the transaction goes through other nodes it will reach Bob's wallet within a few seconds (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The same event can be watched from a third position, in a block explorer, which is why the explorer belongs to this group.\n\nThe group is taken in the order of the journey. Transmission is the first hop out of the wallet. Gossiping is the rule by which each node passes on what it has accepted. Transaction propagation is the resulting spread through the network. The memory pool is where an accepted but unconfirmed transaction waits, on every node that holds it. The recipient's verification is Bob's own check. The block explorer, finally, is the program through which a person without a node can see the same data, at a price in privacy that the chapter warns about (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nPropagation proves nothing about permanence. A transaction that has reached every node is still unconfirmed, can still be replaced in some circumstances, and is recorded only when a miner includes it in a block that other nodes accept. Speed can also mislead: that a payment appeared in a wallet within two seconds says only that the data arrived, not that the money cannot be taken back, which is the subject of the confirmation concept in the next branch." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Propagation> .
chx:S_Transmission a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Transmitting the transaction"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transmitting the transaction" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 55 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Propagation ;
    skos:definition "Transmission is the step in which a finished, signed transaction leaves the wallet that built it and is handed to the network. The chapter gives it a section of its own and states the governing principle at once: because the transaction contains all the information necessary for it to be processed, it does not matter how or where it is transmitted to the Bitcoin network (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). From that moment the wallet's work is over and the network's work begins.\n\nThis indifference to the route is one of Bitcoin's quietly radical properties. A payment is not a request sent to an institution that may accept or refuse it; it is a self-contained document whose validity any recipient can check. The chapter draws out the practical consequences: Alice's wallet can send the transaction to any Bitcoin node over any type of connection, wired, wireless or mobile, and it can also send it to another program, such as a block explorer, that will relay it to a node (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Her wallet does not have to be connected to Bob's wallet directly, and she does not have to use the internet connection offered by Bob, though both of those are possible as well.\n\nThe reader meets transmission as the moment of pressing Send. In the chapter, Alice scans the code, sees a payment for the correct amount to Bob's Store, selects Send to authorize the payment, and within a few seconds Bob sees the transaction on the register (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The same step is why a transaction can be built on a device that has no network at all: the book compares it to writing a cheque at home and later sending it to the bank in an envelope, and says that a wallet that already knows what inputs it controls can construct transactions completely offline (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe mechanism is simply to give the bytes to something that is connected. The small example lists the routes the chapter names, sorted, to make the point that they are interchangeable: a wired link, wireless, mobile, or a program such as a block explorer that will relay the transaction onward. Whatever the route, the receiving node applies its checklist before doing anything else, and only then passes the transaction on, which is the rule that the gossiping concept describes.\n\nTwo things are not guaranteed by transmission. The first is arrival: a wallet that sends a transaction to a single node and gets no acknowledgement does not know that the network has it, which is why wallets usually send to several peers and watch for the transaction coming back to them. The second is acceptance: a node that finds the transaction invalid simply drops it, and the chapter's rule is that a node forwards a valid transaction it has not seen before (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Relaying through a third party such as an explorer also tells that party who is paying whom, which is the privacy cost the chapter warns of." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Transmission> .
chx:S_Gossiping a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Gossiping"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Gossiping" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 56 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Propagation ;
    skos:definition "Gossiping is the name the chapter gives to the way data spreads through the Bitcoin network. The rule is stated in one sentence: any Bitcoin node that receives a valid transaction it has not seen before will forward it to all other nodes to which it is connected, a propagation technique known as gossiping (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Every word of the rule carries weight: valid, so that bad data stops at the first node; not seen before, so that the same transaction is not passed round in circles; and all other nodes, so that no one chooses who hears about it.\n\nGossiping is how a network with no coordinator reaches everybody. Nobody holds a list of participants and nobody decides the route, yet the chapter can say that the transaction rapidly propagates out across the peer-to-peer network, reaching a large percentage of the nodes within a few seconds (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The validity condition is what makes this safe rather than reckless: the book's mining chapter explains that every node verifies a transaction before forwarding it, so that only valid transactions are propagated while invalid ones are discarded at the first node that encounters them (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader meets gossiping as the speed of a Bitcoin payment and, from the other side, as the reason a shop can see a payment that was made on the other side of the world. The course notes for this chapter describe the same mechanism under its other common name, flooding, and record the same observation, that a valid transaction is forwarded to the other connected nodes and reaches a large percentage of the network within a few seconds (Altunel, 2021). Blocks spread by the same rule, which is why a new block reaches every miner quickly enough for the competition to restart.\n\nThe reach grows multiplicatively. If each node forwards to eight peers, then after one round eight nodes have the transaction, after two rounds sixty-four and after three rounds eight to the third power, that is 512, as the calculation above shows; a handful of rounds is therefore enough for a network of thousands, and each round takes only as long as a message between two computers. The numbers are an idealization, since peers overlap and a node ignores what it already has, but the order of growth is why a few seconds suffice.\n\nGossiping does not guarantee that every node will hold a given transaction. A node may be offline, may have a full memory pool, or may apply its own relay policy, and the book's chapter on fees notes that nodes limit how many unconfirmed transactions they store (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Nor does gossiping record anything: the data is in memory, not in the blockchain. And because what spreads is what a node has accepted, a transaction that one node's policy rejects may travel less widely without being invalid, so wide propagation is evidence of acceptance rather than proof of it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Gossiping> .
chx:S_TransactionPropagation a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Transaction propagation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Transaction propagation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 57 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Propagation ;
    skos:definition "Transaction propagation is the spread of a signed transaction across the network until it has reached, in practice, everyone who cares, including the miners who may record it. It is the composition of the two previous concepts: the wallet transmits the transaction to some node, and then the gossip rule carries it onward hop by hop. The chapter's own summary is that the purpose of the Bitcoin network is to propagate transactions and blocks to all participants (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nPropagation is the step that turns a private document into public knowledge, and it is what makes the double-spend check meaningful. If every node sees the transaction that spends an output, a second transaction spending the same output will be rejected by the nodes that already hold the first, so a fraudulent payment has to beat the honest one through the network. Propagation is also what the receiver relies on in the first seconds: before any block exists, the only evidence Bob has is that his own program and everybody else's have seen the payment and found it valid.\n\nThe chapter tracks Alice's transaction through exactly this step and gives the timing twice: within a few seconds, about the same time as a credit card authorization, Bob sees it on the register, and even when the transaction goes through other nodes it reaches Bob's wallet within a few seconds (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Later the chapter says that the transaction was picked up by the network and included in the pool of unverified transactions, and that once validated by a full node it was included in a candidate block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A block explorer shows the same transaction as unconfirmed during that interval.\n\nThe rough arithmetic of the chapter's claim can be done. If a network of about ten thousand listening nodes is reached in about five seconds, that is on the order of two thousand nodes a second, as the division above gives (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Nothing coordinates this: each node, on receiving the transaction, applies the verification checklist, stores it in its memory pool and announces it to its own peers, who ask for it if they do not have it. The book's network chapter describes that announcement as the exchange of inventory, in which a node offers what it has and a peer requests what it lacks (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nPropagation is not confirmation, and this is the misconception the chapter is most careful about: a transaction does not become part of the blockchain until it is included in a block by mining and that block has been validated by full nodes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A widely propagated transaction can still be dropped if it is never mined, and in some circumstances it can be replaced by another version paying a higher fee, which the book's chapter on fees describes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). What propagation gives is visibility and a first, probabilistic assurance, nothing more." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#TransactionPropagation> .
chx:S_Mempool a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Memory pool"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Memory pool" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 58 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Propagation ;
    skos:definition "The memory pool, usually shortened to mempool, is the collection of transactions that a node has verified but that are not yet in any block. The book's network chapter says that almost every node on the network maintains such a temporary list of unconfirmed transactions, and uses it to keep track of transactions that are known to the network but not yet included in the blockchain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The mining chapter adds how the pool is filled: by independently verifying each transaction as it is received and before propagating it, every node builds a pool of valid but unconfirmed transactions known as the memory pool (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe pool is the waiting room of the system, and it is where the market for block space happens. A miner builds a candidate block out of the pool, and the chapter says that transactions are added to the new block prioritized by the highest fee rate first and a few other criteria (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), so the pool is what the fee rate concept is about. For a user, the pool explains the state a payment is in during the first minutes: verified by everyone, recorded by nobody.\n\nThe chapter meets the pool when it says that Alice's transaction was picked up by the network and included in the pool of unverified transactions, and that once validated by a full node it was included in a candidate block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's mining chapter describes the same pool from the miner's side: while searching for a solution, the mining node was also collecting transactions, and by the time the previous block arrived it had collected a few thousand in its memory pool; on receiving and validating that block it removes from the pool any transactions the block contained, and whatever remains is unconfirmed and waiting (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nMaintaining the pool is adding and removing. A verified transaction is added and announced to peers; a transaction that appears in a new block is removed, which the small example shows by filtering a pool of three against a block containing one of them and keeping the other two. Some implementations also keep a separate pool for orphaned transactions, whose inputs refer to a transaction the node does not yet know, and move them into the memory pool when the missing parent arrives, a cascade that the book describes as reuniting the orphans with their parents (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe memory pool is local, not global. The book is explicit that the memory pool and the orphan pool represent a single node's local perspective and might vary significantly from node to node depending on when the node was started or restarted, unlike the database of unspent outputs, which represents the emergent consensus of the network and will not usually vary (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). So there is no such thing as the mempool, only each node's own; a transaction in your node's pool may be missing from another's, and nothing in the pool is recorded until it is mined." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Mempool> .
chx:S_RecipientVerification a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "The recipient's own check"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "The recipient's own check" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 59 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Propagation ;
    skos:definition "The recipient's own check is what happens in Bob's wallet when Alice's transaction arrives. The chapter devotes a short section to it, called Bob's view, and makes three statements. Bob's wallet will immediately identify Alice's transaction as an incoming payment because it contains an output redeemable by Bob's keys; his wallet can also independently verify that the transaction is well formed; and if Bob is using his own full node, his wallet can further verify that Alice's transaction only spends valid unspent outputs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThis concept is where the chapter's opening promise becomes concrete for a particular person. Bob does not have to telephone a bank or trust Alice: his own program reads the transaction and recognises the payment. The three statements also form a ladder of assurance that the course returns to again and again. Recognising an output addressed to oneself requires nothing but one's own keys; checking that the data is well formed requires only the rules of the format; checking that the inputs exist and are unspent requires the whole ledger, which is what a full node has.\n\nIn the chapter, Bob's wallet may even be the first to receive the transaction if it is connected directly to Alice's, and otherwise receives it within a few seconds through other nodes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A shop meets the same step as the line on the register that says a payment has arrived. The book's chapter on fees adds the caution that belongs here, discussing merchants that accept unconfirmed transactions and what they should watch for (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nRecognition is a search through the outputs for one that the wallet's own keys can spend. The small example does exactly that on the two outputs of Alice's transaction, one belonging to Alice as change and one to Bob, and returns the index 1, which is Bob's payment and the output that the chapter's later figure shows Bob spending as Tx2:1. Checking well-formedness is applying the parts of the verification checklist that need no outside data, such as that the lists of inputs and outputs are not empty and that the amounts are in range; checking the inputs needs the set of unspent outputs, which only a full node keeps.\n\nWhat the recipient's check cannot establish is that the payment is final. The transaction is unconfirmed at this point, so the assurance is about validity, not permanence, and the chapter moves straight on to mining for that reason. A lightweight wallet is also limited in a way its user may not notice: it will show the incoming payment, but it has not verified that the inputs were unspent, so it is trusting its peers for that part. And recognising an output as one's own is not the same as being able to spend it yet, since an output in an unconfirmed transaction can only be spent further once there is something to build on." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#RecipientVerification> .
chx:S_BlockExplorer a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Block explorer"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Block explorer" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 60 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Propagation ;
    skos:definition "A block explorer is a web application that operates as a Bitcoin search engine: it allows you to search for addresses, transactions and blocks and to see the relationships and flows between them, in the chapter's own words (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter uses one throughout, saying that while tracking a transaction through the network to the blockchain it will use a blockchain explorer site to visualize each step, and it provides a link for each transaction and block example so that a reader can look it up and study it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe explorer is what makes this chapter checkable by a reader who has no node. All the data in the blockchain is public, but reading it from the network requires software and a copy of the chain; an explorer does that work and presents the result as a web page. For a course it is also the simplest instrument of the subject: a student can see a real input pointing at a real earlier output, and watch a transaction move from unconfirmed to one confirmation and then to more.\n\nThe chapter names three popular explorers, the Blockstream Explorer, Mempool.Space and the BlockCypher Explorer, and says that each has a search function that can take a Bitcoin address, a transaction hash, a block number or a block hash and retrieve the corresponding information from the network (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It then gives links to the payment made to Bob, to the transaction from Alice to Bob's Store and to the block that includes it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The owner's course notes for this chapter list the explorers of their time, among them blockchain.info and the BlockCypher and Insight explorers, which shows that the set of services changes while the data does not (Altunel, 2021).\n\nAn explorer works out from the shape of the query what is being asked for, and the small example shows the idea: a query of sixty-four hexadecimal characters is a transaction or block hash, a string beginning with bc1 is an address of the newer format, and a plain number is a block height. It then looks the item up in its own index of the chain and renders a page, following the links that this chapter has explained, from an input to the output it spends and from a block to its parent. The explorer can also relay a transaction, which is why the chapter lists it among the ways Alice's wallet can reach the network (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe chapter attaches a warning to the explorer and so should the reader. Searching information on a block explorer may disclose to its operator that you are interested in that information, allowing them to associate it with your address on the internet, your browser details, your past searches or other identifiable information; looking up the transactions in the book is harmless, but looking up your own may let the operator guess how many bitcoins you have received, spent and currently own (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). An explorer is also a third party: what it shows has not been verified by you, which is exactly the trust that running a full node removes." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#BlockExplorer> .
chx:S_Blockchain a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Blockchain"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Blockchain" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 61 ;
    skos:definition "The blockchain is the record in which Bitcoin's transactions are finally written down. The chapter calls it the distributed journal of all transactions and, in its opening overview, the consensus blockchain which is the authoritative journal of all transactions (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's own chapter on the subject is more precise about the shape of the thing: the blockchain is the history of every confirmed transaction, and its data structure is an ordered, back-linked list of blocks of transactions, each block referring to the previous one (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This third branch of the chapter is about how a transaction gets into that record and why, once it is there, it becomes progressively harder to remove.\n\nThe branch answers the question that the first two leave open. A transaction that every node has seen and found valid is still only a proposal: nothing yet says that this payment, and not a competing one spending the same output, is the one that counts. The chapter's answer is computation. Bitcoin's system of counterfeit protection is based on computation: transactions are bundled into blocks whose headers must be formed in a very specific way, requiring an enormous amount of computation to get right but only a small amount to verify as correct (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). That asymmetry is what replaces the authority of a bank.\n\nA reader meets this branch as the wait between paying and being sure. In the chapter, about five minutes after Alice's wallet transmitted the transaction a miner finds a solution and announces the block; roughly nineteen minutes later another miner builds on top of it, giving Alice's transaction two confirmations; and the convention the chapter states is that any block with more than six confirmations is considered very hard to change (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Below the block containing Alice's payment are hundreds of thousands of earlier blocks, linked all the way back to block number zero, the genesis block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe branch has three groups. The mining group explains who builds blocks and how: the miner, the candidate block, the proof of work that makes a candidate valid, the block and its header, the merkle tree that commits to the transactions, the difficulty that keeps the pace at about ten minutes, the hash rate that measures the search, the consensus rules that every block must obey, the coinbase transaction and the reward it pays, the pool through which small miners take part, and the sudoku analogy from the course notes. The security group explains why the record holds: confirmations, the probability behind them, the best blockchain, the block height, accidental forks and alternative blocks, the double spend that all of this prevents, the genesis block, and the judgements a merchant may make before any confirmation exists. The spending group closes the chapter by letting Bob spend what he received.\n\nTwo habits of language should be corrected early. First, a block is not added by anybody's decision: a miner publishes a candidate that satisfies the rules, and each node decides for itself whether to accept it, which the book calls independent validation (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Second, nothing in Bitcoin is final in the absolute sense; the book writes that while the protocol always allows a chain to be undone by a longer chain, the probability of such an event decreases as time passes until it becomes infinitesimal (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The security of this branch is probabilistic, and saying so precisely is part of understanding it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Blockchain> .
chx:S_Mining a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Mining"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Mining" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 62 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Blockchain ;
    skos:definition "Mining is the process that turns a pool of unconfirmed transactions into the next block of the blockchain. The chapter introduces it in one sentence, saying that Alice's transaction does not become part of the blockchain until it is included in a block by a process called mining and that block has been validated by full nodes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). This group gathers everything needed to read that sentence: who does the work, what they build, what makes the result valid, and what they are paid.\n\nThe group explains Bitcoin's answer to a problem that has no obvious solution: how a network of strangers can agree on an order of events without a referee. The chapter names the two purposes that mining serves. Miners can only receive honest income from creating blocks that follow all of Bitcoin's consensus rules, so they are normally incentivized to include only valid transactions, which allows users to make a trust-based assumption that any transaction in a block is valid; and mining currently creates new bitcoins in each block, almost like a central bank printing new money, in an amount that is limited and diminishes with time following a fixed issuance schedule (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nMining is met in this chapter as the five minutes Alice waits, and in the world as an industry. The chapter describes the mechanism as a decentralized lottery in which each miner creates their own ticket by building a candidate block, feeds it to a hash function, and wins if the output matches a template set by the protocol (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The owner's course notes for this chapter follow the same story and add the history of the hardware, from a fast desktop computer in 2010 through graphics cards to the application-specific integrated circuits that a mining company runs in a warehouse by the thousand (Altunel, 2021).\n\nThe group is taken in the order in which a block is built. The miner is the specialized node that does the work. The candidate block is what it assembles from its memory pool. The proof of work is the search that makes the candidate valid, and its difficulty is adjusted so that the search takes about ten minutes across the whole network; the hash rate measures how fast that search is being run. The block and its header are the result, and the merkle tree is the device by which the small header commits to all the transactions. The consensus rules are what every node checks. The coinbase transaction and the block reward are how the miner is paid, and the mining pool is how a small miner gets paid regularly. The sudoku analogy closes the group as a teaching picture.\n\nThree warnings belong to the whole group. Mining does not verify transactions on behalf of the network: every full node verifies for itself, and mining decides only the order and the timing. The ten-minute figure is an average and not a schedule, which the security group takes up. And the figures of this group, the reward, the hash rate and the difficulty, change continuously; what is stable is the rule that produces them, so a learner should remember the rule and look up the number." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Mining> .
chx:S_Miner a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Miner"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Miner" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 63 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Mining ;
    skos:definition "A miner is a participant that builds candidate blocks and searches for the proof of work that would make one of them valid. The book's glossary defines a miner as a network node that finds valid proof of work for new blocks by repeated hashing (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), and its mining chapter describes a concrete one: Jing, who earns bitcoin by running a mining rig, a specialized computer-hardware system designed to mine, connected to a server that runs a full node (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A miner is therefore a full node with extra hardware and an extra job, not a different kind of participant.\n\nMiners exist because somebody has to decide the order of transactions, and Bitcoin pays for that service instead of appointing anybody to it. The chapter describes the balance: mining uses electricity to solve a computational problem, a successful miner collects a reward in the form of new bitcoins and transaction fees, but the reward will only be collected if the miner has included only valid transactions, with the rules for consensus determining what is valid, and this delicate balance provides security for Bitcoin without a central authority (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe chapter meets a miner twice: when Alice's transaction, once validated by a full node, is included in a candidate block, and about five minutes later when a miner finds a solution for the block and announces it to the network (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's mining chapter adds what the miner's node was doing before that: like every other full node it receives and propagates unconfirmed transactions, and in addition it aggregates them into new blocks (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Outside the chapter, miners are companies with warehouses of hardware, as the owner's course notes describe (Altunel, 2021).\n\nA miner's income is a sum of two parts. The chapter says that each miner includes a special transaction in their candidate blocks, one that pays their own address the block reward plus the sum of transaction fees from all the transactions included in the candidate block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). With the 2023 subsidy of 6.25 bitcoins, which is 625,000,000 satoshis, and fees of 183,000 satoshis from the transactions in the block, the total is 625,183,000 satoshis, as the addition above shows. The work itself is the repeated hashing of the candidate's header, which the proof-of-work concept describes.\n\nA miner cannot cheat by writing itself a larger reward. The book puts the question directly and answers it: an inflated reward would make the block invalid in the eyes of everyone else, wasting the electricity spent on the proof of work, because the miner only gets to spend the reward if the block is accepted (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Nor is a miner a judge of transactions: it chooses which valid transactions to include, usually by fee rate, but it cannot make an invalid one valid. And mining is not a vote of users: it counts computation, and the nodes that enforce the rules decide what they will accept." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Miner> .
chx:S_CandidateBlock a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Candidate block"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Candidate block" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 64 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Mining ;
    skos:definition "A candidate block is the block a miner is working on: the transactions it wants to include, a commitment to the previous block, and the other header fields, assembled but without a valid proof of work. The book explains the name, saying that the block is called a candidate block because it is not yet a valid block, as it does not contain a valid proof of work, and that it becomes valid only if the miner succeeds in finding a solution according to the proof-of-work algorithm (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe candidate block is where a miner's choices are made, and therefore where the market for block space is settled. The chapter says that transactions are added to the new block prioritized by the highest fee rate transactions first and a few other criteria, and that each miner starts the process of mining a new candidate block as soon as they receive the previous block from the network, knowing that some other miner won that iteration of the lottery (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The candidate is also the lottery ticket in the chapter's own image: each miner can create their own ticket by creating a candidate block that includes the new transactions they want to mine plus some additional data fields (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe chapter meets the candidate when it says that Alice's transaction, once validated by a full node, was included in a candidate block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's mining chapter describes the moment of construction: upon receiving and validating a new block, the mining node compares it against the transactions in its memory pool and removes any that the block contained, and then immediately constructs a new partial block, a candidate for the next block, from what remains (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Miners in a pool are each given a slightly different candidate, so that they do not duplicate one another's work (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nAssembling a candidate is selection plus commitment. The miner takes transactions from the memory pool in order of what they pay for the space they use, up to the block's limit, which the small example illustrates by taking the first three of five pending transactions and totalling the fees they bring, 470 satoshis. It then writes the coinbase transaction that pays itself the subsidy and those fees, computes the merkle root over the whole list, and fills in the header fields: the hash of the previous block, the merkle root, a timestamp and the target. Only the nonce is then left to vary.\n\nA candidate block is not a block: it has no proof of work, so no node would accept it, and the miner may abandon it at any moment. It is also private until it is solved, which is why two miners can be working on candidates that contain the same transactions without any conflict. And the contents of a candidate are a choice, not a rule: a miner may legally build a block with no transactions at all except the coinbase, which is one of the problems the book describes on the test network, where fees have no value (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#CandidateBlock> .
chx:S_ProofOfWork a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Proof of work"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Proof of work" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 65 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Mining ;
    skos:definition "Proof of work is a piece of data that takes a great deal of computation to find and almost none to check. The chapter builds the idea from the hash function: a miner feeds a candidate block into an algorithm that scrambles the data, producing output that looks nothing like the input; the same input always gives the same output, but nobody can predict the output for a new input; and if the output matches a template determined by the protocol, the block is accepted (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter then names the result: the simple verification process probabilistically proves the work was done, so the data necessary to generate that proof, in this case the block, is called proof of work (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nProof of work is what makes the record expensive to rewrite, and therefore what makes a confirmed payment reliable. The chapter puts the number on it: as of writing, the number of candidate blocks miners need to try before finding a winning combination is about 168 billion trillion, and that is also how many times the hash function needs to be run; yet once a winning combination has been found, anyone can verify the block is valid by running the hash function just once (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Changing a recorded transaction would mean redoing that work and then outpacing everyone else, which is the argument the security group develops.\n\nThe reader meets proof of work as the five minutes between Alice's payment and its first confirmation, and as the electricity bill of the mining industry. The book's mining chapter gives a miniature version that can be run by hand: hash the phrase Hello, world! followed by a number and look for a result that begins with a zero. It finds the answer at the number 32 and remarks that although it only takes one hash computation to verify, it took thirty-two to find (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The owner's course notes describe the same mechanism as repeatedly hashing the header of the block with a random number until a solution matching a predetermined pattern emerges (Altunel, 2021).\n\nThe search is a loop with one changing field. The example above is the demonstration version used by the interactive page of the practice branch: hashing a block number, a nonce and the text of a payment, and looking for a digest that begins with four zeros, the first nonce that succeeds is 6630. Four leading hexadecimal zeros mean that about one attempt in sixteen to the fourth power, that is one in 65,536, should work, so a few thousand attempts is the expected order. Bitcoin's real condition is the same test with a far smaller threshold: the double hash of the eighty-byte header, read as a number, must be below the target that the difficulty concept explains.\n\nProof of work proves effort, not honesty and not truth. A block with a perfect proof of work is still rejected if any of its transactions breaks a rule, as the consensus-rules concept explains. The proof is also probabilistic: the chapter's own wording is that the verification process is able to probabilistically prove the work was done, and a lucky miner may find a solution on its first attempt (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Finally, the work is not a puzzle with a meaningful answer; the solution has no use outside the system, which is both the source of its security and the reason its energy cost is debated." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#ProofOfWork> .
chx:S_Block a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Block"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Block" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 66 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Mining ;
    skos:definition "A block is the unit in which transactions are added to the blockchain. The chapter says that transactions are bundled into blocks, and that blocks have a very small header that must be formed in a very specific way, requiring an enormous amount of computation to get right but only a small amount to verify as correct (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's chapter on the blockchain is exact about the structure: a block is a container data structure that aggregates transactions for inclusion in the blockchain, made of a header containing metadata followed by a long list of transactions that make up the bulk of its size (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe block is what gives transactions an order and a time. Within a single chain of blocks the position of a transaction defines whether it is earlier or later than another, and a transaction is valid only if it spends outputs of transactions that appeared earlier, which the book says eliminates the problem of double spending (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The block is also the unit in which block space is sold, so its limit is what makes fees necessary, and the unit in which security accumulates, since confirmations are counted in blocks.\n\nThe chapter meets the block when the winning block containing Alice's transaction becomes part of the blockchain and counts as one confirmation of it, and it gives a link to the block so that a reader can look it up (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's blockchain chapter lists the fields a stored block has: four bytes of block size, eighty bytes of header, a compact count of the transactions that follow, and then the transactions themselves (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe proportions are worth remembering. The header is eighty bytes whatever the block holds, while the transactions can run to about four million bytes, so a complete block can be almost fifty thousand times larger than its header, as the division above gives and as the book states (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). That ratio is the whole reason lightweight clients are possible, since a program that keeps only headers keeps a chain thousands of times smaller than the full one. The link between the small header and the large body is the merkle root, and the identifier of a block is the double hash of the header alone, which the book stresses by calling it more accurately the block header hash (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nTwo points are easily confused. A block is not a fixed number of transactions: the limit is on size, counted as weight, so a block may hold one transaction or many thousands. And the hash that names a block is not stored inside it; the book notes that the block's hash is computed by each node as the block is received from the network, which is why every node can check the name for itself and nobody can rename a block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Block> .
chx:S_BlockHeader a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Block header"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Block header" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 67 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Mining ;
    skos:definition "The block header is the small, fixed part of a block that carries its metadata and that mining actually works on. The book's blockchain chapter lists its six fields with their sizes: four bytes of version, thirty-two bytes holding a hash of the previous block, thirty-two bytes holding the merkle root of the block's transactions, four bytes of timestamp in Unix epoch time, four bytes holding a compact encoding of the proof-of-work target, and four bytes of nonce, described as arbitrary data used for the proof-of-work algorithm (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Four plus thirty-two plus thirty-two plus four plus four plus four is eighty bytes.\n\nEverything the chapter claims about the chain follows from this header. Because the header contains the hash of the parent block, any change to a parent requires the child's hash to change, which requires the grandchild to change, and so on; the book concludes that once a block has many generations following it, it cannot be changed without forcing a recalculation of all subsequent blocks, and that because such a recalculation would require enormous computation, the existence of a long chain of blocks makes the blockchain's deep history impractical to change, which is a key feature of Bitcoin's security (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Because the header also contains the merkle root, it commits to every transaction without containing any of them.\n\nA reader meets the header's fields in the output of any block explorer or of Bitcoin Core's command for retrieving a block: the chapter's own data for the first block shows a version of 1, a merkle root of 4a5e1e4b...deda33b, a time of 1231006505, a nonce of 2083236893 and a compact target written as 1d00ffff (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The mining hardware of the book's example is given nothing but this header to work on, and it tests trillions of variations of it per second (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe header can be assembled and hashed with the standard library, which is the executed example of this concept. Packing the version as a four-byte little-endian integer, then thirty-two zero bytes for the parent of the first block, then the merkle root with its bytes reversed, then the timestamp, the compact target and the nonce as three more four-byte integers, gives exactly eighty bytes; hashing them twice with SHA-256 and reversing the result produces 00000000...0a8ce26f, the identifier of the genesis block that the book prints (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Nothing else is needed: the block hash is a function of the header alone.\n\nThree cautions. The header's version field is no longer simply a version, as the book says its use has evolved over time, and the chapter on Bitcoin Core explains how it came to carry signals for upgrades (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The timestamp is the approximate creation time, set by the miner within limits that the book states as two consensus rules, that no node accepts a block whose time is more than two hours in the future and none accepts a block whose time is not later than the median time of the last eleven blocks, which the book calls the median time past, so a block's time is not a reliable clock (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). And the nonce is only four bytes, so it is exhausted quickly; miners then change other fields, which the book calls the extra nonce solution (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#BlockHeader> .
chx:S_MerkleTree a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Merkle tree"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Merkle tree" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 68 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Mining ;
    skos:definition "A merkle tree, also called a binary hash tree, is a structure for summarizing and verifying the integrity of large sets of data. The book defines it as a tree constructed by hashing paired data, the leaves, then pairing and hashing the results until a single hash remains, the merkle root, and notes that in Bitcoin the leaves are almost always the transactions of a single block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The hash used is SHA-256 applied twice, which the book calls double-SHA256 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe tree is what lets the eighty-byte header stand for a whole block. The book says that merkle trees are used in Bitcoin to summarize all the transactions in a block, producing an overall commitment to the entire set of transactions and permitting a very efficient process to verify whether a transaction is included in a block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It also gives the efficiency: with N elements, checking whether any one of them is in the tree takes about the base-two logarithm of N calculations, so proving that one transaction out of the roughly sixteen thousand in the largest possible block is present needs the transaction, the eighty-byte header and 448 bytes of path, which makes the largest possible proof almost ten thousand times smaller than the largest possible block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA reader meets the root as a field in every block's data: the first block's merkle root, 4a5e1e4b...deda33b, is equal to the identifier of its single transaction, because a tree with one leaf has that leaf as its root. The tree itself is met by lightweight clients, which the book says use merkle paths extensively: a peer sends the block header together with a path of hashes linking the transaction of interest to the root, and the client recomputes the root and so connects the transaction to the block and the block to the chain, receiving less than a kilobyte instead of a whole block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe construction is a few lines of code, and the executed example performs it. Four pieces of data, A, B, C and D, are each double-hashed to give the four leaves; the first two leaves are joined and double-hashed to give one parent, the last two likewise, and the two parents are joined and double-hashed to give the root, whose first sixteen hexadecimal digits are 59e82c45a3662f55. Because the tree is binary it needs an even number of nodes at each level, so the book explains that if there is an odd number the last hash is duplicated (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Bitcoin's way of doing that duplication has a known flaw, which the book quotes at length from Bitcoin Core's source, where it is logged as a vulnerability about duplicate transaction identifiers (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe merkle root commits to the transactions but does not contain them, so a header alone does not let anyone read a block. A merkle path also proves only inclusion: it shows that a transaction is in a block, not that the transaction or the block obeys the rules, which is exactly the limit of simplified payment verification. And the chapter's own text never mentions merkle trees; the concept is brought in here because the chapter's claim that a small header commits to many transactions cannot otherwise be explained." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#MerkleTree> .
chx:S_Difficulty a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Difficulty and target"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Difficulty and target" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 69 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Mining ;
    skos:definition "The target is the number that a block header's hash must be below for the block to be valid, and the difficulty is the inverse way of describing the same thing: the book's glossary calls difficulty a network-wide setting that controls how much computation is required to produce a proof of work, and the difficulty target a difficulty at which all the computation in the network will find blocks approximately every ten minutes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book states the relation plainly: a higher target means it is less difficult to find a hash below it, a lower target means it is more difficult, and the target and difficulty are inversely related (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nWithout an adjustable target the system would not keep time. The book explains that blocks are generated every ten minutes on average, that this is Bitcoin's heartbeat and underpins the frequency of currency issuance and the speed of transaction settlement, and that it has to remain constant over decades while computer power and the number of miners change, so the difficulty of mining must be adjusted to account for those changes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter touches the same idea when it says that over time, as the height of new blocks increases, so does the computation difficulty for the chain as a whole (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader meets the target in a block's data as a four-byte compact encoding: the first block's is written 1d00ffff and its difficulty is reported as 1 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Retargeting is met as the event every two weeks when every node recomputes the threshold. The owner's course notes describe the consequence from the hardware side: the difficulty rose so far that mining became profitable only with application-specific integrated circuits, after passing through desktop computers and graphics cards (Altunel, 2021).\n\nThe compact form packs an exponent and a coefficient into four bytes. Reading 1d00ffff, the leading byte 0x1d is the exponent, 29, and the remaining three bytes are the coefficient, 0x00ffff, that is 65,535; the target is the coefficient multiplied by two to the power of eight times the exponent minus three, which equals 0xffff times two to the power 208, as the check above confirms. With that target, about two to the power thirty-two attempts, roughly 4.3 billion, are expected per block. The adjustment itself is arithmetic on elapsed time: every 2,016 blocks each node independently computes a new target from the ratio between the time the last blocks actually took and the 20,160 minutes they should have taken, and the book notes that the change is limited to a factor of four in either direction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThree details are often got wrong. Retargeting is not performed by anybody: it occurs automatically and on every node independently, which is why all nodes agree on the result (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The window has an off-by-one error that the book records, since the calculation uses the time of the previous 2,015 blocks rather than 2,016, biasing difficulty upward by about 0.05 per cent (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). And the target is independent of how many transactions a block holds or how much value they move, so the energy spent securing Bitcoin does not depend on how much Bitcoin is used (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Difficulty> .
chx:S_HashRate a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Hash rate"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Hash rate" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 70 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Mining ;
    skos:definition "The hash rate is the speed at which hash computations are performed, counted in hashes per second. It is the natural measure of mining effort, because mining is nothing but repeated hashing, and it is used both for a single machine and for the network as a whole. The chapter gives the figure in a different but equivalent form: the number of candidate blocks miners need to try before finding a winning combination is about 168 billion trillion, and that is also how many times the hash function needs to be run (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe hash rate is how the cost of rewriting history is quantified, so it is the number behind every security claim in this branch. The book's note on block intervals makes the link: a malicious miner wanting to double spend a transaction would need to do an amount of work equal to ten minutes of the total network hash rate in order to create a chain with equal proof of work (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A share of the hash rate is also what defines the attacks the book describes, which it calls hashrate attacks and which succeed with probability depending on that share (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nYou meet hash rates in mining advertisements, in charts of the network's growth, and in the book's descriptions of hardware: the mining rigs of its example use application-specific integrated circuits in which hundreds of thousands of circuits run the double SHA-256 algorithm in parallel, testing trillions of variations of the header per second (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book also records the history of growth: the hashing power has increased exponentially every year of Bitcoin's existence, through the move from processors to graphics cards and then to purpose-built chips (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe network's rate follows from the chapter's own number by one division. If about 168 billion trillion attempts, that is 1.68 times ten to the twenty-third, are expected for each block, and a block is expected every ten minutes, that is 600 seconds, then the network is running about 2.8 times ten to the twentieth hashes a second, which the calculation above gives as 280,000,000,000,000,000,000. In the units the industry uses that is about 280 exahashes a second. A single pool miner's contribution is measured the same way, and a pool estimates it by counting shares, as the mining-pool concept explains.\n\nA hash rate is an estimate, not a measurement: nobody counts the hashes of the world's miners, so the figure is inferred from the difficulty and the observed block times, which is why published values differ and fluctuate. It also measures only this one kind of computation, so comparing it with the speed of a general-purpose computer is meaningless. And a higher network hash rate does not make payments faster; the difficulty adjusts to keep the pace at ten minutes, so extra hash rate buys security, not speed." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#HashRate> .
chx:S_ConsensusRules a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Consensus rules"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Consensus rules" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 71 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Mining ;
    skos:definition "The consensus rules are the rules by which a full node decides whether a block and the transactions in it are acceptable. The book's glossary defines them as the block validation rules that full nodes follow to stay in consensus with other nodes, and separately defines consensus itself as the situation in which most nodes have the same blocks in their locally validated best blockchain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter introduces the term through the miner's incentive, saying that miners can only receive honest income from creating blocks that follow all of Bitcoin's consensus rules (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe rules are what makes a decentralized record possible at all. Because every node applies the same rules, no miner can profit from breaking them: the book explains that an invalid coinbase transaction would make the entire block invalid, so the block would be rejected and the transaction would never become part of the blockchain, and that the miners therefore have to construct a block based on the shared rules that all nodes follow and mine it with a correct solution, expending electricity that is wasted if they cheat (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter draws the consequence for users: because miners are incentivized to include only valid transactions, users may optionally make a trust-based assumption that any transaction in a block is a valid transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader meets the rules as the list of checks a node performs. The book names the functions in Bitcoin Core that contain them and gives the items: the block data structure must be syntactically valid, the block header hash must be less than the target, the timestamp must lie between the median time past, which the book defines as the median time of the last eleven blocks, and two hours in the future, the weight must be within acceptable limits, the first transaction and only the first must be a coinbase transaction, and all transactions in the block must be valid by the transaction checklist (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter also refers to the rules when it limits an output's value, which Bitcoin Core fixes in its source as at most 21,000,000 times one hundred million satoshis (Bitcoin Core, 2026).\n\nValidation is the conjunction of independent checks, which the small example renders as a set of five that must all hold; the count is how many were applied and the verdict is whether every one passed. The order matters in practice, since cheap checks come first, but logically a block is valid only if nothing fails. The book calls this independent validation and names it the third step of Bitcoin's consensus mechanism, after independent verification of transactions and the aggregation of transactions into blocks (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nConsensus rules are not the same as consensus, and the book warns against confusing the two: the rules are what nodes check, while consensus is the agreement that results (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The rules also change, but only in careful ways: the book devotes a long section to how they are altered, distinguishing a soft fork, which tightens them compatibly, from a hard fork, which does not (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). And the rules are enforced by nodes, not by miners; a block with a perfect proof of work that breaks a rule is simply discarded." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#ConsensusRules> .
chx:S_CoinbaseTransaction a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Coinbase transaction"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Coinbase transaction" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 72 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Mining ;
    skos:definition "The coinbase transaction is the special first transaction of every block, by which the miner pays itself. The book's glossary calls it the first transaction in a block, always created by a miner, and the mining chapter adds the distinguishing property: unlike regular transactions the coinbase transaction does not consume unspent outputs as inputs, but has only one input, called the coinbase input, which implicitly contains the block reward (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter describes it without naming it, as a special transaction that pays the miner's own Bitcoin address the block reward plus the sum of transaction fees from all the transactions included in the candidate block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThis transaction is where new bitcoins enter the world, so it is the only place in the system where money is created rather than moved. It is also what ties the miner's income to the validity of the whole block: the fees it claims are the differences between the inputs and outputs of the other transactions, so an error anywhere in the block makes the coinbase transaction wrong and the block invalid. The chapter's statement that a successful miner receives the reward after the block is added to the blockchain and the reward transaction becomes spendable refers to this transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader meets a coinbase transaction in every block, including the very first: the genesis block contains a single transaction whose coinbase input holds the text about the Chancellor on the brink of a second bailout for banks, which the book says was intended as proof of the earliest date the block could have been created (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book notes that in 2023 it was common for a coinbase transaction to have two outputs, one a zero-value output committing to the witnesses of the block's segregated-witness transactions and the other paying the miner (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The interactive demonstration of the practice branch has a page devoted to coinbase transactions.\n\nThe arithmetic of the coinbase transaction is the miner's income statement: it spends nothing, and it pays out the subsidy plus the total fees, as the small example shows with zero inputs spent and 625,183,000 satoshis paid. The book gives the formula for the fee part, the sum of the inputs of all the other transactions minus the sum of their outputs, and the function in Bitcoin Core that computes the subsidy part from the block height (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Because the coinbase input spends nothing, it is free space, and the book says the coinbase field provides up to one hundred bytes for arbitrary data (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThree traps. The output of a coinbase transaction cannot be spent immediately: the book's verification checklist requires at least a hundred confirmations, the constant it calls COINBASE_MATURITY, and its blockchain chapter explains that after a hundred blocks there is so much stability that the reward can be spent (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The term is also overloaded: the glossary warns that the coinbase, a field, is not to be confused with the coinbase transaction, and neither has anything to do with a company of a similar name (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Finally, the miner cannot write any amount it likes, because every node recomputes the permitted total." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#CoinbaseTransaction> .
chx:S_BlockReward a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Block reward"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Block reward" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 73 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Mining ;
    skos:definition "The block reward is what a miner earns for a block: the newly created bitcoins, which the book calls the subsidy, together with the transaction fees of everything in the block. The chapter states the first half as a fact about money, saying that mining currently creates new bitcoins in each block, almost like a central bank printing new money, and that the amount created per block is limited and diminishes with time following a fixed issuance schedule (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's mining chapter gives the schedule: bitcoins are minted during the creation of each block at a fixed and diminishing rate, and every 210,000 blocks, or approximately every four years, the issuance rate is decreased by fifty per cent (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reward is the engine of the whole system. It pays for the computation that secures the record, and because it diminishes on a schedule that nobody can change, it also defines Bitcoin's monetary policy. The book draws the conclusion: the finite and diminishing issuance creates a fixed monetary supply that resists inflation, and unlike a fiat currency, which can be printed in infinite numbers by a central bank, no individual party has the ability to inflate the supply (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It also tells the miner's future: after the subsidy reaches zero, blocks will contain no new bitcoins and miners will be rewarded solely through transaction fees (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader meets the figure whenever a block is inspected. For the first four years of the network each block contained fifty new bitcoins; the first halving occurred at block 210,000; the subsidy was 6.25 bitcoins when the book was written in 2023; and the book predicted the next halving at block 840,000, in April or May of 2024, which takes it to 3.125 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The owner's course notes, written in 2021, give the then-current figure of 6.25 newly created bitcoins per block, which is the same era (Altunel, 2021).\n\nThe rule is a halving, which in whole numbers is a right shift. Bitcoin Core computes the subsidy by dividing the block height by the halving interval of 210,000 to get the number of halvings, starting from fifty times one hundred million satoshis and shifting that right once per halving, returning zero once there have been sixty-four of them (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). For the 2023 era, three halvings had occurred, so the subsidy was fifty times ten to the eighth shifted right three times, that is 625,000,000 satoshis or 6.25 bitcoins, as the expression above evaluates. Summing 210,000 blocks of each successive subsidy gives 2,099,999,997,690,000 satoshis in total, which is the figure the book's own script prints and just under 21 million bitcoins (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nAny particular reward figure goes stale, so learn the rule and not the number; the book's own glossary still says the reward is currently 12.5 bitcoins, which was true two halvings earlier (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Note also that the maximum is an upper limit rather than a promise: the book remarks that a miner may intentionally mine a block taking less than the full reward, and that such blocks have already been mined, so the total issued may end up lower (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). And the reward is not paid immediately in a usable form, because the coinbase output must mature for a hundred blocks." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#BlockReward> .
chx:S_MiningPool a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Mining pool"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Mining pool" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 74 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Mining ;
    skos:definition "A mining pool is an arrangement in which many miners search for the same block together and share whatever is found. The chapter describes it in one sentence: miners who participate in a mining pool have set up their software to create candidate blocks that assign the reward to a pool address, and from there a share of the reward is distributed to the members of the pool in proportion to the amount of work they contributed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's glossary calls the practice pooled mining and defines it in the same terms (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nPools exist because mining alone has become a lottery with very long odds. The book says that in this highly competitive environment individual miners working alone do not stand a chance, that the likelihood of finding a block to offset electricity and hardware costs is so low that it represents a gamble, and that by participating in a pool miners get a smaller share of the overall reward but typically get rewarded every day, reducing uncertainty (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Its example is concrete: a miner with a ten-thousandth of one per cent of the network's hash rate would find a block about once every twenty years alone, and in a pool holding one per cent of the rate would average more than one block a day and earn the same amount, only far more evenly (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA reader meets pools as the names that appear beside blocks in an explorer, and as the services that small miners sign up to. The book describes the arrangement: miners configure their equipment to connect to a pool server after creating an account, their hardware stays connected while mining, successful blocks pay the reward to a pool address rather than to individual miners, and the server periodically pays the miners once their share of the rewards passes a threshold, usually charging a percentage fee for the service (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe pool measures contributions with Bitcoin's own proof of work at an easier setting. The book explains that the pool sets a higher target, typically more than a thousand times easier than the network's, so that each time a pool miner finds a header hash below the pool target they prove they did the hashing work and earn a share; each miner is given a slightly different coinbase template so that no effort is duplicated, and the shares measure the work done without any possibility of cheating (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Payment is then proportional, which the small example computes: with shares of fifty, thirty and twenty, a reward of 625,000,000 satoshis splits into 312,500,000, 187,500,000 and 125,000,000.\n\nPools change who controls what. A pool member runs the hardware but the pool decides what goes into the candidate block, so hash rate concentrates in the hands of pool operators, and the book's section on hashrate attacks notes that a pool with a large share can attempt them while its participants might remain blissfully unaware (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A share is also not a bitcoin: it is a unit of accounting inside the pool, worth nothing outside it. And a pool is a counterparty, so its members trust it to measure and to pay." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#MiningPool> .
chx:S_SudokuAnalogy a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Sudoku analogy"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Sudoku analogy" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 75 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Mining ;
    skos:definition "The sudoku analogy is a teaching picture, taken from the owner's course notes for this chapter, in which mining is described as a giant competitive game of sudoku. The notes list the features that make the comparison work: the puzzle is large, several thousand rows and columns in size; it resets every time someone finds a solution; its difficulty adjusts automatically so that a solution takes approximately ten minutes; and if someone shows you a completed puzzle you can verify it quite quickly (Altunel, 2021). The notes conclude that Bitcoin's puzzle, based on a cryptographic hash, is asymmetrically hard to solve, easy to verify and adjustable in difficulty, and so exhibits similar characteristics (Altunel, 2021).\n\nThe analogy is useful because it captures the one property that makes proof of work possible, the asymmetry between solving and checking, in an everyday object. A student who has filled in a sudoku knows from experience that finding the answer is laborious while checking a finished grid is quick, and that a larger grid is harder without being harder to check. Those are exactly the properties the chapter claims for the hash search: an enormous amount of computation to get right, only a small amount of computation to verify as correct (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe analogy is met in the course notes of this chapter and in many introductory presentations, and it is kept here as a bridge for a first reading. It is not in the third edition of the textbook, which prefers a different picture: the chapter calls mining a decentralized lottery in which each miner creates a lottery ticket, and the mining chapter uses a game of dice in which players try to throw less than a falling target (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A learner meets all three pictures and should be able to say what each one gets right.\n\nThe comparison can be made quantitative, which is also where it breaks down. An ordinary sudoku has nine times nine, that is eighty-one cells, and a solver explores a search space that a computer clears in a moment; Bitcoin's search needs about 168 billion trillion attempts per block, a number vastly larger than eighty-one, as the comparison above states (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The notes handle this by enlarging the puzzle to several thousand rows and columns and by resetting it after every solution, which mirrors the retargeting of the difficulty and the start of a new round after each block (Altunel, 2021).\n\nThe analogy must be labelled as one. A sudoku has a unique solution reached by reasoning, whereas a proof of work has very many possible solutions and no reasoning helps: the only method is trial, because the hash function's output cannot be steered. A sudoku also has no reward, no competition between solvers for the same grid and no chain of earlier grids, so the analogy says nothing about incentives, about confirmations or about why a long chain is hard to rewrite. Use it to understand the asymmetry and then put it down." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#SudokuAnalogy> .
chx:S_RuleChange a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Soft fork and hard fork"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Soft fork and hard fork" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 76 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Mining ;
    skos:definition "A rule change is an alteration of the consensus rules, and it comes in two kinds. The book explains that while the consensus rules are invariable in the short term and must be consistent across all nodes, they are not invariable in the long term, because in order to evolve the system the rules can change from time to time to accommodate new features, improvements or bug fixes, and that upgrades to a consensus system are much more difficult than ordinary software upgrades and require coordination between all participants (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A change that older nodes still accept is a soft fork; one they reject is a hard fork.\n\nThe distinction decides whether an upgrade can happen without splitting the network, so it is the practical reason Bitcoin can change at all. The book states the rule precisely: a soft fork is a forward-compatible change to the consensus rules that allows unupgraded clients to continue to operate in consensus with the new rules, and it adds the consequence that is not immediately obvious, that soft forks can only be used to constrain the consensus rules and not to expand them, because transactions and blocks created under the new rules must be valid under the old rules too but not the other way round (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader has already met a rule change in this chapter without being told. The two extra fields at the front of Alice's transaction, and the witness structure at its end, were added as part of the segregated witness change to the consensus rules, and the book notes that the obvious way of implementing that change would have required a hard fork, with all the challenges that brings, so an alternative approach was found that worked as a soft fork (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's glossary describes the other kind as a permanent divergence that commonly occurs when non-upgraded nodes cannot validate blocks created by upgraded nodes following newer rules (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe test is a comparison of what each version of the rules accepts. If the set of things the new rules accept is contained in the set the old rules accept, then nothing new offends an old node and the change is a soft fork, which the small example decides for a new rule set that is a subset of the old one; if the new rules accept something the old ones reject, old nodes will reject those blocks and the chain splits, which is a hard fork. The book adds that the term soft fork does not name a particular method but a family of them, all sharing the property that they do not require every node to upgrade or force unupgraded nodes out of consensus, and it describes two implemented in Bitcoin by reinterpreting opcodes that previously did nothing (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThree cautions. The word fork here is not the accidental fork of the security group, which is two blocks at the same height, and the book remarks that in practice a soft fork is not a fork at all (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A soft fork is compatible but not free: an unupgraded node keeps accepting blocks yet no longer verifies the new rule itself, so a user who wants the new protection must upgrade. And compatibility is a property of the rules, not of intentions: a change that was meant to be gentle is still a hard fork if any old node would reject a block that a new one accepts." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#RuleChange> .
chx:S_Security a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Security"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Security" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 77 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Blockchain ;
    skos:definition "Security, in this chapter, means the confidence that a payment already recorded will stay recorded. This group collects the concepts that make that confidence precise. It explains the confirmation, the unit in which the confidence is counted; the probability that lies behind the counting; the rule by which nodes choose between competing chains, the best blockchain; the block height that positions a block in the record; the accidental fork and the alternative block that a dishonest miner would have to produce; the double spend that all of this is designed to prevent; the genesis block at the bottom of the chain; and the judgement a merchant may make when there is no confirmation at all.\n\nThe group matters because the honest answer to the question is probabilistic, and students are owed the honest answer. The chapter does not say that a confirmed payment cannot be reversed; it says that as the blocks pile on top of each other it becomes harder to reverse the transaction, thereby giving Bob more and more confidence that Alice's payment is secure (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The reason is an argument about cost: the chapter shows that a miner conspiring with Alice would have to produce two blocks where an honest miner produces one, and that the high cost of doing so for a small additional payment is normally much less profitable than honest mining (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA reader meets this group as the advice that merchants follow: nothing for a cup of coffee, a few confirmations for an ordinary purchase, six or more for something valuable. The chapter gives the convention, that any block with more than six confirmations is considered very hard to change because it would require an immense amount of computation to recalculate six blocks plus one new block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), and the book's mining chapter gives the reasoning behind the harder cases, including an example in which a gallery hands over paintings after one confirmation and loses both the paintings and the payment (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe group is read from the unit upward. A confirmation is one block built on top; the probability concept quantifies what each one buys, using the table in Nakamoto's paper; the best blockchain is the rule that decides which chain those blocks belong to; the height is where a block sits; a fork is what happens when two blocks claim the same height, and an alternative block is what an attacker would need; the double spend is the attack itself; the genesis block is the anchor that makes heights and chains well defined; and the acceptance of a small payment without confirmation shows that the right number of confirmations is a business decision and not a protocol rule.\n\nGuard against two opposite errors. One is to treat six confirmations as a magic number: the chapter calls it a convention, and the book's own advice for high-value items is to wait longer, mentioning twenty-four hours, which is about 144 confirmations (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The other is to treat the system as fragile because nothing is absolute; the same arithmetic that denies certainty shows that the probability of reversal falls away very quickly, and the book's figure of a probability of about two in ten thousand after six confirmations against an attacker with a tenth of the hash rate is the concrete form of that (Nakamoto, 2008)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Security> .
chx:S_Confirmation a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Confirmation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Confirmation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 78 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Security ;
    skos:definition "A confirmation is a block that commits to a transaction, counted once for the block that contains it and once more for each block built on top. The chapter defines it as it happens: the winning block containing Alice's transaction became part of the blockchain, and the block containing Alice's transaction is counted as one confirmation of that transaction; each block mined on top of the one containing Alice's transaction counts as an additional confirmation (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's glossary says the same and adds the common rule of thumb, that six or more confirmations is considered sufficient proof that a transaction cannot be reversed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nConfirmations are how the chapter turns a vague feeling of safety into a number that a merchant can act on. Each additional block multiplies the work an attacker would have to redo, because the attacker must not only replace the block containing the transaction but also outpace the honest chain that has grown in the meantime. The chapter spells the arithmetic out: after a second block is mined on top, Alice's transaction can only be changed if two alternative blocks are mined plus a new block on top of them, a total of three blocks, for Alice to take back the money she sent Bob (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA reader meets confirmations in every wallet and explorer, usually as a small counter beside a payment. In the chapter, Alice's transaction gets its first confirmation about five minutes after it was transmitted and its second about nineteen minutes later (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The owner's course notes record the same figures and the misconception they create, noting that waiting for a full six confirmations may take up to sixty minutes (Altunel, 2021). Bitcoin Core reports the count directly; the chapter's own data for the first block shows a confirmations field with a very large value.\n\nThe counting is addition, and the small example performs it: a transaction whose block has two blocks on top has three confirmations. The reason each one matters is the chain of header hashes. Because each header commits to its parent, the book explains that any change to a parent block requires a child's hash to change, which requires a change in the grandchild, and so on, so that once a block has many generations following it, it cannot be changed without forcing a recalculation of all subsequent blocks (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The cost of that recalculation is what a confirmation measures.\n\nA confirmation is not a guarantee and six is not a law. The chapter calls the six-block figure a convention, and the book's mining chapter describes a hashrate attack in which an attacker with enough power invalidates six or more blocks in a row, so that transactions considered immutable become invalid (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the other direction, the number of confirmations says nothing about whether the transaction was correct, only about how hard it now is to remove; a payment to the wrong address is just as irreversible as a payment to the right one." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Confirmation> .
chx:S_Probability a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Probability of reversal"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Probability of reversal" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 79 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Security ;
    skos:definition "The probability of reversal is the chance that a transaction already in the blockchain is removed, because somebody builds a competing chain that excludes it and that other nodes come to prefer. The chapter states that such a probability exists without quantifying it, saying that normally the high cost of dishonestly creating two blocks for a small additional payment is much less profitable than honestly creating a new block, making it unlikely that a confirmed transaction will be deliberately changed (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Nakamoto's paper quantifies the same event, calculating the probability that an attacker ever catches up from a given number of blocks behind (Nakamoto, 2008).\n\nWithout a number, the advice to wait for confirmations is folklore. With one, it becomes engineering: a merchant can weigh the value of the goods against the chance of losing them and choose how long to wait. The paper's own conclusion is the one the chapter repeats in words, that the probability of a successful attack drops exponentially as blocks are added, and that the receiver therefore becomes safer by waiting (Nakamoto, 2008). The book's blockchain chapter puts the same thing as a picture of geological layers, in which the topsoil may blow away while the deeper strata have been undisturbed for ages (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader meets these numbers in the appendix of the book, which reproduces Nakamoto's paper, and in the research record of this course, where the figures were recomputed: for an attacker controlling a tenth of the hash rate the probability of catching up falls from 0.2045873 at one confirmation to 0.0002428 at six, and a transcription of the paper's own procedure reproduces that table exactly (Nakamoto, 2008). The chapter's own figures of five minutes and nineteen minutes are the time axis of the same table.\n\nTwo calculations are worth doing. The simple one is the paper's own expression for an attacker who is already a given number of blocks behind and must close the gap: with a share of a tenth of the hash rate the chance is the ratio of the attacker's share to the honest share raised to the number of blocks, which for six blocks is 0.1 divided by 0.9, to the sixth power, about 1.882 in a million, as the expression above gives. The paper's full formula also allows for the attacker's progress during the waiting time, which raises the figure for six confirmations to about 0.0002428; both numbers were executed for this chapter, and both fall away quickly as blocks are added (Nakamoto, 2008).\n\nThese probabilities rest on assumptions, and the assumptions matter more than the digits. They assume the attacker has a minority of the hash rate, and the book's section on hashrate attacks shows that with a majority the arithmetic changes qualitatively, since success becomes almost certain given enough patience (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). They also describe only this one attack: they say nothing about a stolen key, a faulty wallet or a payment sent to the wrong place, which are far more common ways to lose money than a reorganization of the chain." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Probability> .
chx:S_BestBlockchain a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Best blockchain"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Best blockchain" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 80 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Security ;
    skos:definition "The best blockchain is the valid chain of blocks that carries the most cumulative proof of work, and it is the chain that a node treats as the record. The chapter states the rule in its account of why a confirmed payment is hard to change: when there are multiple alternative blocks to choose from, Bitcoin full nodes choose the chain of valid blocks with the most total proof of work, called the best blockchain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's mining chapter repeats it as the final part of the consensus mechanism, the assembly of blocks into chains and the selection of the chain with the most proof of work (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThis single rule is what produces agreement without agreement being negotiated. Every node applies it to the blocks it has, so nodes that have seen the same blocks reach the same conclusion, and nodes that have seen different blocks converge as soon as the missing blocks arrive. The book says so directly: by selecting the greatest-cumulative-work valid chain, all nodes eventually achieve network-wide consensus, and temporary discrepancies between chains are resolved eventually as more work is added (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is also the rule that sets the price of rewriting history, since an attacker must produce more work than everyone else combined over the period in question.\n\nThe reader meets the rule in the chapter's argument about Alice and Bob: for the entire network to accept an alternative block, an additional new block would need to be mined on top of the alternative, which is simply the statement that the alternative chain must accumulate more work (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's mining chapter adds what a node does with the chain it did not choose: the losing blocks are valid but not part of the best chain, and are kept for future reference in case one of those secondary chains later becomes primary (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nChoosing is comparing two sums. The small example models two chains by the work in each of their blocks and picks the one whose total is larger, which is chain B. A real node does the same with the work implied by each block's target, keeping a running total that Bitcoin Core reports as the chain work. The book describes the procedure on arrival of a block: the node looks at the previous block hash to find the parent, usually the tip of the best chain, and if the new block does not extend the best chain it attaches the header to a secondary chain and compares the work; if the secondary chain is now the best, the node reorganizes its view of confirmed transactions and available unspent outputs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThree corrections to common wording. The rule is most work, not longest chain: a chain of fewer blocks mined at a higher difficulty can carry more work, which is why the book speaks of cumulative proof of work. The rule applies only among valid chains, so a chain with more work that breaks a rule is not a candidate at all. And the choice is each node's own, made independently; there is no vote and no announcement, which is exactly why no authority is needed." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#BestBlockchain> .
chx:S_BlockHeight a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Block height"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Block height" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 81 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Security ;
    skos:definition "The height of a block is its position in the chain, counted as the number of blocks below it. The book's blockchain chapter explains that the genesis block is at block height zero and that each subsequent block added on top is one position higher, like boxes stacked one on top of the other (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter itself uses the word when it says that over time, as the height of new blocks increases, so does the computation difficulty for the chain as a whole (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The vertical language comes from the habit of drawing the chain as a stack, with the first block as the foundation and the newest block as the top or tip (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nHeights are how people and programs talk about where something is in the record. A height is short, ordered and human-readable, where a block hash is thirty-two bytes and says nothing about position, so explorers accept a height as a search term and rules are written in terms of heights: the subsidy halves every 210,000 blocks, the difficulty is retargeted every 2,016 blocks, and a coinbase output matures after a hundred. The chapter's own picture of hundreds of thousands of blocks below the one containing Alice's transaction is a statement about height (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader meets heights everywhere a block is identified: the first block is height 0, and the book notes that height 800,000 was reached during the writing of the book in mid-2023, meaning there were 800,000 blocks stacked on top of the first, created in January 2009, which the addition above records (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter's links to the block containing Alice's transaction and the book's examples of a node at height 277,314 receiving a block that takes it to 277,315 are the same convention at work (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nCounting is addition, but the subtle part is that the number is derived rather than stored. The book explains that in early blocks the height was not part of the block's data structure and that each node identified a block's position dynamically when it was received, and that a later protocol change began including the height in the coinbase transaction, although its purpose was to ensure that each block had a different coinbase transaction, so nodes still have to determine the height themselves in order to validate that field (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA height is not a unique identifier. The book is emphatic: although a single block always has a specific and invariant height, the reverse is not true, because two or more blocks might have the same height while competing for the same position in the chain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Only the block hash always names exactly one block. A height is therefore the right way to speak about the record and the wrong way to refer to a particular block when forks matter, which is the subject of the next concept." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#BlockHeight> .
chx:S_AlternativeBlock a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Fork and alternative block"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Fork and alternative block" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 82 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Security ;
    skos:definition "A fork, in the sense used here, is the temporary situation in which two or more valid blocks claim the same height, so that the chain briefly has two tips. The book's glossary calls it an accidental fork and says it typically occurs when two or more miners find blocks at nearly the same time (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The blockchain chapter explains the same thing from the shape of the data: although a block has just one parent it can have several children, each committing to the same parent, and eventually only one child becomes part of the chain accepted by all full nodes and the fork is resolved (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). An alternative block is one of those competing blocks.\n\nThe concept matters for two reasons, one benign and one not. The benign one is that forks happen by themselves, because the network takes time to carry a block to everybody, and a reader who does not expect them will be puzzled when a block briefly disappears from an explorer. The book says that forks are almost always resolved within one block, and that the ten-minute interval is a design compromise between fast confirmation times and the probability of a fork (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The other reason is that a deliberate fork is exactly what a reversal needs, which is the argument the chapter makes about Alice and Bob.\n\nThe chapter meets the alternative block in its central security argument. It says that after the block containing Alice's transaction has propagated, creating an alternative block with a different version of Alice's transaction, such as one that does not pay Bob, would require performing the same amount of work as it will take all Bitcoin miners to create an entirely new block, and that for the whole network to accept that alternative block an additional new block would have to be mined on top of it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's glossary gives the name of a block that loses such a race: a stale block, successfully mined but not on the best chain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nDetecting a fork is comparing heights, which the small example does: if a new block claims the same height as the current tip there is a fork, and either way the next block will be one higher. Resolving it is the best-chain rule: nodes keep both branches, and whichever branch the next block extends carries more work and wins, after which the node reorganizes its view of confirmed transactions and available unspent outputs if it had preferred the other branch (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book adds that an accidental fork can occasionally extend to two blocks, when two blocks are found almost simultaneously by miners on opposite sides of a previous fork, but that the chance is low (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe word fork is badly overloaded and the book warns about it repeatedly. The accidental fork of this concept is not the same as a soft fork or a hard fork, which are changes to the consensus rules, and neither is the same as a software fork of a source repository (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Nor is a stale block the same as an orphan block, which the glossary defines as a block whose parent has not yet been processed by the local node (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). And an accidental fork is not an attack: it is the ordinary consequence of a network with delays." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#AlternativeBlock> .
chx:S_DoubleSpend a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Double spend"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Double spend" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 83 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Security ;
    skos:definition "A double spend is the attempt to spend the same money twice. The book's glossary defines it as the result of successfully spending some money more than once, and adds how Bitcoin prevents it: by verifying each transaction added to the blockchain to ensure that the inputs had not previously already been spent (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). In the vocabulary of this chapter, a double spend is two transactions whose inputs name the same outpoint, of which at most one can be recorded.\n\nThis is the problem Bitcoin was invented to solve, and the chapter's opening sentences are about it: the system does not require trust in third parties, and software on a user's own computer can verify every aspect of its operation (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Nakamoto's paper puts it at the centre, proposing a solution to the double-spending problem using a peer-to-peer network that timestamps transactions by hashing them into an ongoing chain of proof of work (Nakamoto, 2008). Digital data can be copied, so a purely digital coin can be offered to two people at once; only an agreed order of events can decide which offer counts.\n\nThe reader meets the prevention in three places in this chapter and the next. A node's verification checklist requires that the outputs being spent match outputs in the memory pool or unspent outputs in a block of the best chain, so a second spend is refused on arrival (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The set of unspent outputs is the record that makes the check cheap. And the chapter's security argument is about the remaining case, where the first spend is already in a block and a miner would have to build an alternative chain to replace it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's mining chapter gives a worked attack of exactly that kind, in which a buyer and a friendly pool operator replace a payment for paintings with one that pays the buyer back (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe check itself is a membership test, which the small example performs: if the outpoint named by a new transaction's input is already in the set of spent outputs, the transaction is rejected. Doing this reliably is what the whole branch is for. Within one chain of blocks the order is unambiguous, and the book says that the enforcement of that order ensures no two valid transactions can spend the same output, eliminating the problem of double spending (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The hard case is not within one chain but between two competing chains, and that is resolved by the best-chain rule.\n\nA double spend of a confirmed transaction is not impossible, only expensive, and the book notes that it can only be done on the attacker's own transactions, for which the attacker can produce a valid signature (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Replacement of an unconfirmed transaction is a different and much cheaper matter: the book's chapter on fees explains that an unconfirmed transaction can be replaced by one paying a higher fee rate, and warns the receiver of an unconfirmed transaction accordingly (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). And a double spend is not the same as a fork: a fork is two blocks, a double spend is two conflicting transactions." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#DoubleSpend> .
chx:S_GenesisBlock a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Genesis block"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Genesis block" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 84 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Security ;
    skos:definition "The genesis block is the first block of the blockchain, the one with no parent. The chapter introduces it at the bottom of the stack, saying that below the block containing Alice's transaction are hundreds of thousands of blocks linked to each other in a chain all the way back to block number zero, known as the genesis block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's blockchain chapter adds that it was created in 2009 and is the common ancestor of all blocks, so that if you start at any block and follow the chain backward in time you will eventually arrive at it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe genesis block is what makes the chain's security well founded rather than circular. Every other block is trusted because of the work piled on top of it, but the first block has nothing below it, so it is fixed in the software instead. The book explains the consequence: every node always starts with a blockchain of at least one block because the genesis block is statically encoded within Bitcoin Core so that it cannot be altered, every node always knows its hash and structure, the fixed time it was created and even the single transaction within, and thus every node has a secure root from which to build a trusted blockchain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA reader meets the genesis block as the block at height zero in any explorer, with the hash 00000000...0a8ce26f, and in the chapter's own data, where Bitcoin Core reports its height as 0, its version as 1, its merkle root equal to the identifier of its one transaction, its time as 1231006505, its nonce as 2083236893 and its compact target as 1d00ffff (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It is also where Bitcoin's one famous piece of prose lives: the book says its coinbase input contains the text about the Chancellor on the brink of a second bailout for banks, taken from the headline of a British newspaper, which was intended to offer proof of the earliest date the block could have been created (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nEvery claim about the block can be recomputed from its fields. Its timestamp, 1231006505, is a count of seconds since the start of 1970, and converting it gives the third of January 2009, which is the executed example of this concept and the date the book gives for the start of the network (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Assembling the eighty bytes of its header from the same fields and hashing them twice reproduces the published hash, as the block-header concept shows. And because the block contains one transaction, its merkle root equals that transaction's identifier, 4a5e1e4b...deda33b.\n\nThe genesis block is special in ways that break general rules, so it should not be used as an example of an ordinary block. Its previous-block field is all zeros because it has no parent; its reward was never spendable in the usual way; and its presence in the software means that a node does not verify it in the way it verifies every later block, which the book describes by saying that full nodes validate every block after the genesis block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Note also that the test networks have genesis blocks of their own, which is why the book shows a different first hash for each of them (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#GenesisBlock> .
chx:S_UnconfirmedTransaction a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Unconfirmed transaction"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Unconfirmed transaction" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 85 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Security ;
    skos:definition "An unconfirmed transaction is one that has been verified and propagated but is in no block. The book's network chapter uses exactly that word for the contents of a node's memory pool, transactions that are known to the network but not yet included in the blockchain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The chapter's own framing is the complement: Alice's transaction does not become part of the blockchain until it is included in a block by mining and that block has been validated by full nodes (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThis state is where most of the practical confusion about Bitcoin lives, because it looks like a completed payment and is not yet one. A merchant sees the money arrive in a few seconds and must decide whether to act. The chapter is careful about the two different intervals: within a few seconds Bob sees the transaction on the register, and approximately five minutes after the transaction was first transmitted a miner finds a solution for the block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Those two sentences describe propagation and confirmation, and treating them as the same thing is the error this concept exists to prevent.\n\nThe reader meets unconfirmed transactions in a wallet as a pending payment, in an explorer as a transaction without a block, and in the chapter as the pool of unverified transactions that Alice's payment joined before being included in a candidate block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The owner's course notes for this chapter treat the same point under the heading of the ten-minute misconception, observing that transactions must be confirmed, that confirmations ensure the transaction has been accepted by the network, and that waiting for a full six confirmations can take up to sixty minutes (Altunel, 2021).\n\nThe arithmetic behind that figure is a multiplication: six blocks at an average of ten minutes each is sixty minutes, as the expression above gives. The important word is average. The book explains why the interval is ten minutes and that blocks are generated every ten minutes on average, with the difficulty adjusted to keep that pace, but nothing makes any particular block arrive on time: a block may follow the previous one within a minute or after half an hour, and the book's own example has a miner finding a solution almost eleven minutes after starting (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). So the waiting time for a confirmation is a random quantity with a known mean.\n\nThree cautions. An unconfirmed transaction can be replaced, not only ignored: the book's chapter on fees describes replacement by a version paying a higher fee rate and advises the receiver of an unconfirmed transaction accordingly (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). It can also simply never be mined, if its fee rate is too low for the current market, in which case it may eventually be dropped from the memory pools that hold it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). And the number of nodes that have seen it is not a measure of safety, because propagation is not confirmation." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#UnconfirmedTransaction> .
chx:S_SmallPaymentAcceptance a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Accepting a small payment unconfirmed"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Accepting a small payment unconfirmed" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 86 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Security ;
    skos:definition "Accepting a small payment unconfirmed is the practice of treating a transaction as paid as soon as it has propagated, without waiting for a block. It is not a rule of Bitcoin but a judgement about risk. The book's mining chapter describes it in the setting of this very chapter: Bob, the seller, is willing to accept payment without waiting for confirmation because the risk of a double spend on a small item is low in comparison with the convenience of rapid customer service (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe practice matters because it shows that the number of confirmations to require is an economic question, not a technical one. The book offers the comparison that makes it intuitive: it is similar to the practice of coffee shops that accept credit card payments without a signature for amounts below twenty-five dollars, because the risk of a chargeback is low while the cost of delaying the transaction to obtain a signature is comparatively larger (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The same chapter draws the opposite conclusion for expensive goods, warning that selling a more expensive item runs the risk of a double-spend attack in which the buyer broadcasts a competing transaction that spends one of the same inputs and cancels the payment (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA reader meets the practice in shops, at vending machines and in the chapter itself, where Bob sees the transaction on the register within a few seconds and the sale proceeds (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The owner's course notes record the same reasoning: a merchant may accept a valid small-value transaction with no confirmations, for something like a cup of coffee, where a ten-minute delay is unnecessary, accepting that it is riskier than a card payment (Altunel, 2021). The book's counter-example is the gallery that hands over paintings after a single confirmation and loses them to a pool-assisted double spend (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe decision is a comparison, which the small example renders: a value below the threshold is accepted at once, a value at or above it waits. Setting the threshold means weighing the value of the goods, the chance of a reversal, which the probability concept quantifies, and the cost of delay. The book's guidance at the other end of the scale is explicit: a merchant selling large-value items must wait at least six confirmations before handing over the product, may reasonably wait for more, and for high-value items may wait twenty-four hours, which corresponds to about 144 confirmations (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nDo not read this practice as a claim that unconfirmed payments are safe. They can be replaced by a version paying a higher fee, as the book's chapter on fees explains, and they can be deliberately double spent by a buyer who is willing to try (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Note also what is being accepted: a payment whose validity has been checked, so the risk is reversal and not forgery. And the threshold is nobody's recommendation; the twenty-five dollars in the comparison is about credit cards, not about Bitcoin, and each merchant must set its own." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#SmallPaymentAcceptance> .
chx:S_Spending a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Spending the transaction"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Spending the transaction" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 87 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Blockchain ;
    skos:definition "This last group of the branch follows what happens after Alice's payment has been recorded: Bob spends it. The chapter gives the step a section of its own and begins it by saying that now that Alice's transaction has been embedded in the blockchain as part of a block, it is visible to all Bitcoin applications, and each full node can independently verify the transaction as valid and spendable (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The group has two concepts, the onward spending itself and the weaker verification that a lightweight client can perform.\n\nThe group closes the circle of the chapter. It began with Alice spending an output that Joe had created and ends with Bob spending the output that Alice created, which shows that there is only one mechanism in Bitcoin and that every participant uses it in turn. The chapter says exactly this in its summary: it saw how transactions build a chain that moves value from owner to owner, and tracked Alice's transaction from the moment it was created in her wallet, through the network, to the miners who recorded it on the blockchain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe first concept follows Bob, who can now pay a contractor or a supplier by transferring value from Alice's podcast payment to these new owners, and who in doing so extends the chain of transactions; the chapter's last figure shows the chain running from Joe through Alice and Bob to Gopesh, Bob's web designer (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The second concept is the verification that a wallet without a full node can do, which the chapter describes as confirming that the transaction is in the blockchain and has several blocks mined after it, and which the book calls simplified payment verification (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nBeing spendable is not the same as being spent, and being in the blockchain is not the same as being in a wallet's control: Bob can spend the output only because his wallet holds the key that the output's script demands. Note also the one exception to onward spending that this chapter has met, the coinbase output, which must wait a hundred blocks before it can be spent at all (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Spending> .
chx:S_SpendingOnward a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Spending onward"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Spending onward" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 88 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Spending ;
    skos:definition "Spending onward is the use of a received output as the input of a new transaction. It is the same operation that the chapter began with, seen from the other side: Alice spent an output that Joe's transaction created, and now Bob spends the output that Alice's transaction created. The chapter says that Bob can now spend the output from this and other transactions, for example by paying a contractor or a supplier, transferring value from Alice's podcast payment to these new owners, and that as he spends the payments received from Alice and other customers he extends the chain of transactions (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThis step is what makes the word money appropriate. A payment that could be received but not spent again would be a receipt, not a currency; because every received output can become an input, value circulates and the chain of ownership that the chapter opened with continues indefinitely. It is also the step that makes the whole history auditable, since each spending leaves a link that anyone can follow backward to the block in which the bitcoins were first created.\n\nThe chapter's own example is Bob paying his web designer Gopesh for a new website page, and its final figure shows Alice's transaction as part of a transaction chain from Joe to Gopesh (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The earlier figure of the chain gives the numbers: Bob creates a transaction, labelled Tx3, that spends the output he received in Tx2, pays 67,000 satoshis in a single output and therefore pays a fee of 8,000, and because it has only one output the book calls it a changeless transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe arithmetic is the same subtraction as before. Bob received 75,000 satoshis and pays 67,000, so the difference of 8,000 satoshis is the fee, which the expression above computes and confirms against the figure the book prints (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). His transaction's input is the outpoint Tx2:1, the second output of Alice's transaction, and his wallet must supply whatever the output's script demands, which here is a signature made with the key for the address Alice paid. No change output is needed because the amount he is paying is close to what he has.\n\nTwo cautions. Spending onward needs the key, not just the record: the output is public, but only the holder of the key that satisfies its script can spend it, which is why losing a key loses the funds. And a changeless transaction like Bob's is only practical when the amount to be paid is roughly the amount available minus the fee, as the book says; otherwise the surplus would be handed to the miner (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#SpendingOnward> .
chx:S_PaymentVerification a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Simplified payment verification"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Simplified payment verification" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 89 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Spending ;
    skos:definition "Simplified payment verification is the method by which a program that does not hold the whole blockchain can still gain assurance about a payment. The chapter describes what it achieves without naming it: lightweight clients can partially verify payments by confirming that the transaction is in the blockchain and has several blocks mined after it, thus providing assurance that the miners expended significant effort committing to it (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's network chapter gives the name, saying that for space- and power-constrained devices a simplified payment verification method is used to let them operate without validating the full blockchain (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe method is what makes a Bitcoin wallet possible on a telephone, and it is the clearest example in the course of a deliberate trade between cost and trust. What the client gives up is stated plainly by the book: lightweight clients cannot construct a full picture of all the unspent outputs available for spending because they do not know about all the transactions on the network, and they rely instead on peers to provide partial views of relevant parts of the blockchain on demand (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). What they keep is the ability to tell that work was done committing to their payment, which is the guarantee that this whole branch is about.\n\nThe reader meets this method in the chapter's closing section, where it is contrasted with what a full node does, namely validating every transfer of the funds from the moment the bitcoins were first generated in a block through each subsequent transaction until they reach Bob's address (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's blockchain chapter describes the mechanics: the client receives a message containing the block header and a merkle path linking the transaction of interest to the merkle root, uses the path to connect the transaction to the header and the header to the rest of the chain, and so receives less than a kilobyte instead of a whole block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe efficiency comes from the shape of the merkle tree. Proving that one of N transactions is in a block needs about the base-two logarithm of N hashes of thirty-two bytes each, so for the roughly sixteen thousand transactions of the largest possible block that is fourteen hashes, 448 bytes, as the calculation above gives and as the book states (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The client then recomputes the pairwise hashes along the path and compares the result with the root in the header it already has; if they match, the transaction was in that block.\n\nThe limits of the method must be stated as clearly as its benefit. It proves inclusion and work, not validity: a lightweight client has not checked that the transaction's inputs existed and were unspent, so it is trusting the miners and its peers for that. It also leaks information, since asking peers about particular transactions reveals an interest in them, and the book devotes a section to lightweight clients and privacy for that reason (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The honest summary is the chapter's own word, partially: the client verifies part of what a full node verifies, and knowing which part is the point." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#PaymentVerification> .
chx:S_Semantics a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Semantics"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Semantics" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 90 ;
    skos:definition "This branch reads the chapter with the eyes of the course rather than of the book. A transaction chain, as the first branch described it, is a record of what came from what: each output exists because a transaction consumed earlier outputs, and each transaction happened because somebody who held a key authorized it. That is precisely the subject matter of provenance, for which the World Wide Web Consortium has published a standard vocabulary, the PROV Ontology (Lebo et al., 2013). The branch shows that the chapter's own structure can be expressed in that vocabulary, which is why this course treats Bitcoin as a case study in semantic modelling as well as a payment system.\n\nThe branch matters for two reasons. The first is academic honesty about what the course is doing: every chapter of this material is turned into an ontology, and a student is entitled to see why the subject suits that treatment. The second is practical. Once a transaction chain is described in a standard vocabulary, questions about it can be asked in a standard query language and answered by a general tool, instead of by a program written for Bitcoin; and the same description can be joined to descriptions of other things, which is what makes a vocabulary worth standardizing.\n\nThe branch has one group with three concepts. The ontology is the general idea of writing down the concepts of a domain and their relations in a form a machine can use. The triple is the unit in which such descriptions are written, a subject, a predicate and an object, and the graph is what a set of triples forms. The provenance graph is the application to this chapter: Alice's transaction as an activity, the outputs as entities derived from one another, and the key holders as the agents responsible.\n\nTwo things this branch does not claim. It does not claim that the Bitcoin software uses any of these standards; it does not, and the description here is a model of the chapter's content, built for teaching and for querying. And it does not claim that provenance in this sense identifies anybody: the agents of the model are key holders, and a key holder is not a person until some evidence outside Bitcoin connects them, which is the same caution the first branch gave about addresses." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Semantics> .
chx:S_ProvenanceModel a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Provenance model"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Provenance model" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 91 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Semantics ;
    skos:definition "A provenance model is a description of where things came from: which things existed, which processes made or changed them, and who was responsible. This group introduces the vocabulary in which such a description is written and then applies it to the chapter. The standard it uses is the PROV Ontology, a World Wide Web Consortium Recommendation of April 2013, which the specification describes as a set of classes, properties and restrictions, expressed in the OWL2 Web Ontology Language, which is the World Wide Web Consortium's standard language for writing ontologies, that can be used to represent and interchange provenance information generated in different systems and under different contexts (Lebo et al., 2013).\n\nThe group is the bridge between this chapter and the method of the course. Bitcoin's blockchain is unusual among records in that its provenance is complete and public: for any output one can follow the chain back to the block in which the bitcoins were created, which is exactly what the chapter says a full node does when it validates every transfer of the funds from the moment the bitcoins were first generated (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A record with that property is the ideal case study for a vocabulary designed to express where things came from.\n\nThe group begins with the ontology in general, since a reader must know what kind of thing is being built before seeing one. It then gives the unit of construction, the triple, and the structure that triples form, the graph. It closes with the provenance graph of this chapter, in which the three starting-point classes of the standard, the entity, the activity and the agent, are matched to the output, the transaction and the key holder.\n\nKeep the model and the thing modelled apart. The triples described here are written by this course, not by Bitcoin, and a different modeller could reasonably choose differently: an output might be modelled as an entity or as a state of an entity, and a transaction as one activity or as several. A model is a tool for answering particular questions, so it is judged by whether it answers them, not by whether it is the only possible description." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#ProvenanceModel> .
chx:S_Ontology a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Ontology"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Ontology" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 92 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ProvenanceModel ;
    skos:definition "An ontology, in computing, is an explicit description of the concepts of some area of knowledge and of the relations among them, written in a form that a machine can process. It names the classes of thing that exist, arranges them in a hierarchy of more and less general, gives properties that relate them, and states which individuals belong to which class. The PROV Ontology is one: the specification says it provides a set of classes, properties and restrictions that can be used to represent and interchange provenance information, and that it can be specialized to create new classes and properties for different applications and domains (Lebo et al., 2013).\n\nFor this course the ontology is the form in which each chapter is recorded, so that the material can be queried, checked and reused rather than only read. Three benefits follow. Terms are defined once and used consistently, which is why this chapter insists that every technical word it uses is itself a concept. Questions can be stated in advance as competency questions and the description tested against them. And because the vocabulary is standard, the result can be joined to other descriptions, as this branch does by borrowing the provenance vocabulary of the Web consortium.\n\nThe reader meets an ontology as the structure of the page they are reading: the branches, groups and concepts of this chapter are the classes of an ontology, the worked examples are individuals, and the explanations are the documentation. Outside this course, ontologies are used for medical terminologies, product catalogues, scientific data and the descriptions that search engines read from web pages. The World Wide Web Consortium's provenance work is itself an example published as a Recommendation (Lebo et al., 2013).\n\nThe smallest useful description is a list of classes with their parents, which is what the example computes: four classes of which two have no parent, and therefore two roots and a hierarchy below them. A real ontology adds properties, such as the relation between a transaction and the outputs it spends, and constraints, such as that every concept must have a definition. The ontology of this chapter is generated from the text you are reading and is checked before it is published: every number quoted in it is executed, and shapes are applied to the result.\n\nAn ontology is not a database and not a program. It says what kinds of things there are and how they relate, not how to store them efficiently or what to do with them, and a statement in an ontology is a claim about the world that may be wrong. Nor is one ontology ever the ontology of a subject: the same chapter could be modelled with different classes, and this one is organized around how a learner meets the material, which is a pedagogical choice and not a discovery." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Ontology> .
chx:S_Triple a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Triple and graph"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Triple and graph" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 93 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ProvenanceModel ;
    skos:definition "A triple is the unit in which these descriptions are written: a subject, a predicate and an object, read as a single sentence. The statement that Alice's transaction used Joe's output is a triple whose subject is the transaction, whose predicate is the relation used and whose object is the output. A set of triples forms a graph, in which the subjects and objects are nodes and each triple is a directed edge labelled with its predicate. The same structure that this chapter has already used to picture the network, nodes joined by edges, is the structure of the description itself.\n\nTriples are chosen because they combine without planning. Two descriptions written by different people, about different things, can simply be put together, and anything they have in common joins up; nothing has to be merged, because there are no tables and no columns. This is why the provenance standard can be reused here: its predicates for derivation, generation and attribution can be applied to this chapter's outputs and transactions without changing anything in the standard.\n\nThe reader meets triples in every file this course publishes, where they are written in a compact text form, and in the query language that is used to ask questions of them. Outside the course, the same form carries product and event descriptions on web pages, open government data and scientific catalogues. The provenance standard lists the starting-point predicates of its vocabulary, among them was derived from, was attributed to and was generated by, each of which is the middle element of a triple (Lebo et al., 2013).\n\nCounting a small graph shows how a description grows. The three triples of the example mention four distinct things in all, Alice's transaction, the output it spent, the output it created and Alice herself, because the transaction is the subject of two of them; so three statements already describe a connected fragment with a path from Joe's output through the transaction to Bob's. Adding Bob's onward transaction extends the same graph without altering what is already there, which is the property that makes the form useful for a chain that grows block by block.\n\nTriples are a notation, not a guarantee of truth: a graph records what somebody asserted, so the question of who asserted it, which is itself a provenance question, is always worth asking. A graph is also not a hierarchy, although hierarchies can be expressed in it, and the absence of a triple means only that nothing was said, not that the opposite is true. Finally, the graph of this chapter is a description of the record and not the record: the blockchain itself is a sequence of bytes, and these triples are what this course says about it." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Triple> .
chx:S_ProvenanceGraph a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Provenance graph"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Provenance graph" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 94 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ProvenanceModel ;
    skos:definition "A provenance graph of this chapter is the transaction chain written in the vocabulary of the provenance standard. The standard's three starting-point classes fit the chapter's parts closely. An entity, which the specification defines as a physical, digital, conceptual or other kind of thing with some fixed aspects, is a transaction output. An activity, which it defines as something that occurs over a period of time and acts upon or with entities, including consuming, processing, transforming and generating them, is a transaction. An agent, which it defines as something that bears some form of responsibility for an activity taking place or for the existence of an entity, is the holder of the key that authorized the spending (Lebo et al., 2013).\n\nReading the chain this way makes the course's theme visible in the chapter's own material. The chapter says that each input spends an earlier output, that anyone can follow a payment back through the chain of ownership, and that a full node validates every transfer from the moment the bitcoins were first generated in a block (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). Those are statements about derivation, and the provenance vocabulary exists to express exactly them, so Bitcoin's ledger becomes an example of a complete and public provenance record rather than a special case needing its own language.\n\nThe chapter supplies the graph twice in picture form: the figure of three transactions in which the output of one is the input of the next, and the closing figure of Alice's transaction as part of a chain from Joe through Alice and Bob to Gopesh, Bob's web designer (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). A block explorer draws the same graph interactively, and a reader who follows the links from an input to the output it spends is walking it by hand.\n\nThe translation is mechanical, which is what the executed example shows: a chain of outputs becomes a list of derivation triples, each saying that one output was derived from the one before, so the chain Tx1:0, Tx2:1, Tx3:0 yields two statements. A fuller description adds, for each transaction, that it used the outputs it spent and generated the outputs it created, and that it was attributed to the agent who signed it; the standard's predicates for those are used, was generated by and was attributed to (Lebo et al., 2013). The fee needs no special treatment, since it is the difference between what was used and what was generated.\n\nThe model has limits worth stating. Attribution is to a key holder, not to a named person, so the graph does not identify anybody; the chapter's own remark that the change address is often new, for privacy reasons, shows that even the grouping of outputs by owner is a guess (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The activity has a time only once the transaction is in a block, and even then the timestamp is approximate. And derivation here is between whole outputs, not between amounts: an output derived from two inputs does not tell you which satoshi came from where, because the protocol does not record that." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#ProvenanceGraph> .
chx:S_Practice a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Practice"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Practice" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 95 ;
    skos:definition "The last branch of this chapter is about trying things out. The concepts of the earlier branches are all mechanical, which means that a learner can operate them rather than only read about them, and a small number of interactive pages have become the standard way to do that in teaching. This branch describes the ones the owner's course notes point to: the demonstration pages of Anders Brownworth's Blockchain Demo, which the notes link under the address of its hash page (Altunel, 2021) (Brownworth, n.d.). The branch exists so that the pages are part of the chapter's record and not an unexplained link at the end of a slide.\n\nThe branch matters because the central claim of the chapter, that a chain of hashes makes a record expensive to change, is far more convincing when a student breaks a chain with their own hands and watches every block after the change turn invalid. The pages also make the asymmetry of proof of work physical: mining a single demonstration block takes a perceptible moment, and verifying it is instant. For a course this is the cheapest possible laboratory, since the pages run in a browser and risk nothing.\n\nThe branch has one group, the interactive demonstration, with five concepts, which follow the pages of the demonstration in its own order: the hash page, which computes a digest as you type; the block page, which adds a nonce and a mining button; the blockchain page, which links five blocks and shows the cascade of invalidity; the distributed page, which gives three peers their own copies; and the coinbase page, which puts transactions and a block reward inside the blocks. Each concept says what the page shows, which concept of the earlier branches it demonstrates, and what it simplifies away.\n\nA demonstration is not the system. The pages use a single hash, a difficulty of four leading zeros and a handful of blocks, where Bitcoin uses a double hash, a difficulty astronomically higher and hundreds of thousands of blocks; they have no network, no signatures, no scripts and no fees. Their value is that every simplification is a point of comparison: a student who can say what the page leaves out has understood the chapter." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#Practice> .
chx:S_InteractiveDemo a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Interactive demonstration"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Interactive demonstration" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 96 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Practice ;
    skos:definition "An interactive demonstration, in the sense of this group, is a web page that implements a small part of the chapter's mechanics and lets a reader change the inputs and watch the outputs. The group describes the five pages of the Blockchain Demo, whose navigation names them Hash, Block, Blockchain, Distributed and Coinbase, in that order, each page building on the one before (Brownworth, n.d.). The owner's course notes for this chapter give the address of the first of them as the application with which to experience the subject (Altunel, 2021).\n\nThe group is included because the chapter's claims are of exactly the kind that a demonstration can settle. That a hash changes completely when one character of the input changes, that a block is valid only when its hash matches a template, that changing an old block invalidates every later one, and that different peers can hold different chains are all statements that can be seen rather than believed. The pages are also a shared reference: a class can be asked to do the same thing and compare results.\n\nThe pages work the same way throughout. Each block is a box with fields, the contents of the box are hashed as you type, and the box turns from red to green when its hash satisfies the difficulty. The script of the demonstration sets that difficulty to four leading zeros and limits the search to five hundred thousand attempts, with a note that because a hexadecimal digit has sixteen values, each extra digit of difficulty makes the puzzle sixteen times harder (Brownworth, n.d.). The later pages add fields, a previous-hash field to make a chain, a column per peer to make a network, and transaction rows to make a ledger.\n\nTreat the pages as illustrations of single ideas, not as a model of Bitcoin. Their hash is a single SHA-256 rather than the double hash Bitcoin uses, their blocks have no merkle root and no timestamp, their mining is a button rather than a competition, and the amounts in the coinbase page are written as plain numbers with no keys or signatures behind them. The group names these differences for each page so that a learner can translate in both directions." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#InteractiveDemo> .
chx:S_HashDemo a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Hash page"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Hash page" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 97 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_InteractiveDemo ;
    skos:definition "The hash page is the first and simplest page of the demonstration. Its content is two fields, labelled Data and Hash, under the heading SHA256 Hash, and the page recomputes the digest of whatever is in the data field as the reader types (Brownworth, n.d.). Nothing else happens on the page: there is no block, no chain and no mining, so the only thing on show is the behaviour of the hash function itself.\n\nThe page is the right place to start because every later concept of the chapter rests on this one function. The two properties that the chapter attributes to it are both visible within a few keystrokes: that the same input always produces the same output, which the reader sees by typing a text, clearing it and typing it again, and that nobody can predict the output for a new input, which the reader sees by changing a single character and watching the whole digest change (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The fixed length is equally visible, since the digest stays sixty-four characters long however much is typed.\n\nThe owner's course notes for this chapter end with a link to exactly this page, offered as the application with which to experience the subject (Altunel, 2021). Inside this chapter the same function is met three times over: the identifier of a transaction, the identifier of a block and the proof of work are all results of applying it, twice in a row in each case. Outside the chapter a reader meets it whenever a downloaded file is checked against a published digest, and whenever a password is stored in a form that cannot be read back. The page is useful precisely because it isolates that one function from everything else, so a learner can satisfy themselves about its behaviour before meeting it inside a block.\n\nThe page computes, for a text, the same value that the standard library computes: the digest of the sentence Alice pays Bob 75000 begins 320a22eeeb9c0391, which is the executed example of this concept and can be compared with what the page shows for the same input. A reader should then try the chapter's own test case, the phrase that the book hashes, and the experiment of changing one letter: the course's check for the hash-function concept measures that changing the exclamation mark to a question mark changes sixty-one of the sixty-four digits.\n\nThree differences from Bitcoin should be noted while using the page. It applies SHA-256 once, where Bitcoin's identifiers and proof of work apply it twice. It hashes text as typed, where Bitcoin hashes exact binary structures, so a reader cannot reproduce a real transaction identifier by pasting its hexadecimal form into the data box. And a digest on this page is only a digest: it is not a commitment to anything until something refers to it, which is what the next pages add." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#HashDemo> .
chx:S_BlockDemo a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Block page"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Block page" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 98 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_InteractiveDemo ;
    skos:definition "The block page puts the hash of the previous page inside a box that has to satisfy a condition. Its fields are a block number, a nonce, a data field and the hash, with a Mine button beside them (Brownworth, n.d.). The box is shown in one colour when the hash begins with the required number of zeros and in another when it does not, so the reader can see at a glance whether the block is valid; pressing Mine makes the page try nonce after nonce until the condition is met.\n\nThis page is where proof of work stops being a phrase. The reader has already seen that a digest cannot be steered, so the only way to satisfy the condition is to try, and the page does the trying in front of them. The asymmetry that the chapter describes, an enormous amount of computation to get right and only a small amount to verify as correct, is experienced as the difference between the pause after pressing Mine and the instant recolouring of the box when the answer is in (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nA reader meets the same mechanism in the chapter's account of the lottery, in which a miner feeds a candidate block to the hash function and makes a small change and tries again if the output does not match the template (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The book's own miniature version, hashing a phrase with a number until the digest begins with a zero, is the same experiment with an easier target and a found nonce of 32 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe page's rule can be reproduced exactly. Its script fixes the difficulty at four leading zeros and limits the search to five hundred thousand attempts, and it hashes the concatenation of the block's fields (Brownworth, n.d.). Doing the same here, for block number 1 and the data Alice pays Bob 75000, the first nonce whose digest begins with four zeros is 6630, which is the executed example of this concept. The expected number of attempts for four hexadecimal zeros is sixteen to the fourth power, that is 65,536, so 6630 was a lucky run; the script's own note is that each extra digit of difficulty makes the puzzle sixteen times harder, and that a difficulty of six needs a limit well above five hundred million (Brownworth, n.d.).\n\nThe differences from real mining are large and instructive. There is one miner and no competition, so no block is ever lost to a faster rival. The difficulty is fixed by the page instead of being retargeted every 2,016 blocks. The threshold is expressed as leading zeros rather than as a number below a target, which is a good approximation but not the real rule. And the nonce here is unbounded, whereas Bitcoin's is four bytes and must be supplemented by changing other fields." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#BlockDemo> .
chx:S_BlockchainDemo a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Blockchain page"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Blockchain page" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 99 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_InteractiveDemo ;
    skos:definition "The blockchain page shows five blocks in a column, each with the fields of the block page and one more, labelled Prev, which holds the hash of the block before it (Brownworth, n.d.). Because a block's hash is computed over its own fields including that one, the blocks are chained: the hash of block two depends on the hash of block one, the hash of block three on that of block two, and so on. The page recomputes the whole column whenever anything is typed.\n\nThis page demonstrates the single most important claim of the chapter's third branch. The book states it as a chain of consequences: any change to a parent block requires a child block's hash to change, which requires a change in the pointer of the grandchild, which in turn changes the grandchild, and so on, so that once a block has many generations following it, it cannot be changed without forcing a recalculation of all subsequent blocks (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). On the page this is not an argument but an observation: edit the data of block two and blocks two to five all turn invalid at once.\n\nThe reader meets the same structure in the chapter, which describes hundreds of thousands of blocks linked to each other in a chain of blocks all the way back to block number zero (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), and in the book's blockchain chapter, which calls the structure an ordered, back-linked list of blocks and compares its stability to the layers of a geological formation (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The owner's course notes end with a slide titled Chain of Blocks and the link to this demonstration (Altunel, 2021).\n\nThe cascade is a simple consequence of dependency, which the small example renders: editing the block at position one invalidates it and every position after it, here the positions one to four of a five-block chain. To repair the chain the reader must mine each affected block again, in order, and each one takes its own search, which is the page's way of showing what the chapter means when it says that recalculating six blocks, plus one new block, would require an immense amount of computation (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The work grows with the depth of the change, which is the reason confirmations are counted.\n\nNote what the page cannot show. There is only one chain and one editor, so there is no competition and no best-chain rule, and nothing stops the reader from patiently remining all five blocks, which in Bitcoin would mean outpacing the entire network while it keeps extending the honest chain. The page also has no transactions, so the thing being protected is only a text; the coinbase page adds a ledger, and the distributed page adds the other participants." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#BlockchainDemo> .
chx:S_DistributedDemo a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Distributed page"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Distributed page" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 100 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_InteractiveDemo ;
    skos:definition "The distributed page repeats the five-block chain of the previous page once for each of three peers, labelled Peer A, Peer B and Peer C (Brownworth, n.d.). Each peer has its own column of blocks with its own fields and its own Mine buttons, and the page recomputes each column independently. Nothing is sent between the peers: the point of the page is that each of them holds a copy, and that a reader can compare the copies by eye.\n\nThe page adds the dimension that the chapter's second branch is about. A chain that only one person holds proves nothing about agreement, whereas three chains that agree show what consensus looks like in the simplest possible case. Tampering with one peer's block two makes that peer's chain disagree with the other two from block two onward, which is the picture behind the chapter's statement that full nodes choose the chain of valid blocks with the most total proof of work when several alternatives exist (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nThe reader meets the idea in the chapter's description of the network, in which every participant holds and verifies the same data and no participant is special (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023), and in the book's network chapter, which says that the nodes interconnect in a mesh with a flat topology and that there is no server and no hierarchy (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The page is the smallest possible version of that: three equals, each with the whole record.\n\nComparison is the operation the page invites, and the small example performs it: three peers whose latest hashes are collected, two of which agree and one of which does not, so the set of distinct values has more than one element and the peers are not in consensus. On the page the same comparison is done by eye, reading the last hash of each column. Repairing the tampered peer means remining its blocks until its column matches again, which is the demonstration's version of a node catching up with the network.\n\nThe page leaves out everything about how peers learn from one another: there are no messages, no propagation, no gossip and no rule for choosing between chains, so a reader must supply those from the second and third branches. Nor is there any notion of cumulative work, so the page cannot show why a longer or heavier chain wins; it can only show that disagreement is visible. Three peers are also a very small network, and the chapter's figure of about ten thousand listening nodes is a different order of magnitude (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#DistributedDemo> .
chx:S_CoinbaseDemo a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Coinbase page"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Coinbase page" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 101 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_InteractiveDemo ;
    skos:definition "The coinbase page is the last of the demonstration and the one closest to Bitcoin. Each block of each peer now has a line labelled Coinbase, which pays an amount to somebody, followed by transaction rows that each move an amount from one name to another, and then the previous hash and the hash of the block (Brownworth, n.d.). The heading of the page is Coinbase Transactions, and the three peers of the previous page are kept, so the reader sees a small ledger replicated three times.\n\nThe page closes the circle of the chapter by putting payments inside the blocks whose security the earlier pages demonstrated. It shows the two things the mining group explains: that new money enters only through the coinbase line, and that everything else is a transfer from one party to another. It also makes tampering meaningful, because changing an amount in an early block now changes who owns what, and the cascade of invalid hashes is the reason that change cannot pass unnoticed.\n\nThe reader meets the same arrangement in the chapter, where each miner includes a special transaction that pays their own address the block reward plus the fees, and in the book's mining chapter, which calls that the coinbase transaction and notes that it does not spend any earlier output (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023). The genesis block of Bitcoin is the extreme case, a block whose only transaction is its coinbase (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023).\n\nBalances on the page are read by adding and subtracting along the chain, which the small example does for one block: a coinbase of one hundred paid to a first party, who then sends thirty to a second and twenty to a third, leaves that party with fifty. Doing this for every block of a peer's column gives every name's balance, and comparing the columns of the three peers shows whether they agree about it. The reader should then try the experiment the chapter warns about: spend the same amount twice in one column and see that nothing in the page prevents it.\n\nThat last experiment is the page's most useful limitation. There are no keys and no signatures, so anybody can move anybody's money, and there is no check that a sender has what they send, so a double spend is not rejected; Bitcoin prevents both with the scripts and signatures of the first branch and the verification checklist of the second. The amounts are plain numbers rather than outputs, so there are no unspent outputs, no change and no fees. And the coinbase amount here never halves, where Bitcoin's subsidy is cut in half every 210,000 blocks." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch02#CoinbaseDemo> .
